   455	### New canon minted (author's list)
   456	- **Oxhollow's grain cart (flag).** Since his carts "go round", the keeper sends one to Thornwater for grain on the 85th, eight days out and eight home. Its carter is his sister's son: a long, sleepy-faced young man with very large feet and two grey mules, who says about forty words in six days. The three boxes ride his bed from Oxhollow to Thornwater (85th–93rd) for nothing, because the five walk his road in front of him. No paper, no line. He rests the 91st at Lowmarch and loads grain at Thornwater on the 94th.
   457	- **Lira's boot.** The carter's spare pair, each "as long as Cael's forearm"; he will not split a pair; two marks. She packs the right with a torn shirt over her taped foot; the left hangs from the cart's tail, is left at Thornwater's barn door by the carter, and goes east on Oryn's mule "against the other one going".
   458	- **The road Oxhollow–Lowmarch (flag):** south down a broad valley of alder and rough pasture, small fords, halting-grounds; on the fourth day two hundred yards of moss crossed by a causeway of laid logs (the carter's father's), about one log in six rotten at the heart. It reaches Lowmarch's river on the west bank and crosses by the ferry.
   459	- **Teague's crew on a road:** single file, a crew's length apart; no fire at any halt; chock-stones left as found; the mules at the windward end.
   460	- **The ferryman's rope (flag):** sixty yards of four-strand, laid up by himself. He will take no silver ("a figure's a thing you can pay and walk off from"); he will have a rope when they have one. Entered by Karis as owed, with no price.
   461	- **The two HOLD slips (flag):** one bark and one shingle; a bull to be brought down off top grazing before the snow, and a ford to be staked. Each hold "asked who". They will wait a fortnight for the five and then have Teague. Cael tells Pike on the 91st not to keep them past the fortnight. **They are still on the oak at the end of the movement.**
   462	- **How the posting came (flag):** by the last mail-ferry, to the landing broker, sealed, with a hundred marks in a bag and "a line of direction"; the broker's clerk brought it up the next morning. Pike said it twice that day to forty people, nobody scratched, and he has kept it under his stone since ("A thing on a tack is a thing I'm offering."). The clerk is the hirer's witness: whatever is brought down is to be seen at the landing, marked, and sent west by the next boat. He does not know what the things are or who wants them.
   463	- **The posting as an object (flag), by Seln:** cream paper of an office's weight; a margin pricked with a wheel; "Recovery." set alone as a heading; the sum in words and again in a bracket; the place described and not named.
   464	- **The wall on the 90th:** the five are tenth; one of the two crews they passed is four people, none young, one with a wooden foot, who brought a mule string through the cold road's pass and lost none.
   465	- **Pike's rule for a job with nothing at the end of it:** "I write what was done… if his mark never goes on it, the box stays shut."
   466	- **Eleven witnesses on the 91st:** Teague and his three; the Oxhollow carter; the hide-woman; the old carter under the ash; the shingle pair; the standing-money boy; the broker's clerk.
   467	- **Teague (flag):** he has been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and has never put it to the proof. He once saw a surveyor's kit, "years ago and a long way west". His reason for not taking the paper is his three; his sense that the paper is wrong has no reason, and he tells nobody.
   468	- **The registry's printed sheet (flag):** east of the Line it shows a river or two without their bends, hills drawn alike, and seven dashed roads with no names that run out into white and stop; the third from the top crosses the first water (no ferry, no Lowmarch) and ends three fingers east of it, at about Thornwater's fence or an hour beyond. **"Void"** (flag): Seln says that in a registry's usage an entry is void when nothing is written against it, and supposes that a carter was once shown such a sheet.
   469	- **Three blank circles on the carter's map (flag; see owner flag 4):** the bend east of Thornwater, and two round blanks that Karis had read as thrift, one in his hill-hatching a day south of Lowmarch and a little aside, one far to the south-east among his water-strokes with no road within a day. She rings all three in pencil and writes *guess* beside two. The hide is "a map of days" and will not take a straightedge.
   470	- **Oryn at Thornwater (flag):** she mends the carter's split palm on a plank inside the west gate before she eats ("That's one."). Her changed plan: she had meant to sit at this gate's table until the 99th; she walks the Fallow road in those days, the same six gates waiting the same days; Thornwater's bench "goes short" and is to be paid on her way back. She has been round the ring "thirty times, with the mule, by the ruts" (her round goes on east by that road). The carters' saying, as given: *You'll not want her in the ring; she's no good there.*, first heard at the first gate she ever mended at.
   471	- **Karis's reserve:** her first point since the 78th is set on a lamp-wick in Thornwater's yard on the 93rd, at Oryn's bidding; "three parts full".
   472	- **The long cord:** two box-ropes spliced by Brom, stretched from a fence-post, marked off with red wool against the short cord. **Its length is never stated**, nor is Karis's stride.
   473	- **The Fallow road (flag):** leaves Thornwater by the east gate; forks an hour out (the left branch to a hold up the left-hand water; the right over a low place in the hills); two days due east through tall pale ungrazed grass. The holds either side call it fallow and give no reason.
   474	- **The Ring's ground (flag; one picture for chapters 27–30):** at the crest of a long rise the ruts swing out to the north and come back to their line on the far side, keeping about forty yards from the line all the way round. The line crosses the old straight line of the road **eighty of Cael's strides** beyond the turn; Karis's grey stone marks it (the circle's western point). The camp is at the turn. The floor is three hundred yards from the turn. The perimeter is walked from the stone northward, with the ring on the right hand; Brom cuts a turf at each cord; the constant offset is **six feet and a half** to the cord.
   475	- **The stake:** a square iron bar as thick as two fingers, a hand's length clear of the turf, its head beaten flat, a boot's width from Karis's third station; the registry's stamp a thumb's breadth below the head, with *thirty-one* punched under it. Not touched or drawn.
   476	- **Hound sign outside:** three runs through the grass within twenty yards of each other on the eastern side, crossing the line both ways; none on the north side.
   477	- **What each of the five reports inside** (beyond the protected lines): Karis can see where sixteen points would go and "can't find the hand to reach with"; this is not being empty ("reserve present, sigil gone"). Brom: "There's no next room"; a hundredweight weighs the same. Seln: "It is not there to be asked." Lira: a yard and a half, and no crack. Oryn: the surface count stops at "Four—"; returns at the stride; and stops when she alone is inside with Brom's wrist held across the line.
   478	- **The mule** grazes across the stone with two feet either side and is not troubled.
   479	- **The floor (flag):** on the crown of a low swell, twenty-six of Cael's strides across by two diameters; colder than the turf; soundless under a boot; its rim rolled over like the rim of a dish, a hand above the turf outside and a finger above the floor within; level under a taut cord and under the brass and thread. Eleven squared grey uprights stand a stride in from the rim, each as thick as a man's body, the tallest twice Brom's height and the shortest knee-high. Round each, at chest height, and round the inner face of the lip, a band a hand wide of fine sunk lines, straight, hooked and crossing. None of Karis's forty-odd chips matches.
   480	- **The rubbings (flag):** one sheet to an upright, pencil rubbed over paper held flat by Lira; ten whole and the eleventh half done; numbered, with each stone's height and facing. They are in the middle box (the eleventh rolled inside Karis's coat).
   481	- **The den:** under the eastern lip, where the slope falls away and the earth has slumped from beneath the rim: a mouth as long as a cart and thigh-high, running back under the floor; a trodden apron; at least four worn hollows in a row; coarse hair on the underside of the lip.
   482	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the Oxhollow carter; the carter with the split hand; the crew with the wooden foot.
