     1	# Chapter 57 — Four Make an Argument
     2	
     3	It had been the afternoon of the hundred and nineteenth day when Karis sat down at the far fire with three clean leaves before her, and by the dusk of the hundred and twentieth she had risen from them only to sleep and to pace a marsh.
     4	
     5	She was making one leaf three times.
     6	
     7	She had never done such a thing in her life. A leaf was written once, and dated, and laid in its place, and the place was entered; that was what a box was for. But there were three boxes, and she had seen what a mountain could do to a cart. On the seventy-eighth day everything that the five of them owned had gone into a gorge except those three, and they had come off the hill only because a very large person went back for two of them, and a boy broke three ribs over the third. She had counted the loss that night, when she could count again. It had been blankets, and a kettle, and a rope.
     8	
     9	It would not be blankets now. Until the Fallow there had been nothing in those boxes that did not also stand on a shelf somewhere to the west, where she was no longer let in. Since the Fallow there had been a good deal, and since this morning there was a thing that existed nowhere in the world but on the paper in front of her.
    10	
    11	So there would be one leaf in each box, and the first of them in her coat, with a leaf of trials that had a struck numeral on it.
    12	
    13	She wrote small. At the head she set the three places by the names that carters gave them, since she had no better: the Fallow Ring, and the Long Stair, and the hall. Under each went its perimeter, which was sixty-two cords and twenty strides, three times over; and its radius, ten cords and a piece; and the number on its stake. Thirty-one. Thirty-two, on two bars. Thirty-three.
    14	
    15	Then the walls, and here she was careful, since she had been greedy that morning and knew it. She had eleven sheets from the Fallow, where the bands ran round the feet of eleven stones, and twenty-nine from a room under the Stair. She had twelve from the hall. That made fifty-two, and the last twelve were all from one wall, rubbed upside down in an hour, by a person who was being held by the belt.
    16	
    17	She had gone through them twice by the fire, with the cup of barley at her knee. There were thirty-three kinds on those twelve sheets. Every one of the thirty-three was among her forty-one, and there was not a stroke on any sheet that was not. She entered it so: *one wall; thirty-three of the forty-one; none strange; a sample, and no more.* It would have been very easy to write a larger sentence, and she had struck larger sentences out of better people's work.
    18	
    19	There was the other thing, between the courses.
    20	
    21	She had seen it. It had been a hand below her chin for a quarter of an hour, on a wall above a spring: the line that stood by itself, with its small divisions, as it stood between the courses under the Stair. She had counted those divisions there against her short cord, twice, and they had given her the radius of the circle. Here she had had a pencil in one hand and her own weight on the other, and no cord at all.
    22	
    23	*The lone line is present. Divisions seen; not counted; not measured.*
    24	
    25	She read that over, and let it stand. It was true, and it was less than she wanted, and she had learned from a man with apple trees to tell those two apart.
    26	
    27	A blanket had come onto her shoulders at some hour of the first afternoon. She had not seen it arrive. She had noticed, some time later, that she was no longer shaking, and had put up a hand, and found wool; and under her fingers at the corner there was a darn that she knew, since she had watched it made by lamplight in a loft, in very small stitches, by a person who mended his own things and nobody else's.
    28	
    29	She had not turned round. She had gone on writing, and had drawn it a little closer.
    30	
    31	---
    32	
    33	He came himself on the second evening, when she had reached the figure that she could not supply.
    34	
    35	She had her own reach for each of the three, in cords. What she wanted beside it was the other man's: the figure that a surveyor had gone over twice with his pencil, in a room under a floor, until it cut the paper. She had heard it read out once. She had written it on her cuff, in the dark of that room, and the cuff had since been in a marsh.
    36	
    37	She had not asked. She had only stopped, with the pencil over the place, and looked at her wrist.
    38	
    39	Seln sat down on his heels at her elbow. He had the limp little book out of his coat already, open at the first page, and he held it to her fire at the height of her eyes, with his thumb beside the line.
    40	
    41	"*Reach, edge to centre,*" he said. "As he has it."
    42	
    43	She copied the figure. She copied it into the second leaf, and the third. He turned the book a little toward the light for each, and did not once offer to put it into her hand; it stayed in his, with two of his fingers closed on its spine, as it had stayed there for five days.
    44	
    45	She understood that well enough. She had lived for years beside shelves that she was allowed to read at and forbidden to carry from. And he had been the keeper of one shut thing for twelve years; it was not to be supposed that he would take up a second and hold it open to everybody. He had come twenty yards across the turf with it, unasked, which was a great deal farther than she had expected.
    46	
    47	"His eight stations," she said. "The figure that he has against each. Is it this one?"
    48	
    49	"It is this one, eight times."
    50	
