   173	"Thirty-four," he said. "And beneath the number, the stroke and the bar."
   174	
   175	He moved to the next on his knees, and the next.
   176	
   177	"Thirty-four. Thirty-four. Every one."
   178	
   179	Lira had gone along the fence to the left, counting under her breath, and Brom to the right. They came back together.
   180	
   181	"Forty," said Lira, "and it doesn't stop. I could see a dozen more."
   182	
   183	"Forty on mine," said Brom. "The same."
   184	
   185	Karis had her lamp lit by then. She had set a point on its wick with her own hand, outside that line, and she had the long cord laid along the feet of the bars with Cael at its far end. There was no call for anybody's foot. The line was marked for her at every pace, in iron, by people who had wished it to be found.
   186	
   187	She laid it twenty times. At each cord's end she set her short cord from the last bar to the line that the long one would next take, as she had on three other rounds. Cael, holding the lamp for her, saw that the short measure did not come to the knot that it had always come to. It came to half.
   188	
   189	She did it twenty times because she did not believe it, and he watched her cease to disbelieve it at about the twelfth.
   190	
   191	They had gone twenty cords along that fence when she stood up, with the short cord in her fist. It was full dark.
   192	
   193	"Half the bend is twice the round. Four hundred. Near enough. Twice the radius. The same shape, at twice the size, round a thing I can't see."
   194	
   195	Her voice was quite steady. She opened her notebook against the lamp, and did the sum in it, with her lips moving. She had said two hundred of the Stair, by her eye, and had been twenty short and a stride; and he saw the figure that she wrote now, which was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from a line that she had not crossed to a middle that she had not seen.
   196	
   197	Seln had not got up from his knees. He was looking along the bars, away into the dark, as he looked through a paper.
   198	
   199	"That's not a survey. That's a cordon. They've been here more than once."
   200	
   201	Nobody asked him who. He had said the name of it once, at a stake, and it had not grown any smaller since.
   202	
   203	"There was a sheet with two," he said, after a breath. "I told you that I could not place it. A marsh three days behind us, and this: I can place it now."
   204	
   205	Lira was standing with her right hand closed on the head of a bar. She was on the outside of it, and she kept her boots there.
   206	
   207	"Twice the size. Is it twice the *anything else*?"
   208	
   209	It was the question, and all five of them knew it. A line half this size had put out five sigils at a stride and given them back at a stride, the same, with nothing lost. Would one of twice the size give them back? There was no cord that could tell her that. Somebody would have to walk over that iron with a Path in her and come out again; and the one person who could have stood at the fence with her sleeves up was two days gone through a gap.
   210	
   211	"I'm not putting a foot over that," said Brom. "I've one arm, and I've been told what it's good for, and there's nobody on this side of it to tell me again."
   212	
   213	"I have not a single sheet of this place," said Karis. "I should like that entered beside whatever is ruled. I want it as I wanted the Stair. I am not going to say that I would go alone, since I have learned what that sentence costs other people."
   214	
   215	"There are forty bars to the left of me that I have counted, and a great many more that I have not," said Seln. "I should want to walk every one of them by daylight before I advised anybody to stand inside them. I do not think that we have the bread."
   216	
   217	Cael had waited for the four. There was a silence after the fourth, of about the length that a hoarse voice would have taken to say its piece; and he let it run to its end.
   218	
   219	"Not without her. Not without knowing what a circle twice the size does to the four of you. And not without the map finished."
   220	
   221	"It's finished," Karis said. "It's a line."
   222	
   223	"Then it's a line that points somewhere. We go home and find out where."
   224	
   225	She had the hide out under the lamp before he had done speaking. She made a fourth ring on it, at the very edge of the skin, where there was no ink of the carter's at all, and wrote no word beside it; and she laid the brass through her three dots. It ran through the middle of the new ring, and off the hide, and on across the lid of the box.
   226	
   227	They climbed back to the crest and slept there, in the wind, with the valley black below them and nothing lit in it. He wrote by her lamp, when the others were down.
   228	
   229	*Four sites. One line. The last one's twice the size and the Compact fenced it. The bearing runs through it and keeps going. Karis says four points is an argument. I say it's a bearing — the kind their maps draw as nothing. Home is Lowmarch now. I just wrote that without noticing. Nineteen, ten fragments, a still place at the center of me, and a line on a map that somebody drew before there were maps. We're not done. We're oriented.*

