/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md' in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Chapter 48 — Two Stakes
     2	
     3	"Well?" said Oryn.
     4	
     5	She had called the halt herself, an hour south of the fork, where a grey rock as big as a cart stood up out of the ridge and broke the wind. The two mules were nose to tail in its lee. Brom had the bread out. She had sat down on a ledge of the rock with her coat pulled round her, and had looked at Cael across the trodden grass for as long as it takes to count ten before she spoke.
     6	
     7	He knew what she meant, though he had been turned away at a bench three hours before, in six words, and had gone and sat down, and it had not once crossed his mind since that the matter might be opened again by her.
     8	
     9	It was hers to open. He saw that, and he saw also that she would not have him take a thing that he had not asked for; if he was to have it, he must ask it outright of the woman who was sitting on that rock.
    10	
    11	"May I read you?"
    12	
    13	"Yes. That's the answer, and I'm the one giving it, and I want you to notice that it's the same day as the lean-to and a different question, and that the rule held both times. Put your hand on it. Surface first. Count of ten. Don't go in past the count until I say."
    14	
    15	Lira had stopped chewing, and Karis, who had her notebook half out of her coat, pushed it back in. Cael had not stirred. There were two words in that answer which he could not make fit.
    16	
    17	"A different question?"
    18	
