     1	# Chapter 50 — The Square of Grey
     2	
     3	They had the day in that room by the colour of one small square.
     4	
     5	It stood at the head of forty steps, a man's height on each side; for the first hours after the lamp went out it was grey, and everything else was black. Cael learned the room by its sounds.
     6	
     7	Lira sat on the thirty-fourth step, six above the floor, with the bow along her knee, and Seln stood below her in the doorway at the stair's foot. Brom was on the ground against the wall to the north of that doorway, with three coats on him, and the healer sat on her heels at his left hand. Karis had put her back against the wall opposite; and in the middle of the floor, where nobody went, a case lay in the dark with its cord.
     8	
     9	That was six below, and there were six above; he had Seln's word for it every hour.
    10	
    11	Seln went up for them himself. He climbed to the fifth step from the top and no higher, and lay along the stair with his chin on the stone, so that his eyes came level with the floor outside and nothing else of him did. From there, he said, the rim stood all round against the sky like the edge of a dish, and he counted what was on it. Then he came down, without a sound, and said it to the room in his mild voice.
    12	
    13	"Six. Two at the gate, two south, one west, one north. None has left his place."
    14	
    15	"What are they doing?" said Brom, the first time.
    16	
    17	"The one at the gate with the bow is doing what you would do. He is finding his range."
    18	
    19	They heard it for themselves before he had finished speaking.
    20	
    21	It was a small hard tick on the roof over their heads, like a pebble dropped on a lid, and after it a long dry skittering that went away across the stone. Karis drew her breath in, and nobody spoke until it had stopped.
    22	
    23	"That is five," said Seln. "He means to learn whether he can put one into that hole from where he stands. The stair runs down to the west, and he is to the east of it and a little above. He is the only one of the four who could."
    24	
    25	"Can he?" said Lira, from the stair.
    26	
    27	"I should say that it will take him some time."
    28	
    29	It took him the better part of the forenoon. He did not hurry. A tick came, and a skittering, and then nothing for as long as it takes to walk a quarter of a mile; and then another. Cael counted them, since he could not help it: some fell short and rang on the far side of the roof, and one struck so near the hole that they heard it skate past the lip.
    30	
    31	"Six. Seven. Eight," said Seln, at intervals, from the doorway. He might have been reading out a floor schedule. "Nine. Ten. Eleven."
    32	
    33	The square had gone from grey to white. It was near noon, and bright up there, and a little of the brightness came down as far as the tenth step and lay on it like spilt milk.
    34	
    35	Then there was a sound that was not on the roof.
    36	
    37	It was in the stair. It began high up, a crack and a rattle, and it came down toward them very fast, striking one edge and the next, like a stick drawn along a fence. Cael heard Lira's breath stop, and he heard the thing leap, and strike, and slide.
    38	
    39	It stopped.
    40	
    41	For the length of three breaths nobody moved. Then Lira spoke, and her voice was quite level.
    42	
    43	"It's on the thirty-first step. I can see the feathers. That's three above my knee."
    44	
    45	"Twelve," said Seln.
    46	
    47	"He's got the hole," said Brom.
    48	
    49	"He has the hole. He has not the stair. That bolt came in at the top and spent itself on thirty steps of stone; it would not have gone through a coat when it reached her." Seln had not raised his voice. "He knows that as well as I do. He wished us to know that he can reach the door. It is a kind of knock."
    50	
    51	Cael had gone to the foot of the stair, where he could see her against the white, a dark shape with one arm, and the bow lying along her knee with its nose toward the square. It had not stirred.
    52	
    53	"You didn't shoot," he said.
    54	
    55	"At what? There's nobody in it." She did not turn her head. "I've one in the groove. If I loose it at a patch of sky I've an empty bow that I can't span, and Seln has to come up six steps into the light to do it for me. He'd like that, up there."
    56	
    57	She let her breath go out.
    58	
    59	"I've stopped. I'll go on being stopped till there's something standing in that square." Her right hand moved on the stock, a finger's breadth, and lay still. "It stays in the bow."
    60	
    61	---
    62	
    63	The white lasted a long time, and they filled it with small things, since there was nothing else to fill it with.
    64	
    65	There was the water, of which they had five flasks among six, since the healer had come over a stone at a run with nothing in her hands; and Brom, who could not lift his right arm, had them all set down in front of him in the dark and shook each of them at his ear.
    66	
    67	"Two swallows apiece at noon," he said. "And I'm keeping two full, shut, for the top. I'll not have us get out of this hole and find that we've nothing to walk on."
    68	
    69	"You're very sure that we get out," said Lira, from the stair.
    70	
    71	"I'm sure about the water. Somebody's got to be sure about something."
    72	
    73	There was the bread, which was his own, and black on both faces. He broke it into six by feel, and ate his share with a great deal of noise, so that the healer should hear him do it.
    74	
    75	"I've a list," he said presently. He said it to the roof. "I've been keeping it since the ford. There was a dog that had my arm, and I drowned it. There was a cart that I had by the tail. A bowstring. Now there's a man on a hill whom I never saw." He shifted, and grunted. "It's been a better class of thing each time. I'd like that noticed."
    76	
    77	"It's noticed," said Karis.
    78	
    79	"What's next after a man on a hill?"
    80	
