   343	He had been over the floor, and through the grass below the stone, and he had an armful of ash shafts, which he laid on the turf in a row beside four crossbows, one of which was in two pieces. Lira drew the one from her belt and laid it at the end of the row. There was an iron head lying by the stone that had no shaft to it; the boy looked at that, and at Brom, and went rather red.
   344	
   345	Brom had taken up one of the shafts, and he tried it along the stock of the healer's bow, where it stood proud of its channel by the thickness of a finger, and would not lie down.
   346	
   347	"Too stout. They'll not go in hers." He put it back in the row. "I'd as soon not have them, come to that."
   348	
   349	Teague had come down from the brow, and he looked at a tarred case on the turf, and at Cael, and he put no question about either.
   350	
   351	"We've a stake to set at a weir," he said. "There's four marks hangs on it." His eyes went to the calfskin that Karis was unrolling. "I'll tell the one-armed man that he's five strokes to look at. He'll want to know what he's to write."
   352	
   353	He went, with his three behind him at their distances.
   354	
   355	Cael sat down against the grey stone, in the full sun, and opened Hesk's book.
   356	
   357	*Hundred and fifteenth day. The stone at the Stair. All six up, and nothing spent. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three. A hundred and two in hand. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against a broker's mark, and that mark is never going on this paper.*
   358	
   359	*Instruments recovered. The contract's paid, and the man who posted it is never going to see them, because Seln's keeping the ledger and I'm keeping the kit. Someone measured this place five years ago and wrote bearing and stopped. Karis has a straightedge. She's been waiting her whole life to use it. The Ring, the Stair, and whatever's on that bearing. Pike said a handful. We have two. The kit says three. We're going to go look.*
   360	
   361	He shut the book.
   362	
   363	Karis had the calfskin flat on the lid of the middle box, with a pebble on each corner. There were three rings on it in pencil: one lay two days east of a square with a dot, and one lay a day south of a ring with a bar, and a little aside, and she had struck out the word that stood beside it.
   364	
   365	She took the strip of brass out of its cloth, which had come from Ternhall, across a Line and a mountain. She had told them on the ninety-second evening that the carter's hide was a map of days, and would not take an edge; and she had not forgotten that she said it.
   366	
   367	"I am going to find out how far wrong a map of days can be," said Karis, "along one line."
   368	
   369	She held the brass over the hide, in both hands, a finger's breadth above two rings. It had not yet touched anything.

