editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:5:She had called the halt herself, an hour south of the fork, where a grey rock as big as a cart stood up out of the ridge and broke the wind. The two mules were nose to tail in its lee. Brom had the bread out. She had sat down on a ledge of the rock with her coat pulled round her, and had looked at Cael across the trodden grass for as long as it takes to count ten before she spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:13:"Yes. That's the answer, and I'm the one giving it, and I want you to notice that it's the same day as the lean-to and a different question, and that the rule held both times. Put your hand on it. Surface first. Count of ten. Don't go in past the count until I say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:35:He let it go, and under his breastbone the tenth fragment, the lightest thing that he carried, turned toward his hand, as a person turns her head at her own name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:101:He eased his neck. He had seen a man's shin close under those hands in an hour, and had watched her walk to a mule and unload it, for five days that she could not spare; and he had taken her for a person who gave things up. She gave nothing up. She was made as a road is made, to get to where the hurt was, and everything in her that was not road had been spent on it years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:117:She said it without any pride, and without the least surprise, as she might have agreed that her coat was brown. Then she got up off the rock and went to take the grey mule's rope out of Brom's hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:119:Cael stayed where he was a little longer, with his right hand lying on his knee like something that he had borrowed, and he was as hungry as he had been after a day under barley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:127:They came to the end of the ridge with the sun a hand above the western hills, and the ground opened under them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:131:The ridge did not run down into a valley. It broke off, as a table breaks off at its edge, and its western face went down in one long slope of pale grass to a low place that lay below them like the print of an enormous thumb. It was a bowl, though so shallow that a cart might have been driven anywhere in it. He put it at five hundred yards from side to side, which was far wider than the swell that the Fallow Ring had stood on, and it had not half that swell's height.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:133:The carters' ruts did what he had seen them do once before. Halfway down the slope the ground eased into a kind of brow, and at the brow the live ruts swung away to the north and went round the whole of that low place, keeping their distance from it, and came back to their line on the far side. He could see them all the way, two pale threads on the grass, and inside them an older road went straight on down, grown over, with a hundred years of turf in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:137:It was the same grey, and he knew that grey now as he knew the colour of his own hands. It lay in the lowest part of the bowl, round, and a good deal bigger than the other had been, and the low sun slid over it without finding anything to catch on. There was no ring of stones, and not an upright thing on it of any kind; it was as bare as a threshing floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:149:Nobody moved, and the wind had dropped; in all that low ground there was not a bird, or a beast, or any smoke; there was a bare grey floor with a square cut in it, and three steps that somebody had made for feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:151:Seln went down first, as he went down everything: he walked the old road to the brow and stood there; then he went along the live ruts to the north until he was small, and came back, and went a little way south. When he returned to them his face told nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:153:"There are runs in the grass, on the north side. They are old. Nothing has used them since the frosts began." He glanced at the healer. "I should have looked for a den, and I find none from outside. I do not know why, and I shall not guess. On the ruts there is no mark newer than a salt cart's, a week old."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:161:At forty paces she stopped as if she had come to a wall. Her sigil had gone out at that stride, as it had gone out on the Fallow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:167:One by one they came down and tried it, since no one of them would take another's word for such a thing: Lira, and Brom, and Seln, each a stride past the stone and a stride back. The healer went over last, with two fingers on her own wrist and her lips moving, and came out again and nodded. Cael walked across beside Brom, and felt what he had felt on the Fallow, which was grass under his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:177:She had gone a few steps along the line to the north, with her eyes on the turf, as she went along any new ground; and she stopped with the toe of the great boot against something that stood a hand's length clear of the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:181:It was the same pattern as the one that Cael had knelt by on the ninety-sixth day, a cord north of a grey stone: a square bar as thick as two fingers, with its head beaten down by a mallet. This one was scaled all over with rust, in leaves, so that it looked more like a thing grown than a thing made. A thumb's breadth under the head there was the registry's stamp, and under the stamp, punched deep, were two figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:185:"You said *like as not*," said Karis. "I shall enter that you said it, and the day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:195:It stood on the south side of the grown-over ruts, eight feet from the first, with Karis's stone between the two. It was the same bar, but the rust on it was thin and bright, a skin and not a bark, and the grass about its foot had not yet healed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:199:"That first has stood five cold terms. I said so of its fellow on the Fallow, and I see no cause to alter it." He bent closer. "This has had one. Two, at the most. And it is the same number. Thirty-two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:221:There were two bars of iron, eight feet apart, with a road going between them. Anchor had been asking at every sill and gate and rail for the better part of a year, and it asked now, in its thin held note: here was a doorway, and here were two posts to it, and would he not like something set across? He told it no, as he had told it at a stake on the Fallow. He left unexamined whatever might have been set there already, since he had no cause to examine it; and that refusal he was to go over more than once in the day that followed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:249:"We go at first light. We go in file, ten yards apart, down the old road: myself, and then Lira, and Karis, and Cael, with Brom last. The bow is spanned before we cross, with a bolt in the groove, and Brom carries it. Each of us has a day's water and a day's bread on his back. I have known a morning's errand to take a day." His eyes went to the boxes. "Nothing else goes over. The three boxes stay at this stone with both beasts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:275:*Hundred and thirteenth day, night. At the edge of the second one. A stone, and two bars, both numbered thirty-two; one five cold terms old, one a cold term or two, with a mark under its number that Seln won't read. A floor with no stones on it, and a hole, and a stair. Nobody on the ruts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:277:*Tide: once, on the healer, on the ridge road. I asked twice today and she granted the second. The short count and then a minute of the long, at one sitting; I am counting that as one, since it was one asking and one yes. At three there were banks. I knew the Path by ten. In the minute, one channel and one thing in it, going somewhere. It cost her nothing that she would notice. It cost me the use of that hand for an hour, and most of a loaf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:283:He slept a little toward morning. When Brom woke him the stars were going out, and the frost lay so thick on the old road that it showed like a chalk line all the way down to the floor. Seln was coming back along the ruts from the north, and he shook his head at them from thirty yards away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:9:"The margin is pricked by the same wheel," said Seln. "There is a tooth missing from it. Every eleventh hole stands a little wide of its neighbours, on both sheets, in the same place. And the hand is one hand. He makes the foot of that letter with a second stroke, which is a fault, and he has made it twice." He lowered the papers. "They were written at one desk, by one clerk, I should say at one sitting. He ruled two sheets and filled them one after the other, and they were folded into the same wrapper before the ink of the second was a day old."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:17:"The first was a question, and I said so at a stake. It sent a crew to fetch what was not there, in order to learn what became of the crew. A hundred marks is a fair price for a question. It is high, since nobody had ever taken such a paper; but a carter could believe in it." His finger moved to the bracket. "Nobody who has kept a book believes in this. It is two days out and two home, for a case that one person can carry on a strap. There is not a hold on that sheet that would pay thirty for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:23:The fire settled, and Cael kept his eyes on a sheet of cream paper and went back, since he could not help it, to an evening at an inn's boards twenty-two days before. Four of them had given reasons for the first paper. He had given his own last but one, and it had been two words, and he had been rather proud of how plain they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:27:Cael gave it to him: south of Lowmarch, and the first day, and where the road drops off the ridge and the ground opens.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:35:Nobody at that fire needed the list read out. There was a Wind on it, and an Iron Skin, and an Ember, and a Shadow, each with a tier and a rank, in files that had been kept for years on the far side of the Line. And there was a boat gone west ten days ago to say that every one of those four had walked onto a certain kind of ground and been able to do nothing there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:43:His hand had gone to the strap across his chest, as it went a dozen times a day, and he did not seem to know that it had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:47:He said it to the fire, in the mild voice in which he had once read out a floor schedule, and then he sat back out of the light. Cael understood him well enough: a broker's clerk had stood at a plank for a hundred days and watched which crew took which paper, and it had wanted only one fact about a boy to write the second sheet; the boy had supplied that fact himself, aloud, at an inn's boards, when he showed that he had never yet walked past a paper that paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:59:"He's just told you what it is. It's got a ruled edge and a word for itself. I don't need it read to me a third time." Her right hand had closed on the knot. "There's a walk on that oak at fifty that nobody's scratched, a string of salt to the middle holds before the pass shuts. There's a fence at forty, the day the hold comes off it. That's ninety marks of work that was posted by people who want a fence. We'd be down from both before the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:63:"I'm coming to them. You said four wheres. One's walked. One's this, with a paper nailed across it." She nodded at the sheets on Seln's knee. "The third's a long way south and east, with no road inside a day of it, and nobody's put a price on it at all. So we go and pace that one, on our own time, with a healer at the edge. I'm not saying leave them alone. I'm saying I'll not walk into the one that somebody's paying us to walk into."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:79:Cael kept still, since it was the third time in twelve days that Brom had spoken of that house, and he had never before told a whole thing out of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:85:"It went over backward. Not every day. It would carry you a fortnight like a lamb, and then it would stand straight up on a hard road and come down on its own saddle. It broke his collarbone, and a groom's leg, and the groom never walked right after." He picked the stick up again. "The dealer hadn't cheated him. That's what I've kept of it. The dealer had priced that horse to a penny. It was my father who read the figure as luck."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:99:"I am saying it first, so that it is not taken for a vote on anybody else's arm. I have a cup of barley and a bowl in the middle box. In the cup are the kinds that I should swear to. In the bowl are the kinds that only a second floor can give me or take away, and I have had my thumb over the figure for nine days." Her voice was small and quite steady. "A man with apple trees told me that I should not tell a keeper from an eater in the one basket. I have one basket. I shall have one basket until I have stood on another of those floors with paper in my hand, and here is another, a day and a half from this fire."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:133:"Seln's read the paper right, and Brom's read the number right. It's a trap, and it's been cut to our measure by somebody with our files open. Everything in it has been reckoned." He kept his voice low. "But it stands on one belief, which is that a certain kind of floor does the same to all of us. And that's the one thing about this crew that nobody in the west can find out. They sent a paper to learn whether we'd come out of the first, and we did, and what they've learned from it is wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:155:Lira's eyebrows went up, and Cael felt his own go. In two years on a bluff, and a hundred and twelve days of roads, he had never heard Brom repeat what another person had said to him. Brom carried other people's words inside his shirt, on dead slips, and did not say them over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:189:*Hundred and twelfth day. Lowmarch, the meadow. Pike said the eighth line in our hearing, twice. Eleventh; Teague ten up. He says his book has no column for what the line means. A hundred and two in hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:213:Pike looked at it once, and then at Cael, and then at the sheet again; and when he had done he opened the horn pot and dipped his pen without a word. He wrote five short strokes at the foot, side by side, as he had written them on another paper twenty-two days before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:223:He got up, which he seldom did once he was down, and turned his shoulder to what was left of the room. He gave it out as he gave out a line: the recovery, and the Stair road, and the ridge, and the three hundred. He said that it was taken, and by whom. And then he said the other word, and he said it slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:237:"You're taking the Stair contract."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:247:"My crew's at Millrace in two days," Teague said. "If you're late back, we'll come look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:249:He had said four things to Cael at a ford, and every one of them had been a price. This was not a price. Cael went over it for the catch, since he had been taught in a hard school to look for one, and there was none in it: there was a place, and a day, and four people who would walk up a ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:269:Seln could hear the dictation in it. *South of Lowmarch; the first day.* A clerk left to himself would have put *one day south*. Somebody had stood at that man's shoulder with a surveyor's notes in his hand and read them out a stop at a time, and the clerk had set them down as they came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:271:So there had been two people in that room, at the least: there was a hand that wrote, and a voice that knew the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:307:But a boy had come up a hill with a word on his file where a tier should have been, and had looked at every instrument that was turned on him as though somebody had built it. That had been the end of the weather. If a thing was built, then there was a builder, with a walk, and a way of holding a pen, and a tiredness at the end of the day. Seln could no longer do without seeing one: he wished to stand on the same ground as a single one of them, in daylight, and learn what a person looked like who did this for his living.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:321:The paper followed the case. He had said so himself. If the case were to go south tonight, by itself, on one man's chest, then by first light there would be nothing at this fire that anybody in the west had priced. Five people would wake to a walk at fifty and a fence at forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:323:He knew the south road; he had walked the first hours of it alone in their second week here, as he had walked every road out of this town. He could be on the ridge before the stars turned, and he would have his face, very likely; and an arm two weeks old would never come within a mile of that floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:337:The case lay where it had lain, against his breastbone, with its lock toward him. He knew what was in it to the leaf. He had not opened it since a reading room above Ostrand, and he did not open it now; there was nothing in it that would tell him who was waiting at the foot of a ridge, and there was one leaf in it that was not a copy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:3:They were out of Lowmarch two hours before the light on the hundred and thirteenth day, with the stars still hard over the river and every blade in the meadow standing white.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:5:It was Seln's hour, and Cael had given it to him without argument. A loaded cart took the whole of a short day to reach the mill; a crew that walked as they walked could be on the ridge by sunrise and at the fork before noon, and Seln wished to have the afternoon in hand. So Brom loaded the mule by feel. Lira ate standing. They went past Pike's bare plank and the smith's cold chimney, and took the south road in file, with the frost ringing under five pairs of boots and four small hooves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:7:They passed the well at the fork in the dark. It had a new rope on its windlass, and somebody had mended the curb where six stones had been set; and Cael, who had hauled foul air out of that shaft for three days on a bitten leg, put his hand on the stonework as he went by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:9:The road climbed from there. By the time there was grey enough to see by they were on the back of a long ridge, bare and broad, with pale grass on it that the wind had combed all one way. The country fell off on both hands. To the east it was hills beyond hills, hatched with the red-barked trees that Karis would not name. To the west the ground went down in long folds toward a valley that he could not yet see into, and a line of mist lay along the bottom of it like wool caught on a fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:13:"The mill water," said Karis. She had the calfskin half unrolled against her chest, and she was reading it as she walked. "He draws it with three strokes where it is forded and with one where it is not. It comes round out of the hills to the north and east. A person leaving that hold by its northern wicket would meet it on the second morning, and it would bring her here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:15:The sun came up on their left hand, small and white. It showed them their own breath, and the mule's, and a road that ran on south along the ridge as far as they could see; and a little before the middle of the forenoon it showed them a place where the ruts divided.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:17:One pair kept the ridge. The other turned down the western face, slantwise, through a notch that wheels had worn, and went out of sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:31:The answer came from a lean-to that had been built against the mill's southern wall, where the sun fell; and it came at once, low and hoarse, from somebody who had not troubled to come to the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:45:It was a bad hand. He was one of the miller's men, and a rope had taken it nine days before, against the drum of the hoist that lifted the sacks; it had been bound up in a meal-bag since, and it lay in her palms like a thing that had been trodden on. She had her eyes shut. Her lips were moving, and Cael found that he knew where she was by the count: at three she had the banks, and by seven she had gone along the bed to where it was stopped. He had lain under those hands for twenty minutes. He had a fragment in him of what she was doing, the tenth, and it sat quiet under his breastbone and did not stir toward her, since it stopped at the length of his own arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:51:It was a girl of three or four, carried in by her father, with a wet rattling breath that Cael could hear from the bench. The healer did not put her on the plank. She sat down on a keg and took the child on her own knee, with one broad hand spread on the small back and the other on the breast, and bent her cropped head until her ear was against the shoulder-blades. She stayed so for a long time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:53:He saw her face over the child's hair. There was another chest, at another gate, that she had last listened to on the eighty-ninth evening. She had counted the days to it on a hill, aloud, with nobody by. None of that showed. She had one child under her hands, and she gave it the whole of herself, as she gave everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:65:"You were to wait for me on your own doorstep. I'd put a day by for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:81:"That's three," she said to the floor. "That's the day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:121:He took Hesk's book out of the satchel. He had written nothing in it yet that day, and he wetted the pencil and put this down before anything else, with the date over it, so that it should stand first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:123:*Hundred and thirteenth day. Millrace, the healer's bench, a little after noon. Asked her leave to touch the boy's wrist while she held it, so that I could learn what came to her hand. She said no. I stepped back. Entered before anything else today, since it is the first refusal under the rule and I want it to head the leaf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:133:He put it down, with her initial; and he had hardly lifted the pencil when a hoarse voice from the lean-to said that the arm had taken no harm on two days of frozen road, that all eight were as they had been, and that the knot had been retied by somebody who tied like a carter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:141:"That's my bench, then. Which of you is going to tell me why you're a day south of your board, with a mule of mine and three boxes, on a road that goes nowhere but a ford?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:147:He gave it in order and left nothing out: a line that was said twice at a hearth, and a nail, and a sheet of cream paper that had lain sealed in a broker's box beside the first. He gave her the sum on it. He told her what Seln had found in its margin, and what the five of them had made of it at a fire, and that one of the five had said walk away and been entered. He said that the place was half a day up the ridge from the fork over her head, and that he meant to be at its edge by dusk and across it in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:149:"You said not without you. So I've not come to ask you for anything. I've come so that you should have it from me today, with the bench done, and not from a carter in a fortnight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:153:"I was to be at this gate on the ninety-sixth. I came in on the hundred and twelfth. That's sixteen." The thumb went down. "There's a day's bench here still, that I'd have done tomorrow, and the road beyond it: two gates, and then the ford with the oxen on the hundred and twenty-first, where they looked for me on the hundred and fourth. That's seventeen." A finger. "Now put your ridge into it. It's a half-day up to wherever this is, and I'd not see the place before dark. A day while you're in it. A half-day down. That's two, if every one of you walks out on his own legs, and you've not once done that since I knew you." Another finger. "So the ford's nineteen. And beyond the ford there's a child that I heard on the eighty-ninth and can't hear now before the hundred and twenty-eighth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:171:"No. There isn't one." She got up off the keg. "You've a girl there with a fortnight's arm, and you're taking her onto a floor that somebody chose for her. I said that somebody should stand outside with her sleeves up. I meant it when I said it. I didn't think that I'd be shown the place inside three days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:179:"That one-armed man said it under his eave, and wrote it. A clerk said it to a boat." She did not turn round. "I've a round. It's seven gates and twenty days and it's the only thing that I own. A woman who's one of six belongs to the six. I've seen what that does to a healer; she ends up mending the same four people for the rest of her days and calling it a calling."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:187:"You go north tomorrow," said Oryn. "You'll say this at every fence you stop at, in these words, and you'll have it said on to the ford with the oxen. The healer's late. She was seventeen at the ford, and she's nineteen now, or more. She's a half-day south of the mill, at the edge of bad ground, since there's a crew gone onto it that she's had her hands in." She waited until his lips had done moving. "And at the gate beyond the ford, where the little one is: if the breathing changes, they're to send down the water to this mill, and the mill sends up the ridge. I'll come from wherever I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:189:"Late, nineteen, bad ground south of the mill," said the carter. "And the little one sends here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:209:"And I go when I go. The day that the round can't spare me, I turn north, and nobody says a word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:245:*Hundred and thirteenth day, the fork above Millrace. Oryn came. On her terms. She'll turn north the day she has to and I won't ask her not to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:247:He read it over. Then he looked at six people on a bare ridge, and at the left-hand ruts, which ran on to the south with the white sun over them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:3:The bar came out of Thornwater's western gate on the hundred and eleventh morning for five people, three boxes and a mule that belonged to none of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:13:They went down the stretch at the one time of day when nothing had ever come up it. Cael walked last. He had the white stone on his left hand at a hundred and ten yards, and he gave it the look that he gave it every time. Fifty-seven evenings had gone by since anything stood there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:15:Lira was in front of him, and he watched how she went. The ankle was taped, and under a healer's order still, so that she set the great boot down whole at every stride, heel and toe together, and rolled nowhere. She had done it for fifteen days on turf. On frozen ruts it was a different trade. Twice in the first mile the boot skated, and twice her right arm went out and her left stayed where a knot held it; she had been told that she was to fall on her face sooner than put that hand out, and he saw that she had taught the hand to believe it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:41:They made their camp that night on a carters' halting-ground a little above the wash, with the mule hobbled at the windward end and the three boxes under her blanket. Seln lit a small fire with his flint. They had kept that custom since the fifty-ninth day, on the strength of a finding, and it was a comfort still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:45:He owed a healer two readings of himself with the tenth fragment, on two different days, with a night between; and after those he owed her the whole of it at once. She had set it at a well that morning, with a pannier on the coping. Nine days had gone by since the first reading, in a loft, and he had let every one of them pass. She had seen why from a stride and a half. He had found a place in himself that did not move, and he had been afraid of it, and he had liked it better as a line on a leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:53:*Hundred and eleventh day. The halting-ground above the wash. One reading done, two owed, and nothing tried tonight; I was in a hurry, and she never asked for that. Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:57:They came over the saddle above Lowmarch in the afternoon of the hundred and twelfth day, and he stopped on the crown of it and counted what was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:61:The cold had been at it. Where forty fires had burned when the roads were dry, he made out twelve, drawn in close about the great ash, and half the trodden grass had gone back to white. The ferry's cable stood across the water like a chalk line, with frost on it. The scow lay on the near bank. A brown gelding with one white foot was walking his circle in the capstan-shed's doorway with a blanket on his back, and Cael was glad to see that somebody had thought of the blanket. Both inns were smoking. There was a skin of ice along the eastern bank as wide as a cart, and beyond it the river ran dark and fast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:73:She put it exactly as they had been told to put it, in a loft, by the healer herself. The round man took no offence at being asked a thing before he had been wished good day, and he answered her as a man answers who has been asked the same question at every door for a fortnight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:75:"Ours. She came in at Millrace last night, down the mill water off the hill track, with a rope on her shoulder and no beast. It's the next gate on her round after that grain hold of yours, and she's sixteen days behind at it." He jerked his chin at the canvas. "I'm the mill's carter. I left a bench full when I pulled out this morning, and my own sister's boy on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:77:"Millrace," said Karis. "I have it on the hide: a square with a dot, a day down the south road from this town."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:79:"A carter's day. Less, for legs." He looked at the brown mule with some attention, and then at the boxes, and Cael watched him decide that it was no business of his. "You'll want to be quick, if it's her you want. She's never more than two days at our fence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:83:They stood with it on the hill for a breath. She had told them that if they wanted her sooner than Thornwater they were to ask a carter, and they had asked the first carter that they met, and she was a day from where they stood. Cael had expected to count gates for a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:89:"I'm only saying that it's a day south, and I don't know of anything we want to the south."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:97:"He's not said ours," said Brom. "The Fallow. He wrote it eleven days ago, and sent it up to us on a piece of bark, and it's not been said where we could hear it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:109:It was the eighth line under their heading. Cael had read it on a scrap of bark in a grain hold, across the bones of a goat, and he knew every word; but he had never heard it said. Pike gave the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the broker's posting. He said that it was taken and witnessed. He said what had not been found there, and that the crew had come back. He gave the count of those who went in and of those who came out, which was the same count, and at the end of it he said that no price was paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:115:It was a thin house. There were perhaps twenty people on benches that had held forty, and every one of them had heard that line on the hundred and first day, when it was written. They had not then had the five to look at. Cael felt the room turn its attention on them as he had felt a yard do it once, at an academy on a hill. But this was no registry's look. Nobody's eyes went to where a tier ought to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:119:Three old men sat beyond her, at boards of their own. Cael had seen them under the ash for a hundred days without ever learning what they did, three grey heads over a pair of dice. The dice lay on the wood between them now. The eldest had his hand half closed over them, and he neither threw nor gathered them up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:121:By the hearth sat the old carter who had come east with them from the first ford, and had asked Cael on a road whether he was paper. He had his chin lifted. He was looking past the five at the dark doorway behind them, steadily, as a man looks who wishes to see whether anything else is coming in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:Pike had dipped his pen. He did not write with it. The line had been in his book for eleven days. He raised his eyes to the sailcloth over his head, and found the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:161:"I'll make one the day I know what goes at the head of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:183:"In the broker's strong-box, with his seal on it, and him not knowing what was in it. He says so. I believe him; he's not a man who'd keep three hundred marks shut up for a month if he knew that he had them." Pike took his finger away. "I said it to the meadow, twice. Nobody scratched. Then Teague came down off his fence for flour, and read it, and hung it where you found it. That's eight days it's been on my nail."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:187:*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Stair road. South of Lowmarch; the first day; where the road drops off the ridge and the ground opens. Three hundred marks (three hundred).*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:191:For the space of a breath there was only the fire. Three hundred marks was more than twice what the five of them had earned in a hundred and twelve days. He did the sum without wishing to, and saw Brom doing it, with his lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:201:"The Stair road," said Lira. "Which is that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:203:"South. Past the well you cleared, and up onto the ridge. It's a carter's day to Millrace by it, and the road forks above the mill." Pike rubbed the stump of his arm through the pinned sleeve. "One branch drops to the ford. The other keeps the ridge half a day more, and comes off the end of it, and the carts go round what's there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:211:"A day south, and a little aside," she said. "In his hill-hatching. I rang it on the ninety-second evening, and I wrote *guess*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:11:The six on the rim were where they had been all day: two on the brow above him, two away to the south, one north, and one far off across the low ground against the last of the light. The two on the brow were on their feet; the one with the bow had it up across his chest, spanned, and the other was holding his own hands out before him and looking into them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:23:*Front. Back. Shoulders.* He loaded nothing on the third. He stood through it, with Wind lying quiet in his soles, and he watched a hooded shape forty paces up a hill. A girl had come down off a gate-walk eight days ago with a sore palm and three words, which a lean man had given her. Shooting was stopping. The stock on the brow settled into a shoulder. The shape behind it grew smaller, as a person does who is letting out his breath. And then it was entirely still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:27:The air cracked like a whip over the stone, and the frost went up off the grass in a white fan. He was twenty feet to the north, in pale stems to the knee, and something had gone through the place where he had been with a sound like cloth tearing. He heard it strike the frozen road, a long way down behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:41:It was a corridor as long as a cart and team, set down in that trough with its high end at his feet and its low end up the hill, and everything in it went one way at once. Frost went, and the seed that hung heavy on the grass at either lip, and chaff, and the dust of a hundred dry days; it went up that channel in a grey flood and burst out over the brow, twelve yards on, into two hoods.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:49:He had run up a ditch on the Fallow in that lag, with hounds behind him, and he knew it; but he had not known what it was to close with a man in it. His boots struck the turf, and then he was told that they had struck. The brow came up to meet him. There was a hooded shape in a cloud of chaff with one arm across its face, and it had let the string go half drawn and caught the bow up by its stirrup end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:61:Cael did not strike him. He could have; the man stood open, a yard off, with his cloth blown half across his hood and grass-seed in the folds of it, and there was nothing in that hood that Cael could have sworn to afterward but that it was the height of a person. He looked at it for the length of one late breath, and turned away from it, to the south.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:87:The two of them went north along the carters' road, side by side, at a trot. They did not run until they were sixty yards off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:93:To the south there were two shapes still. One of them, the one that had stood all day with its arms folded, raised a single arm above its hood and let it fall. Then both stooped, and straightened, and went away over the rim to the south, with nothing in their hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:95:When he looked to the north, there was nobody there at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:97:The west was the last. The sky there was green yet, low down, and the rim stood against it as black as a cut edge. A small upright shape had stood on that edge since first light and had never once loosed. It bent now, and laid something down. It walked away; and at the top of the rim, where another step would have taken it out of his sight, it stopped, and turned itself round, and looked back across five hundred yards of grass at a boy with a light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:111:Seln stepped over the stone, and stood a breath on the outside of it. Cael saw his shoulders come down by the width of a finger, as a man's do when a Path that he has done without all day is handed back to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:119:The healer came over between two of them, with Brom's good arm across her shoulders and his weight on her; and Cael watched her do what he had seen her do at a rock on the Fallow, eight times in one grey morning, when she thought that nobody saw. She took one stride past the stone. She stopped dead, with her eyes shut and her lips moving, and her free hand closed on Brom's wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:135:Brom made no sound that could be heard at ten yards. His boots moved on the turf. Her two hands were round the shoulder before the wood was clear, one above the place and one below; her eyes shut; and Cael, who had sat all day in a stone room and heard her count that man's heart with two fingers, saw her lips reach three and stop counting, since she had what she wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:153:"I know that it held. I felt where. I'm not scolding the arm." Oryn drew the wool up and tied it, shorter than before, so that the hand lay high against the collar-bone. "That's two, on a road, after three at a bench yesterday. There isn't a third in me tonight for anybody, and I'd thank you all to stay whole till morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:159:It came all at once, as it had come at the stone on the Fallow. Across his left palm there was a white weal as long as a finger. He had held a knife with a flame on its point in a hand too cold to know that the hilt was hot, and he had found it out only when he tried to open that hand again. He could not stop shaking. He had had a week of barley sacks and tables to rest in, and he had spent the whole of what it gave him between a stair and a brow, in less time than a kettle takes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:167:He went round the three boxes under their blanket with the lamp held low, and stooped over each cord in turn, and touched nothing. When he came back his face was as it had been all day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:181:"There was a maker's stamp here. It has been taken off with a file, and the place oiled. There is no armoury number. It was bought for one day, by somebody who meant from the first to leave it lying." His voice had not risen. Cael had never heard it so quiet. "I walked past the place where that man lay, twice. I have sat in a hole since sunrise with his bolt on the stair. I have his bow. And I could not tell you tonight whether he was tall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:193:*Hundred and fourteenth day, night. Outside the stone. All six out. Brom: a bolt through the right shoulder, drawn and mended, an hour. Lira: the arm opened on the stair wall and is shut again, a quarter. Thirteen bolts loosed at us in the day, and one hit. Ours: five, and none shot.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:195:He ruled a line, and put down what he had spent, one thing to a line, as he had on the Fallow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:251:It was the second time in three days that Cael had been told so. A one-armed man had said it of a line in a book. This man said it of him, to his face, across a lantern, with no heat in it and no fear; he might have been reading out the weight of a load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:259:It had been the hundred and thirteenth morning, a day and a half before, when Teague took a slip that he did not want.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:261:He had been at the plank before the mist was off the river. The five had gone by it in the dark, two hours earlier, and he had heard the mule. Pike was setting out his book; and there was a shingle on the oak, six along from the left-hand post, which had hung there for nine days because no crew of any standing would trouble with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:273:"You've no road south. You've forty marks of fence east, with my capitals on it, and you've a day's posts still to set."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:275:"Then it'll want its capitals a day or two longer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:279:He had told a boy at a door that his crew would be at Millrace in two days, and it had been true when he said it only because he had decided, between one sentence and the next, to make it true. He had been turning that over ever since. A man does not walk up to another crew and hand it the hair on his own neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:281:But he could be half a day from where a certain paper pointed, with a spear and a bow and a boy, on honest business. Nobody could say that it rested on nothing, since it rested on three stakes and four marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:307:They were at the weir by first light on the hundred and fourteenth, and the woman who kept the ford told him the news before he had asked for it. Five had come off the ridge the day before, at noon, with a mule; and the healer had gone up again with them, and had taken the mill's jenny, and had left word with a carter for a child at some gate to the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:311:He set two stakes with the spear and the boy, in water to the thigh, while the bow sat on the bank and sharpened the third. He did the sum as he drove them: it was an afternoon up to that place, by the ruts, and a morning to go in and bring a case out, if it was a walk and a carry, so that they would be down at the ford again by the middle of the day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:313:The middle of the day came. He stood in the mill water with a maul in his hands and looked up at the notch in the ridge, and nobody came through it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:329:They were on the ridge at dusk, on that same day, half a mile short of where the road tips over, when he saw it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:343:There were others moving. He saw them along the far edge of the low ground, against what was left of the sky: two together, going away south, and one to the west. They did not go as beaten men go; they went as people go who have practised leaving. He had seen a crew break like that only once, and he had not liked that crew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:349:On the brow, where the ruts turned, two people had lain all day, and the frost showed him the two shapes, two yards apart, each a man's length. There was no ash of any fire, and nobody had dropped a crumb there, or a thread, or the paring of a nail. Beside the northern shape he found the round print of a stirrup pressed into the turf again and again in the one place. He counted, and there were eleven prints, and a twelfth half made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:355:He sent the spear south-about by the ruts with the lantern, and the boy with him. They were gone the better part of two hours. They came back by the north, having walked the whole of the ruts, with a crossbow each and a third across the boy's shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:369:He had a rank that he had never sent west. He had earned it on a ledge in the cold, where there was no station to see it, and had let the registry go on writing the old one; it had seemed to him then that what a man had done was worth more than what was put down about him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:379:But a crew that has been shot at from four sides through the whole of a day will keep looking up for a month afterward, and Teague had seen what that does to people. If there was a thing to be fetched out of that hole, they would fetch it better without having to watch the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:381:He put the spear on the southern rim, where two had stood, and sent the boy west, since the boy had the legs for it. The bow he set on the north, which was the shortest walk, and he took the brow himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:5:Karis took her hands away from it. It was a strip no longer than her forearm, and it had come from a shelf at Ternhall, in the bottom of a box, across a Line and a mountain with a herd on it. It lay now on a carter's hide, on the lid of that same box. Its upper end rested on the ring that she had walked on the ninety-sixth day, and its edge passed through the middle of the ring that she had walked that morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:19:"By a finger, at the end of four hands of calfskin, on a map that a man made by counting his suppers." Her voice was small and quite steady, and she had not taken her eyes from the gap. "I said that I should find out how far wrong a map of days could be along one line. That is how far. I think it very handsome of him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:23:"The ruler is brass. It is the two rings under it that I answer for, and I set one where a carter bent his road and the other where a broker's paper said a day and a little aside." She laid a finger beside the third. "This one I drew round a place where he had lifted his hand. He told me, on the evening that he gave me the skin, that he had seen that corner once, from a hill, with water standing in it, and had never had a wheel within a day of it. So I have three things on this lid, each of them a little wrong, and I cannot say by how much."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"His south road lies under your edge for a day's ticks beyond this stone. After that there is a single stroke, which is how he draws a track that he would not take a cart along, and it keeps beside the edge for a day more. Then it stops, and there is nothing under the brass that a wheel made."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:33:"Then that is the order in which we shall walk it: a road, and a track, and whatever a man remembered from a hill." Karis rolled the skin. "A ruler on a map of days gives me a direction that is good to the breadth of this pencil at the length of my arm, and by the third evening that is a strip of country a mile wide. I am not going to walk a line. I am going to walk a strip, with six pairs of eyes, and look at all of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:39:"Three days, by his ticks?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:45:"I shut that shoulder last night. A mend so deep wants a week of somebody's eye on it, as her arm wanted its fortnight, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends an hour of herself on a thing and walks off in the week that it's deciding. That's the hundred and twenty-first." A finger followed the thumb. "There's an edge at the end of that brass. I said in a loft where somebody ought to stand when you cross one, and I learned yesterday what it's worth to stand there." Another finger. "And I've sent word north that I'm nineteen, or more. It's the *or more* that I'm spending."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:55:It was Brom who answered her, and he did it by going to the left-hand box for the bowls. He set out six on the turf between the two irons, as he had on every night since a grey stone on the Fallow; and the healer, who had told a girl at a mill that she would eat out of the sixth and not sit in a place, watched him do it and held her tongue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:59:They left the stone at first light on the hundred and sixteenth day, and for three days the country was kinder to them than it had any need to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:61:The frost had gone off in the night. Cael woke to a low grey sky and a smell of wet earth, which he had not smelled for three weeks, and by the middle of the forenoon the ruts were dark and soft where they had rung the day before. The carters' road came back to its line beyond the low ground, as a one-armed man had said that it would, and went on south and east under the foot of the ridge. A salt cart had been along it a week ago, and nothing since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He let it stand. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:81:On the second morning the road swung away south toward a hold whose smoke they could see, and the brass did not go with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:83:There was a track where Seln had said there would be: two ruts, grown over, with a tree as thick as Brom's leg standing in one of them. Nobody had driven it in a lifetime. It ran on along the low ground between pale grass and standing pools, and the day was so mild that gnats hung over the water in the afternoon, and a few yellow leaves were still on the silver-backed trees. Brom walked with his coat open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:93:She had the brass on the hide again as she said it. In three days he did not once hear her use the word that Brom had used; she spoke of *the third*, and of *the place*, and of what she would do with her cord when she stood at its edge, as another person might have spoken of a town that she had lived in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:99:"He saw this," she said. "From that hill to the west, I should think, with the sun behind him. He drew every stroke of it from there, and he never came a step nearer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:109:Seln had his hand flat on the breast of his coat, where a surveyor's book lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:137:"You shall, with the cord," said Karis. "Not today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:139:She had brought a stone down from the rise in both hands, grey, and as big as a loaf. She set it on the turf where Lira's toe had been, as she had set one on the Fallow and one between two bars of iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:153:"That has stood five cold terms. It is of an age with the Fallow's, and with the old one at our last stone, and I should say that the same mallet drove all three." He brushed his knee. "There is no second. Nobody has come back to this place with a newer bar and a mark under his number; or if he came, he drove nothing. I shall not choose between those."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:157:"He wrote it in a room under a floor, three days from here. I can tell you nothing more of him than that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:193:"We learned what the Fallow did to each of us before we walked a furlong into it," she said. "I should like to know what this one does before I kneel down in it with my back turned."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:197:"It is the old work. It comes up out of the mud as those stones on the Fallow came up out of their floor, with no bed under it and no join, and whatever heaves that mud has not shifted it by a hair. I think that it will carry us." He looked round the five of them. "We shall go as we went down a mile of grass once with a grey thing behind us: at one pace, in file, and nobody stops to look at anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:275:She gave it to him before she had thought, and then she stared, and so did the rest of them. He had read out a great many things to that notebook in a hundred days: the width of a road, and the age of a bar, and what a paper meant. He had asked each time that it should be entered as said. He had never once put a mark in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:283:"I have. I gave a report at a stake on the Fallow, by word of mouth, and let you carry it. It has come to me since that a man who has seen a thing ought to put his own hand to it." He gave her back the pencil. "It came up under our feet, twice, for a stone lying in the dark of its own mud, and I could find no eye in what I saw of it. I should like that to stand as mine, if it is wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:285:She wrote the day beneath it, and her initial, and did not alter a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:359:"I'll lift nothing. I'll not sit at a stone and listen, either. I did that for a day in a hole."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:381:Cael wrote the day's log with his back against the old stake, as he had written one at another stake three days' walk behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:383:*Hundred and eighteenth day. The edge of the third one, at the track's end. A marsh, and the reeds stop where the sigils stop. One bar, thirty-three, old, and no second. A hall in the middle of it, and a thing under the hall's floor that a carter told the healer nobody has seen whole.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:391:*In at first light. Karis hasn't said if since the Stair.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:5:It was the hundred and fifteenth morning. He had slept as a stone sleeps, and woken to find that the ground came to his feet when it should, and that his right arm would bend. His left palm was wound in what remained of a roll of tape. He had counted four upright shapes on the rim before he had his boots on, one at each of the places where a hood had stood the day before, and he had been on his feet with nothing in his hands before he saw whose they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:13:"Then you'll be an hour or two with your backs to the sky. I'd not care to be, after yesterday." His grey eyes came round. "We'll hold it till you're up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:25:It was the same that she had made at Thornwater, two box-ropes spliced end to end and marked off with twists of red wool; and she had it laid along the turf from the grey stone northward, with its near end under her boot. She looked as though she had not slept, and she looked very happy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:45:The spear stood on the south, a tall man, grounded, with the butt of his weapon on the frozen turf and both hands on the shaft; and he watched a girl with one arm find an edge with her foot, sixty times over, and said nothing whatever. On the west it was the boy, sitting on his heels in the ruts with a heel of bread. He stopped chewing when Lira came abreast of him, and he had not started again when they were a hundred yards on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:51:Karis went down the page with her pencil, twice. Then she turned back in the notebook to a leaf that was nineteen days old, and laid her finger on the foot of it, and looked from the one to the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:53:"It is the same," she said. "Cord for cord, and the odd strides are the same number of strides." Her voice was not quite steady. "I said two hundred, from the ridge, by my eye, and I was twenty short of it, and a stride. It is the Fallow's round, and so it is the Fallow's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:57:"To a stride. I should like somebody to tell me how that comes about by accident, five days' walk apart." She shut the book on her finger. "Nobody will. I am going down to look at a wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:67:They were going down with two lamps. Karis had filled her brass one to the neck from the can in the left-hand box, and Teague's horn lantern swung in Seln's hand at the head of the file; and by the pair of them the stair was a different place from the one that Cael had gone down at a run. The steps were the grey of the floor, cut out of it and not laid. Every edge was as sharp as on the day that it was made, since no foot had ever worn one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:77:"Since the twentieth," said Seln, without turning round. "I was counting. The air stops there. I had it yesterday in the dark, by the feel of it on my face, and took it for my own fear."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:85:She had said at the stone that she would not sit outside a second day and listen. Four people who knew what a crossbow was were holding the rim, and she meant to be where a certain shoulder was; if anything opened, it could be carried up forty steps and two hundred yards with her walking beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:87:Cael paced the room from the door to the western wall, and then across. It was ten strides each way, as he had put it the day before, and that much of his count held. The roof did not. He had called it a foot above Brom's head; and Brom put up his left hand, and his palm lay on the grey with the elbow still bent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:89:"A hand," said Brom. "I'd have had my hair in it yesterday, if I'd stood up straight. I wasn't standing up straight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:91:None of them had been. They had seen that room once, for as long as a healer needed a lamp, with a bolt in one of them; and Cael went round it now as he would have liked to then. The roof was the underside of the floor that they had crossed, without a beam or a crack, and when he held the lantern up to it he found his own light looking back at him dully, as from still water. The floor was the same grey. There was a dust on it as thin as the bloom on a plum, which had taken their boots of yesterday along the northern wall and beside the door, and nobody's anywhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:95:Karis stood in the middle of the eastern wall with her lamp held up, and for some time she did not move at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:103:The healer had stopped at her stride and a half from the nearest wall. She had been round eleven stones on the Fallow thirty times and had never gone near enough to see that they were cut, and she had sat with her back to this for a whole day in the dark. She held up her two palms to it, slowly, as she held them to a patient whom she had not yet touched; and then she let them fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:113:"I do not know that they are letters. I said so on a floor nineteen days ago, and I do not know it yet. They are lines, cut in groups, by somebody who meant something, and that is as far as I can swear." Her lamp moved along the course. "It is a great deal farther than I could swear a month ago."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:127:It was the cord that Cael's eye went along first, since it was the only thing there that was going anywhere. It ran out over the floor toward the eastern wall, pale and thin, with a knot in it at every fifth span, and it lay as straight as a ruled line for half the width of the room. There it stopped. The rest of its length lay beside it in a coil that nobody had paid out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:185:She had gone back to her wall, with Lira, and had a sheet of the thin paper spread against the lowest course. She kept her face to the stone; but Cael saw that her pencil was not moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:225:A sheet of thin paper slid down the eastern wall and folded itself on the floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:227:Karis had let go of it. She had been kneeling at the lowest course, with her left hand spread to hold the sheet and her right about to begin; and she had come up off her knees as if somebody had laid a cold hand between her shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:239:"He does not need to say where. That is the whole of what a bearing is." Karis's eyes had gone to the middle box, which Seln had carried down forty steps on his back and set against the northern wall. A calfskin was in that box, with two rings on it that she had walked, and a strip of brass. "It does not have to be written on his leaf. It is written on mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:243:"No. Not down here, by a lamp, on my knee. I shall do it once, in daylight, on a level thing, with every one of you looking." She drew a breath that shook. "Lira. I want a sheet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:255:It was there, as it was every time. Lira could not hold a sheet against a wall and rub it; that wanted two hands. But she had the roll under her right arm and the pebbles in her belt, and before the first hour was out she had learned the walls better than their reader. She had counted the courses, and had seen that a group which stood in the second course of the east wall came again in the fourth course of the south; and she was standing at the south wall with a sheet torn off and ready while Karis was still on her knees at the east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:279:She counted the grains back into it, one at a time, with all of them standing round and Brom holding his breath. Then she uncovered a figure in the corner of a leaf, which had had her thumb over it for twelve days, and looked from that to the cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:287:"Kinds. Forty-one on the Fallow, when the doubtful ones are taken out or let in as this wall bids, and forty-one in the courses here. I have been over the courses of these four walls for six hours, looking for a forty-second, and there is none in them; and there is not one of the forty-one that is wanting." She looked up. "A man with apple trees told me that I wanted to see them both at the thaw. I have seen them both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:291:There was one thing more, and she had kept it for the last of the oil. She had been over the Fallow's eleven strips for four days at a trestle in a barn, and she had every stroke of them by heart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:293:She went to the eastern wall's sheets, knelt on one knee, and put the end of her pencil on a line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:295:"This one. It's not on the Ring. I looked for it for four days and it isn't on any upright. It's here, on every wall, and it's not in the courses with the rest — it's *between* them. On its own. Here." The pencil moved along the sheet to the next one, and the next. "And here. And here. I've paced the spacing because I pace things. It comes every — " she looked at the notebook, where she had written it, and read it out as a number she had checked twice — "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, to the stride. It recurs, on the wall, at the perimeter's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:331:"It was forty steps yesterday, with a bolt in, and I counted them." He looked at the three of them, and then at the healer, who had opened her mouth and shut it again. "It's the only thing that we ever fetched for a paper and found, and I'd like to be the one that brings it up. I didn't do a great deal else down here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:335:It was full day at the top, and bright, and so warm after that room that Cael's face ached. The sun stood in the south-west over the rim, and they went up the old road in file, with Seln carrying the middle box and twenty-nine sheets rolled under Karis's arm; and on the southern rim a tall man watched them come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:341:The boy came down from the west while they were drinking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:357:*Hundred and fifteenth day. The stone at the Stair. All six up, and nothing spent. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three. A hundred and two in hand. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against a broker's mark, and that mark is never going on this paper.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:359:*Instruments recovered. The contract's paid, and the man who posted it is never going to see them, because Seln's keeping the ledger and I'm keeping the kit. Someone measured this place five years ago and wrote bearing and stopped. Karis has a straightedge. She's been waiting her whole life to use it. The Ring, the Stair, and whatever's on that bearing. Pike said a handful. We have two. The kit says three. We're going to go look.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:363:Karis had the calfskin flat on the lid of the middle box, with a pebble on each corner. There were three rings on it in pencil: one lay two days east of a square with a dot, and one lay a day south of a ring with a bar, and a little aside, and she had struck out the word that stood beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:365:She took the strip of brass out of its cloth, which had come from Ternhall, across a Line and a mountain. She had told them on the ninety-second evening that the carter's hide was a map of days, and would not take an edge; and she had not forgotten that she said it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:367:"I am going to find out how far wrong a map of days can be," said Karis, "along one line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:3:A hawk over that place at first light on the hundred and fourteenth day would have seen three rings, one inside another, and a line drawn across them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:5:The outermost was the carters' road, two pale ruts going the whole way round the low ground. Forty paces inside it lay the edge that could not be seen, where a grey stone sat on the turf between two iron bars. And in the middle, two hundred and twenty yards from the stone, was the floor, as round as a coin, with a square hole at its centre no bigger than a cart. The line across all three was the old road. It came down off the ridge from the east, over the brow where the ruts turned aside, between the two bars, and straight on down the frosted grass to the floor's edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:15:He went fourth. He felt the turf under his boots and the cold on his face, and nothing else whatever; the place behind his breastbone was as dark on the far side of that stone as on the near. The ten things that he carried lay as they had lain. He had made up his mind in the night that he would ask none of them for anything this morning unless the roof fell in, and he walked down the old road ten yards behind a small straight back with his hands empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:17:Brom came last. Cael heard him grunt at the stride, as he had grunted on the Fallow, like a man who has felt a door shut behind him. The healer's bow was on his left shoulder, spanned, with a bolt lying in its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:31:Cael looked up the old road, into the sun, and had to put his hand over his eyes. On the brow, where the ruts turned north, there was a shape standing that had not been there when Seln walked the ruts in the dark. It stood forty paces above the stone. There was a second shape beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:51:They came from the old iron, on the north side, and they went to the newer one, eight feet away, at the height of a knee and a chest and a hand above a tall man's head. He saw them arrive, and they did not creep; they swung across as a gate swings to, all three together, and caught on the newer bar, and ran on past it to the south until they were beyond his seeing. On the north side they ran away from the old iron along the turf in the same three courses, and went out of his sight in that direction too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:89:There were two on the brow to the east, where the old road came over. One had a crossbow, with its nose on the ground and his boot in the stirrup; he was drawing the string with a hook, steadily, like a man doing a thing that he had done ten thousand times. The other stood two yards south of him and had nothing in his hands at all, and his arms were coming down to his sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:91:There were two on the southern rim, a quarter of the way round. They had risen out of the white grass behind the ruts while he was looking at the brow. One of them was kneeling over a bow, and the other only stood, a little apart, with his arms folded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:93:There was one on the west, a long way off beyond the floor, so small that Cael could tell only that it was upright and that it held something across its body.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:95:And there was one on the north, kneeling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:109:He had not called them. They had risen as they had risen in a lane on the Fallow, each offering what it had. Wind was in his feet, and though it could not take him through three courses of that fence, it could take him a long way along the inside of it, faster than a hooded man could run round the ruts. Storm, the heaviest of them, lay ready; there was a rut at his feet, running straight up the hill to the brow, with seed on the grass at either lip. Ember had fire for his hand, and there was a great deal that a boy could do from where he stood, in a place where four of his friends could do nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:119:She had seen it on the Fallow from a foot away, and she saw it now from three yards. He watched her know what he was doing, and why, and what it was going to cost somebody before the day was out. Then her eyes left his face, and went up to the brow, where a man had got his string behind its catch and was reaching to his belt for a bolt. They went south, and west, and north, right round, in the time of one breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:133:She ran *down*. She went away from the stone, and from the two mules, and from the only ground on which a Path would answer, straight down the middle of the old road toward a bare grey floor with a hole in it. She ran on the whole of her foot, slapping, like a child in its father's boots; a healer had told her four days ago that a certain bone was her own again, and she was using it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:155:They were not aimed, or not as the first had been. Cael saw them only when they were falling, two dark strokes dropping steeply from the north and from the south. They had been lofted from better than two hundred and fifty yards by people who knew to a hair how far their bows would throw. One struck the plate ten feet from Brom and leapt up again, end over end, and the other came down beyond the hole and slid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:197:Karis made a sound in her throat. From the floor to the roof, on all four sides, the grey was covered with the fine sunk lines that had been round the feet of eleven stones on the Fallow: straight, and hooked, and crossing. They ran in bands, one above another, each as tall as a man's forearm. There was more of it on one wall of that room than on the whole of the other place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:221:"Why not?" said Lira. She was sitting on the lowest step with the bow across her knees and her left side turned away from the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:273:"You'll sit there. You'll not lean on it, and you'll not be brave." She wiped her fingers on her breeches. "I said in a loft that somebody ought to stand outside one of these with her sleeves up. I was outside. I'd my sleeves up. And I'm in here with a roll of tape, and that's a clean wound that I could shut in an hour on the far side of a rock." Her voice was low and quite even. "I came south for exactly this. Enter that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:287:For a breath there was no answer. Then Lira came down the six steps, slowly, and into the light; and the wool across her breast was dark and wet from the elbow to the wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:293:The healer undid her own knot and drew the wool down, and two of the eight small marks had opened, the lowest of each row, and were bleeding steadily.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:295:"The rest have held," she said, after a long look. "It's not gone through. It's opened where it was newest." She was already tearing a strip from the tail of Cael's shirt, which he had held out to her. "I can't shut that either. I can bind it, and I can tie it higher, and you can keep it against your chest until there's a sky over us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:9:It was Karis who lit it. She had come over the stone with a morning's pencil in her arms and her cheek bleeding, and she put the sheets into Lira's lap and held out her right hand; and a point stood on the kindling that Seln had laid ready before first light. It was the first thing that any of them had declared in a day, and Cael saw her look at her own palm afterward, as she had looked at it outside another stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:27:She sat down where she had knelt, on the frozen turf, with her knees drawn up and her forehead on them, and it was as long as it takes to count a hundred before she lifted her head. Nobody went to her. Cael had heard her say on a mountain that her reserve was a purse; he had watched her empty it at a mill, and at two stones, and he understood by now that what she spent on such a morning was the very thing that a child at some northern gate was waiting for, and that she knew it to the grain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:29:"Twice. It'll hold twice. It will not hold three times this season, so don't ask it to," she said. "I've mended that twice in six days. The first time it was a clean hole, and I'd a road to do it on. This time it was torn, and I did it sitting in a ditch. There isn't a third in that flesh. If it goes again before the thaw, I shall be binding it like a carter's wife, and it'll knit as a carter's does, crooked, in three months. Have you got that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:95:"They're not gone again; you'd not have walked that path. They've been leaned on, by a thing that I'd not like to put a weight to." She sat back on her heels. "I can't shut what I can't read. You've heard me say it till you're sick of it. That's ten days of knowing that you've ribs, and they'll talk when it turns cold, and they'll not do more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:97:"Ten days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:103:"I told you a night that's only a night, and I meant one with nothing in front of it. You've not had one since, and you'll not get one on this road. But I've been looking at you for a quarter of an hour, and there's another kind." She nodded at his chest. "You read it frightened, in a loft. Tonight there's nothing left in you to be frightened with; I doubt that you could be afraid of a wolf. So you'll read it tonight, when the rest are down. Then you'll sleep, and you'll read it once more when you wake, before you've had time to think. That's three, on three days, with a night between each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:131:He left her to it, since she had sat on a stair for a day once with that same bow, for the same reason, and had been right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:139:Cael had been on roads with that woman for nineteen days in all, on a mountain and a moor and a ridge, and he had never once seen it. She lay down last at every camp, a stride and a half beyond the farthest of them, with a mule between, and at Thornwater she had shut a door. She had told him on a rock that there was nobody there who had not seen her asleep, and he had taken it for a manner of speaking; she slept as a person pays a debt, privately, to have it over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:151:Not one of the four of them got up to put the blanket back, since she would have woken. They let her be, in the middle of them, with a big man's wrist in her hand, while the short day went over toward the rise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:153:He wrote the day's bill then, with his eyes still swimming a little, and he kept it to what the day had cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:155:*Hundred and nineteenth day. The track's end, outside the stone. All six out. Brom's shoulder opened on a wall and is mended a second time, an hour; it will not be mended a third. Four bolts spent; one in the bow, with Lira.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:159:*Compression: the strike, standing. The old ribs. Ten days, she says.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:171:Seln had the watch. He sat on the grey stone where Lira had sat, with his back to the fire, and whatever he heard behind him he gave no sign of hearing; the rest were four long shapes under blankets. Cael lay on his back between the embers and the old iron, and laid two fingers on the inside of his wrist and a palm on his breastbone, and let his breath go out until he had none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:183:He had been afraid of it for seventeen days. He looked for the fear as he went, and it was simply not there, having been spent that morning with a good deal else on a ledge; and he came to the crowded place under his breastbone as a tired man comes to his own gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:187:There was no sound in it, and nothing in it moved. He brought the ten to it one after another, as he had in a loft, and every one of them leaned aside and went by and closed again beyond. Those were the three things that he had counted on the night of his birthday; he counted them again, and they had not changed by a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:211:*Hundred and twentieth day, before light. The third: where it was, as it was, the same.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:249:"Under the breastbone, where they lie closest, there's a place. I could put a finger on it. Nothing in it moves, and there's no sound in it. Every one of the ten goes round it. None goes through. The tenth goes round with the rest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:287:He did, and it surprised him how little he wished to move it. Nine days ago he would have given a good deal to hear that one word; he had tried to buy it at a well with a thing half looked at, and had been told what a single morning was worth. He had it now for the full price, three readings and a night between, and it was only a word about a debt, and it left the rest exactly as heavy as she had made it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:The inn's keeper was a slow heavy man with a cloth over his shoulder, who had drawn their jugs for a hundred and twenty days and had never been asked for anything else. She told him what she wanted: a dry floor, and a door that shut, and a table that nobody ate at. It was for paper, she said. She would have it until the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:21:She laid them in three heaps along the deal, and did not try to make their edges agree; the Fallow's were long strips, and the Stair's were leaves, and the hall's were whatever a person could tear from a roll with her teeth. There were eleven in the first heap. There were twenty-nine in the second, and twelve in the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:25:"Fifty-two sheets. Forty-one kinds, at the two that I could do properly. Three perimeters of one size, and a fourth that is twice it." Karis leaned on the deal with both palms. "I had three boxes of other people's stone when I crossed the Line. I should not have said that there was anything east of it to put in them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:31:*Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:37:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:47:The last ferry crossed at noon on the hundred and thirtieth day, with two fingers of snow on its rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:49:The ferryman had been at the shelf since first light with a pole and an axe, to cut her a lane. Half the meadow stood on the landing to see it. The scow went out along its cable slowly, shouldering the broken ice, with the gelding walking his round in the shed and the boy calling the turns. On the far bank a man in a hat with one side cut away stood waiting beside a mule, as he had stood at a ford on the fourth day past the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:55:"For the smith; from his brother, by the hand. For the smith." A folded sheet went along the oak. "For the house on the rise, a bill; they'll not want it said twice, and I'm saying it twice. For the landing: two, under his own seal, and I've not looked at them. For the landing, two." The broker's clerk took those. He did not meet anybody's eye. "For the woman with the hides, from a tanner. For the hold at the top of the north water. For Teague's old woman, from her daughter, and it's the first that ever came for her by this boat. For Thornwater, to go up tomorrow with the carter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:71:An old man in Denvash had cut that leaf from his pad a long while ago, and had rolled a ruler in it for a boy who was going where there were no instruments. It had lain in a bag on the wrong side of a river for a month and more. And in that time the boy had walked off the end of another person's brass for three days and come to a marsh, and had stood at a fence of iron with a line going through it. He had not written one word of that west. The old man could not have known that there was a line. He had only known the boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:99:It had been the hundred and twenty-second morning, eight days before, when Oryn left five people standing at a heap of chock-stones and took the left-hand road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:103:The jenny went as she had always gone, which was badly. She led as a gate swings. For the whole of the first morning Oryn's right hand kept going out to a small brown cheek that was two days behind her on another road; it met a grey ear like an oar, and came back. She let it. She had told a girl in a yard that a beast should not be named, and had learned at a wicket how little that helped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:107:She had been a stop for nine days. She said it over without softening it. There had been a bench at the mill that she left a day short. There were two gates beyond the mill that had not seen her at all. There was the ford with the oxen, where they had looked for her on the hundred and fourth. She had said seventeen to a loft, and nineteen to a carter, and *or more* to a stone, and it would be the hundred and twenty-fifth before she so much as came down out of these hills. That was three weeks, to the day. Beyond the ford was the gate that had given her the coat, and beyond that the child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:111:Then she let herself go over the other, since she had four days and nobody to see her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:113:She began with what her hands had, as she had been taught. They were full again. She had mended nobody for three days, and slept in a ruined hold with her back to a wall, and she could feel the purse of her reserve sitting heavy and whole, as it had not sat since a mill. They had held a shoulder twice, and it was mended, and bound by a woman who knew more about holding than she did; she had watched a drover's turn laid three times and could lay it now herself. They had held an arm with eight marks on it, all taken. They had been on a boy's wrist for a count of ten and had got what they always got.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:131:The gap took the four days that a carter's skin had given it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:133:It was a high bare way between two hills, with nothing on it but frost and a few sheep that belonged to nobody, and she slept each night with her back against the jenny's belly and her feet in her pannier. On the fourth afternoon the ground began to fall. There was a valley under her with a pale road in it, going east and west, and she knew that road; she had gone round a ring of stones by it, thirty times, with a mule.

