editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:3:It had been the afternoon of the hundred and nineteenth day when Karis sat down at the far fire with three clean leaves before her, and by the dusk of the hundred and twentieth she had risen from them only to sleep and to pace a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:7:She had never done such a thing in her life. A leaf was written once, and dated, and laid in its place, and the place was entered; that was what a box was for. But there were three boxes, and she had seen what a mountain could do to a cart. On the seventy-eighth day everything that the five of them owned had gone into a gorge except those three, and they had come off the hill only because a very large person went back for two of them, and a boy broke three ribs over the third. She had counted the loss that night, when she could count again. It had been blankets, and a kettle, and a rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:9:It would not be blankets now. Until the Fallow there had been nothing in those boxes that did not also stand on a shelf somewhere to the west, where she was no longer let in. Since the Fallow there had been a good deal, and since this morning there was a thing that existed nowhere in the world but on the paper in front of her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:13:She wrote small. At the head she set the three places by the names that carters gave them, since she had no better: the Fallow Ring, and the Long Stair, and the hall. Under each went its perimeter, which was sixty-two cords and twenty strides, three times over; and its radius, ten cords and a piece; and the number on its stake. Thirty-one. Thirty-two, on two bars. Thirty-three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:15:Then the walls, and here she was careful, since she had been greedy that morning and knew it. She had eleven sheets from the Fallow, where the bands ran round the feet of eleven stones, and twenty-nine from a room under the Stair. She had twelve from the hall. That made fifty-two, and the last twelve were all from one wall, rubbed upside down in an hour, by a person who was being held by the belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:21:She had seen it. It had been a hand below her chin for a quarter of an hour, on a wall above a spring: the line that stood by itself, with its small divisions, as it stood between the courses under the Stair. She had counted those divisions there against her short cord, twice, and they had given her the radius of the circle. Here she had had a pencil in one hand and her own weight on the other, and no cord at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:43:She copied the figure. She copied it into the second leaf, and the third. He turned the book a little toward the light for each, and did not once offer to put it into her hand; it stayed in his, with two of his fingers closed on its spine, as it had stayed there for five days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:45:She understood that well enough. She had lived for years beside shelves that she was allowed to read at and forbidden to carry from. And he had been the keeper of one shut thing for twelve years; it was not to be supposed that he would take up a second and hold it open to everybody. He had come twenty yards across the turf with it, unasked, which was a great deal farther than she had expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:53:"A cord knotted at the fives. It is in Brom's case." Seln shut the book. "I can tell you no more of him. He writes a small upright hand, and presses. I have been through a page and a half of it every night since that room, and I could not tell you whether he was tall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:59:She unrolled it on the lid of the box and set a pebble on each corner. There were three rings on it still, and one of them was a lie: she had drawn it on the ninety-second evening round a place where a carter lifted his hand, and since noon she had known to a stride where it ought to have been. She took the crumb of bread that she kept for such work and rubbed the old ring out. She drew the new one a finger nearer to the corner, where a cord and a girl's left foot had put it, and wrote the day beside it, and *moved; paced.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:61:Then the middles. She had stood on all three. One was the centre of a white floor with eleven stones about it, and one was a square hole with a stair in it, and one was a spring. She made a dot in each ring with the point of her pencil, as small as she could make it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:67:She did not trust that, and she set about breaking it. A straight thing will lie through any two marks on a skin, and so two of those dots proved nothing whatever; the whole question was the third. She moved the brass. She laid it through the Fallow's dot and the hall's, and looked at the Stair's: it sat under the edge. She laid it through the Stair's and the hall's, and the Fallow's sat under the edge. She took her pencil and made each dot again a pencil's breadth to one side, which was as far out as she believed that she could be on a map of days, and laid the brass a fourth time, and a fifth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:69:It would not miss. She could make it lie a hair to this side of a dot, or a hair to that; she could not make daylight show between the brass and any of the three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:71:And she had not chosen the third. That was the thing which she kept returning to, as a tongue goes back to a tooth. She had laid that edge on two rings at a grey stone, in daylight, with five people looking, before any one of them had seen a reed. It had run off into a corner of the skin where a man once lifted his hand. They had walked along it for three days, in a strip a mile wide, and had come over a rise onto a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:It was entered. She sat and looked at what lay above it. There was a perimeter, three times, to the stride. There were forty-one kinds, and none strange. There was a line on a wall that was divided to a circle's radius, and stakes that were numbered in a row. And now there was a ruler lying through the three of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:81:She had laid leaves side by side on a trestle once, before it was light, and had known what they said when they lay so. There had been three of them. It had been a smaller thing that morning, and she had been able to put a clean leaf back into a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:91:She laid it out for them on the hundred and twenty-first morning, in daylight, on the lid of the middle box, with all of them looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:97:She showed them the three dots. Then she laid the brass down, and took her hands away from it, as she had at a grey stone six days before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:119:He went over it as he went over anything. There was meal for six days in the left-hand box, and a little bacon. There was a shoulder that was bound and would not be mended a third time, and an arm in a sling that was to hold nothing till the snow. The frost had come back in the night, harder than they had yet had it, and a healer had said at a stone that the snow would not be long. And there was a line. He had been walking toward something for a hundred and twenty days without knowing that it had a direction, and it had one now, and it went off the edge of a calfskin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:121:"Three days out along it," he said. "No farther. If there's nothing under the brass by the third dusk, we turn for Lowmarch, and I'll say so then without anybody having to ask me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:129:"I've a fence at forty with a day's posts to set," he said, "and a one-armed man to tell what I saw. I'll tell him what I saw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:135:Cael went and stood a stride and a half from her. He had come to her at a mill, eight days before, with this same errand, and he remembered how he had put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:145:"See that you do." She looked past him, at the hide. "There's a road a mile east of this water; I saw it from the rise. That quiet one says that it lies under your brass for a day, and that it forks. The left hand goes up through a gap, north and west, to Thornwater, in four of that carter's ticks." She took up the rope. "So I walk your road for a day, since it's mine as well. Where it forks, I'm the route."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:149:Cael was the last to leave the grey stone. He looked down the ruts to the reeds, and along the pale straight path, to four walls standing in water with the morning in them. A thing lay under those walls with four bolts in it. It would go its round again, he supposed, once in a quarter of an hour, when it was able; and no carter would ever know that anybody had been in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:155:They struck the road an hour from the marsh, and it rang under them. Every rut had a spine of ice in it. The pools on either hand had shut in the night, and the pale grass stood stiff and white to the knee, and their breath went up from eight mouths, counting the mules', and hung behind them along the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:163:It was what six people sounded like on a hard road. He knew the sound as he knew his own breathing, and he knew that he would learn it better tomorrow, when it was shorter by a pair of boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:165:They met one cart all that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:177:They came at dusk to a third hold, and it had been dead for years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:203:"It's been entered since the day you said it. There's a leaf with a session's number on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:213:"Five bowls tomorrow," she said, presently.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:The inn's keeper was a slow heavy man with a cloth over his shoulder, who had drawn their jugs for a hundred and twenty days and had never been asked for anything else. She told him what she wanted: a dry floor, and a door that shut, and a table that nobody ate at. It was for paper, she said. She would have it until the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:15:It was a narrow room with one small window, and it smelled of barley. There was a table in it of scrubbed deal. The keeper moved his meal-sacks to one end, and by the time the hearth had been banked for the night she had made the other end her own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:25:"Fifty-two sheets. Forty-one kinds, at the two that I could do properly. Three perimeters of one size, and a fourth that is twice it." Karis leaned on the deal with both palms. "I had three boxes of other people's stone when I crossed the Line. I should not have said that there was anything east of it to put in them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:29:Cael did not go to the hearth with the others. He sat down at the meal-sack end of that table, with Hesk's book, while she worked; and he did the thing that he did at the start of anything, which he had last done on the night that he was nineteen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:31:*Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:37:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:47:The last ferry crossed at noon on the hundred and thirtieth day, with two fingers of snow on its rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:49:The ferryman had been at the shelf since first light with a pole and an axe, to cut her a lane. Half the meadow stood on the landing to see it. The scow went out along its cable slowly, shouldering the broken ice, with the gelding walking his round in the shed and the boy calling the turns. On the far bank a man in a hat with one side cut away stood waiting beside a mule, as he had stood at a ford on the fourth day past the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:55:"For the smith; from his brother, by the hand. For the smith." A folded sheet went along the oak. "For the house on the rise, a bill; they'll not want it said twice, and I'm saying it twice. For the landing: two, under his own seal, and I've not looked at them. For the landing, two." The broker's clerk took those. He did not meet anybody's eye. "For the woman with the hides, from a tanner. For the hold at the top of the north water. For Teague's old woman, from her daughter, and it's the first that ever came for her by this boat. For Thornwater, to go up tomorrow with the carter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:71:An old man in Denvash had cut that leaf from his pad a long while ago, and had rolled a ruler in it for a boy who was going where there were no instruments. It had lain in a bag on the wrong side of a river for a month and more. And in that time the boy had walked off the end of another person's brass for three days and come to a marsh, and had stood at a fence of iron with a line going through it. He had not written one word of that west. The old man could not have known that there was a line. He had only known the boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:75:"Tonight. I've two letters to write, and that boat's not going to wait for me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:83:*Vell. There's a second ledger on the continent with my name in it, kept by a one-armed man in a town on no map, and this week he stopped numbering me. He said his book was wrong about us by being too small. You said once that somebody should have a record that doesn't answer to the Compact. There are two now. The session still stands. — C.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:99:It had been the hundred and twenty-second morning, eight days before, when Oryn left five people standing at a heap of chock-stones and took the left-hand road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:103:The jenny went as she had always gone, which was badly. She led as a gate swings. For the whole of the first morning Oryn's right hand kept going out to a small brown cheek that was two days behind her on another road; it met a grey ear like an oar, and came back. She let it. She had told a girl in a yard that a beast should not be named, and had learned at a wicket how little that helped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:105:She did her sum as she climbed, since she did it every morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:107:She had been a stop for nine days. She said it over without softening it. There had been a bench at the mill that she left a day short. There were two gates beyond the mill that had not seen her at all. There was the ford with the oxen, where they had looked for her on the hundred and fourth. She had said seventeen to a loft, and nineteen to a carter, and *or more* to a stone, and it would be the hundred and twenty-fifth before she so much as came down out of these hills. That was three weeks, to the day. Beyond the ford was the gate that had given her the coat, and beyond that the child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:111:Then she let herself go over the other, since she had four days and nobody to see her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:113:She began with what her hands had, as she had been taught. They were full again. She had mended nobody for three days, and slept in a ruined hold with her back to a wall, and she could feel the purse of her reserve sitting heavy and whole, as it had not sat since a mill. They had held a shoulder twice, and it was mended, and bound by a woman who knew more about holding than she did; she had watched a drover's turn laid three times and could lay it now herself. They had held an arm with eight marks on it, all taken. They had been on a boy's wrist for a count of ten and had got what they always got.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:119:She had said the only true thing that she had. In eleven years she had put her hands into more bodies than she could number, the hurt and the whole and the dying, and every one of them had been all current, fast or slow, standing behind a break or running free. She had never met a still thing in a living person. The dying went still all at once, and everywhere. This was neither of those.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:125:*So are you.* It had come out of her crooked, and she had heard it come. She went over it now, strictly, with her knuckles ready. He was on the route. That was the whole of what the words said, and they were true of a miller's man and an old woman on a hurdle. She had gone into a dish of water after a shoulder, since she was nearest. He had gone into a hole after her. Neither of those had anything to say to a morning on a plank at Thornwater, when a boy lay still and let her spend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:131:The gap took the four days that a carter's skin had given it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:133:It was a high bare way between two hills, with nothing on it but frost and a few sheep that belonged to nobody, and she slept each night with her back against the jenny's belly and her feet in her pannier. On the fourth afternoon the ground began to fall. There was a valley under her with a pale road in it, going east and west, and she knew that road; she had gone round a ring of stones by it, thirty times, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:135:She came down to Thornwater's eastern gate at dusk on the hundred and twenty-fifth day. The bar was drawn for her before she had called.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:151:There were seven of them under the back room's window in the morning, in the frost, and the first was sitting bolt upright on the bench's end with a stick across her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:153:She was a very small old woman. Oryn had last seen her going away up a valley on a hurdle, between two grandsons, thirteen days before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:155:"You were to rest that a fortnight," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:157:"I rested it ten days. Then a carter came by my door and told me that you'd gone off your round after a crew, south, onto bad ground. So I thought that if you weren't coming to my doorstep, I'd find out whether the knee would come to you." The old woman rapped it with her knuckles. "It's a day and a half over the low place. I did it in two. I'd one of the boys with me to carry the bread."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:171:The word came on the second day, with the last of the grain carts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:173:It was a long stooped carter whom she knew, fetching for the gates up the water, and he had been as far as the ford with the oxen and a gate beyond. He gave it to her in the yard, as he had been given it, in the order of the road. The mill had had nothing worse than a crushed thumb. The two gates above it had buried nobody. At the ford a man had lost a finger to a chain and kept the hand; they had bound it themselves, badly, and it had not gone bad. And at the gate where the little one was, the breathing was no different. She coughed at night, and slept after.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:177:Three weeks, then, and seven gates, and nobody dead of it. She did not take that for a reason. A person who has crossed thin ice once has not learned that ice will bear; she has been lucky, and knows it by the cold in her stomach afterward. But the round was whole. Every gate on it was where she had left it, with everybody in it that she had left there, and waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:179:She owed that bench two days more. Then she would go north by the wicket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:183:It was the steading's woman who told her about the carter, on the evening of the hundred and twenty-eighth, with the sky gone the colour of a slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:185:"He goes down to the board at first light, with my bark. He'll not come up again this side of the thaw, if that does what it looks like." The woman jerked her chin at the north. "If you've anything to send to that crew of yours, it's tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:193:She did not keep a book. She had said so to the pale one across a fire: a healer who needs a page to know who is owed has stopped looking at them. She had never in thirty rounds set down a word about any person whom she had had her hands in. But those five were two days south, with a saddle between that would be shut by morning, and she could not look at them. There was a girl down there with an arm in a knot of hers, who had been told *first snow*; and by the feel of that sky she would be looking at it out of a window tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:205:And there was what a round of three weeks had cost, which was the sum that all five of them would have been doing since a fork. It was not a kindness to tell them. It was a finding, and she gave people their findings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:209:She went over what she was leaving out, as she would have gone over a bench at the end of a day, to be sure that nobody had been missed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:211:There would be nothing in it about a plank. That stood where she had put it, and a strip of bark was no place to move it from. There would be nothing about a still thing under a boy's breastbone; she had told him to leave it alone, and she would not be the one who put a finger on it from two days off. She would not ask after a mule. And she would not tell five people that she had been glad of their bowl, since she had eaten out of it standing, on purpose, for nine days, and they had every one of them seen her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:213:She dipped the pen. She wrote slowly, in a hand that had not been used since a healing house's morning list, and she did not write one word that she would not have said aloud at a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:217:*Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:221:The carter came over the saddle on the hundred and thirty-first morning, with a hand's depth of snow on the road and his eyebrows white, and he had a strip of bark for Pike and another that was not for Pike at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:227:It was a hand that Cael had never seen. It was square, and stiff, and it pressed, like a person lifting something heavy. He read the four lines out, as they stood, a stop at a time: the route, and a knee, and what three weeks had cost; and then the thaw, and a map, and what he was to do when he was told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:245:He had the strip from Denvash in his coat. It was cold from the back room, and it took the firelight all along one edge. He set it down on the skin beyond hers, with its end on the ring by the marsh, and slid it up until the two lay in one length; and he bent, as she had bent beside a fire in the dark, and looked for daylight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:3:They came to the fork two hours after sunrise on the hundred and twenty-second day, and the healer stopped the grey mule in the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:27:"It rode on my mule for thirty rounds in a cover, and I looked at it at noon one day, at a stone, and left it where it was. It's done more across your knee than it ever did for me." Oryn did not put out a hand. "There's one in it. See that it stays in till it's wanted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:31:[[TIER-B: Oryn at the fork, "I'm three weeks late there…" — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:33:"I've been a stop on my own route for nine days. I'm going back to being the route."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:35:Nobody answered her. She had told them at a mill what she would do on the day that the round could not spare her, and what they were to say to it. Cael had written down, where he wrote such things, that he would not ask her to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:49:She shifted the rope on her shoulder. She was looking up at the notch, and her face was as tired and as level as he had ever seen it; and whatever had given under her at a marsh's edge, three days before, had been put back where she kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:59:There were four of them behind him, and one mule. They stood at the heap of stones as she had left them: a small man with a case on his chest, and a small woman with a hide under her arm, and a very big one bound up like a parcel, and a girl with a bow on her back and one sleeve empty. It was the crew that had come down off a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second day past the Line, and it looked a good deal the worse for wear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:63:*Hundred and twenty-second day, the fork. Oryn north. On her terms. I didn't ask. Five on the bearing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:73:They went up, with Brom on the low side of the mule and three boxes swaying. It was a great whale's back of pale grass, a mile broad on its crown, with the country falling off on either hand. In every fold of its flanks a line of white lay, where the frost had not lifted for a week and would not now lift before the thaw. From above they looked like chalk strokes ruled on a slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:91:"Good." She put the great boot down whole, and the next. "She'd have answered you, and then she'd have had to carry having answered for four days through a gap. I thought that you'd know that. I wasn't sure of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:93:They slept on the ridge in a hollow, without wood, in every blanket that they had. Brom laid six bowls on the turf. He looked at the row, and took one back; and it passed without remark, since he had done exactly that on a hundred nights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:95:It was that night that the ribs began to talk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:97:She had told him that they would. The cold got into the three old breaks some time after the stars came out, and stayed there, like a thumb pressed on a bruise. He could breathe, and he could walk, and he could not find a way to lie. He counted it: this was the fourth night of her ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:99:The second day on the ridge was the clearest that he had seen past the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:101:By noon he could see for thirty miles. There was no road in any of it, and no smoke, and not a fence or a roof; there were hills going away blue behind hills, with the white lying in their folds, and a sky over them so large that he could not look at the whole of it at once. Four people were strung out along the turf ahead of him, small and dark. A man who knew every road out of a town was walking toward a place where there was none. A girl who had been ranked every week of her life was a mile from the nearest thing that could have measured her, with a bow on her back that she could shoot and could not bend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:111:It was true, and he let it stand; and the other thing was in the same coat with it, and had been all morning. They were walking along a ruler. Somebody had set three floors in a row, as a person sets stones along a path; and when he looked ahead down the ridge, to where the strip went on into the blue, he was more afraid of what was under the brass than he had been of a thing in a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:117:Seln was standing on the crest when they came up to him at dusk, and he did not say the word that he had said seven times the day before and nine times since morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:127:Cael could not have said what. It was grey, and upright, and it was taller than a man; how much taller he had no means of telling, with nothing beside it to measure by and the better part of half a mile of dusk between. It was not a ring of stones. It was not a floor with a hole in it, or four walls. It had a top that was not level. That was all that the light would give him, and when he tried to hold more of it, the dusk kept the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:139:Below the ruts, a furlong down the slope, there was something else going round. It was a line of small dark uprights, set close, running away on both hands with the curve of the ground until the dusk had it. From the crest it looked like a row of stitches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:151:At a hundred and ninety, or within a pace or two of it, her left boot came down beside one of the dark uprights, and she stopped as she had stopped at the reeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:155:"A hundred and ninety," said Karis, at the ruts. "Not forty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:169:These did not stand alone. Anchor had known it before his eyes did; it had asked at two bars with an old road between them, ten days ago, and here it asked at every pace, in its thin held note, as far as he could hear. There was a bar at his right knee, a hand's length clear of the turf, and another a pace beyond it, and another a pace beyond that. They went on so in both directions, one to a pace, round the head of that valley, for as far as he could see iron against the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:195:Her voice was quite steady. She opened her notebook against the lamp, and did the sum in it, with her lips moving. She had said two hundred of the Stair, by her eye, and had been twenty short and a stride; and he saw the figure that she wrote now, which was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from a line that she had not crossed to a middle that she had not seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:203:"There was a sheet with two," he said, after a breath. "I told you that I could not place it. A marsh three days behind us, and this: I can place it now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:209:It was the question, and all five of them knew it. A line half this size had put out five sigils at a stride and given them back at a stride, the same, with nothing lost. Would one of twice the size give them back? There was no cord that could tell her that. Somebody would have to walk over that iron with a Path in her and come out again; and the one person who could have stood at the fence with her sleeves up was two days gone through a gap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:215:"There are forty bars to the left of me that I have counted, and a great many more that I have not," said Seln. "I should want to walk every one of them by daylight before I advised anybody to stand inside them. I do not think that we have the bread."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:3:They turned for home on the hundred and twenty-fourth morning, and it took them six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:They did not go back as they had come. Seln had read the hide by Karis's lamp on the crest, and had found them a shorter way than a marsh and a stair: along the ridge to the heap of chock-stones, and then north by the hide carters' road, which ran through shut holds toward a river that all of them knew. He walked it ahead of them, as he walked everything. Four times in a day he came back along the file and said his word, and after the second day Karis left off writing down the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:7:It grew colder with every morning. On the first the sky was clear, and the ruts rang. On the third there was a ring round the moon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:13:"Three days. Four, if we're lucky, and we've not been."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:23:He could not lie on them. He slept sitting, against a box or a wall, with his knees drawn up. He woke at every turn of the watch with the three old breaks as plain to him as three fingers laid on his side. They were no worse on the road. They only spoke, exactly as a healer had said that they would, and he counted her ten days off against them each night as a man counts off a sentence: the sixth on the ridge, and the seventh at the stones, and the eighth under the fence of a hold that would not open its gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:25:Lira walked in front of him, and he watched her do it for six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:27:The foot was taped. A roll had been put into her hand at a fork, without a word, by a person who had then walked north. She wound it each morning herself, sitting on a box, with her one hand and her teeth, as she had been shown on a mountain. Then she set the great boot down whole, heel and toe together, at every stride of every mile, on ruts that were iron and on turf that was glass. She rolled nowhere and pushed at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:29:It went from under her on the fourth day, on a sheet of ice where a spring had crossed the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:47:Nobody at that fire said what *it* was. Cael noticed that none of them had, in six days. They spoke of the foot, and the boot, and the tape, and of what she had been told. A healer had refused that joint once, on arithmetic, and a girl had made up her mind that she would not ask for it a second time; and its name went all the way from a fence of iron to a river without being spoken by anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:49:He woke on the hundred and twenty-ninth morning lying on his left side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:51:He had rolled onto it in his sleep, and it had let him. He lay and breathed, slowly, to the bottom of his chest, and nothing answered. It was ten days to the morning since a woman wet to the waist had pressed her thumb in two places and told him to count it himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:55:On the hundred and twenty-fourth night, the first of the road home, Seln read a page and a half that he had by heart already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:57:He read it on his watch, as he had on every night since a room under a floor. He sat with his back to four sleepers and the limp little book open on his knee, tilted to what the embers gave. He did not need the light. He used it regardless, since there is a difference between remembering a hand and looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:65:This was the nearest that he had come. It was a person's own book. In twelve years of that matter he had handled a great deal of paper. Every leaf of it had been written by a clerk, at dictation, for somebody up a stair. Nobody had dictated this. A man had gone alone into a hole in the ground with a cord and a mallet, and had put down what he found there, for himself and for no reader, in the order in which he found it, and had been astonished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:69:Seln knew that counter. He had stood at one like it in his first posting, and had been given a satchel and four keys and a sheet to put his name to. The storeman had not looked up. *Returned or accounted for,* the sheet said, above the place for the name. He had thought nothing of it in his first year. It had not come into his head that there was a third thing which could happen to a man and his issue, and that the sheet had no line for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:73:He had sat in that service for fifteen years. He knew what it did with its instruments when they were done with, and he had supposed that he knew the worst of it. He had not known that it would write *abandoned* on a sheet of cream paper, about a kit that was still lying where its owner knelt, and offer three hundred marks for it as a hook to hang five people on. The man with the upright hand had been a clerk of a kind. He had written true lines at somebody's bidding and had not been told what they were for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:75:He had written *nothing to report* at the foot of a weekly return for twelve years, over the top of a case, and had thought it the hardest sentence in the service to write honestly. This man had written a better one. He had written what was there. He had put down eight figures that he did not understand, because they were so, and had underlined the one that frightened him; and Seln, who had read some thousands of returns, could not remember another that had been made by a person so little concerned with what his reader wished to hear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:79:It lay where it had lain for nine days, on his breastbone, beside the other thing; and he sat for some time with his hand over the pair of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:83:It was on the fourth day of that road, with the wind gone into the north, that he saw what he had been doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:85:He was walking a furlong ahead of the others, and he put his hand up to his coat as he did a dozen times in a day; and for once he noticed that the hand had two things to find. There was a case, locked, which he had carried shut through twelve years and three postings and across a Line. And there was a book, which he had carried shut for twelve days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:89:He had seen people stand so before him for fifteen years, on the other side of a counter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:91:He went over it as he would have gone over a return. The case was shut for cause. There were names in it, and what was done to their owners; and there was a leaf in it that was no copy. It would be opened on a day, before the right people, or it would burn those whom it was kept for. He did not doubt that. He had audited it on a great many watches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:95:He had spent fifteen years learning to keep things from being read. He could think of no worse shelf for a dead man's true figures than the coat of such a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:121:He took the hide from under her arm, so that she should have both hands, and carried it for her until dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:123:His own hand went to his coat that evening, on watch, and found a strap, and the corner of a lock, and nothing beside them. He let it lie there. He had thought that he might want the thing back by nightfall, as a man wants a tooth that has been drawn. He left it where he had put it, that night and the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:141:Above the landing there was a long low building with a deep eave. The plank under the eave was bare of its book. There was the smith's chimney, and the taller house on the rise, with two. Along the water lay the meadow, where forty fires had burned in the dry months and twelve when he went south. He counted three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:153:"Nor have I. Sixty yards, four-strand." He knocked a last cake of ice into the river. "I'll not want it this side of the thaw. There's one more crossing in her, tomorrow, if that shelf lets me; the post comes down to the far bank for it. Then I take my lines in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:189:"It's first snow," said Pike. "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw. I've done forty today. This is the last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:203:He took it by its corner and swung it on the bench, so that the writing faced the other way. Cael had read every line of that book upside down for a hundred and twenty days, across an oak plank, as he had once read a bailiff's list on a counter. It lay the right way up before him now. He read the season in a square black hand, and at the end of it a ruled space with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:205:"Teague says you need a column I don't have. I've kept this ledger thirty years. First time it's been wrong about a crew by being too small."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:211:"I have. I'd put a figure there for any crew on that cloth, and be within a line of right. I'll not put one where I know that it's wrong." He looked at the space. "It can stand till I've a column. I told the girl so, the night that you went south."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:215:Cael stood before a hearth with snow melting on his shoulders. A magister had written of him once that no instrument would see his like. He had meant it kindly, and had entered it in a daybook that Cael was not let to carry away. This man had turned his own book about on a bench, in front of fifty people, to show a boy the hole in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:217:By the fire one of the two carters had tipped his head back, to look at the sailcloth over Pike's bench. Five names stood there on the eleventh line, as they had for seventeen days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:229:It was not an offer, and it was not a question. He had seen a strip of brass run off the edge of a calfskin, and had asked nothing about it, and had walked five days home to say this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:241:"Fence. Forty. Teague says it's yours if you want it; he's got the cold road," he said, to the fire, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "It's the long one, east, with the axe-head on its gate. He'd a day's posts still to set when he went off after you, and there's the whole far side to mend before the thaw. A hold will feed a crew that's mending its fence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:243:All four of them looked at Brom. He had asked a one-armed man once what a board paid somebody with no Path at all, and had carried the answer against his skin for seventy days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:7:It was done as it had been done at the Stair, with one difference, which was that nobody needed a sigil to find the way. Lira went along the reeds with the cord's end shut in her right fist and her boot half over the line, saying *gone* and *back* under her breath, and at every station her toe was within a hand of the first stem. Seln cut the turfs, which came up black and dripping. Cael wrote. His eyes had come back to him in the night, and he set down sixty-two stations in a better hand than the last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:9:They were the whole of the forenoon about that perimeter, with the marsh on their right and the frost going grey on the turf. Not once in that round did the water stand a yard inside the line, or a reed a yard beyond it. A spring had filled that basin for longer than anybody could say, and it had filled it to a line that five people could feel in their heads and no farther. Cael could not have said what water knew about such an edge, and not one of them tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:15:"The same perimeter, cord for cord and stride for stride." She had not underlined it. "I was glad of it twice. A third time it is merely so, and I should have been a good deal more interested if it had come out otherwise."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:41:"A bolt, six days gone. I drew it and shut it on a road. Then he came off a wall yesterday and hung by it, and I shut it again, in that ditch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:53:"It's what I've been telling it since yesterday," said Oryn. "I've no linen left that isn't on him already. Have you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:71:It had been the hundred and seventeenth morning, three days before, when Teague took his crew up the notch from the mill with one more in it than had come down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:73:He had not sent for her. She had come of her own motion, as she did every year. She went up the north water when the roads dried, to her daughter's hold, and set whatever that valley broke until the first hard frost. Then she walked down to Lowmarch, and asked a one-armed man where the crew was. This year he had told her Millrace. She had ridden down on the mill's cart, on the sacks, and had been sitting on the flat stone by the ford when Teague came off the ridge with a stake still to set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:77:That was his business with that crew done, by any reckoning that he had ever used, and he sat on the bank at dusk with his boots off and found that it was not done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:79:He went over it as he went over a load. Six people had lain round that low ground for a day with their faces covered, and had gone off it north and south and west when a boy stood up with a light. They had gone as people go who have practised leaving. He had read where they lay, by lantern, and had found nothing above the height of a boot. People who take such pains over what they leave behind are not people who go home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:99:"There's a man with them who had a bolt through his shoulder," Teague told her. "Their healer shut it that night, with her hands, on the grass. She's a good one; I've seen her work. He walked off next day with a case on his other side."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:103:"Three days, by now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:115:He walked behind it for three days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:117:It kept a road for the first of them, and a grown-over track for the second, and he read the six as he went. The big one was carrying on his left side. The girl was setting her right foot down whole, at every stride, and had been told to. The small one stopped at each halting-place and knelt, and laid something on her knee; and the quiet one, whom he had needed two looks to find in an inn, had been out in front of the rest the whole way, alone. On the third morning the track gave out, and the trail did not so much as waver. That was what he could not get past. People go to water, or to a roof, or along whatever road there is. These had gone over wet grass with no mark to steer by, round nothing, toward nothing that he could see, as a plumb line hangs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:121:There was no print over theirs but his own crew's. He had gone back along it himself at each dusk, a mile, to be sure. He had lain on his face on a knoll for an hour on the second night, with the cold coming up through his coat, and watched the way that they had come. Nothing moved on it. Nobody was behind them, and nobody had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:127:"Then we've walked three days after a crew that's in no trouble."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:129:"We've walked three days and found that out. I'd call it cheap."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:131:It was the middle of the hundred and twentieth day when the ground fell away in front of him, and he saw reeds, and grey walls standing in water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:133:He had never been within two days of that place. He knew what it was. Every salt man on the cold road had a tale of it, and not one of the tales had a man in it who went down to the reeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:147:It was dusk before he went himself. The boy was sitting alone at the near fire, against a scaled stake, with a book shut on his knee. Teague had stood over a great many men in his time, and it had got him the answers that men give to somebody who is standing over them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:165:The tenth did not stir, since it never asked for anything. But he knew what was within his reach, as a man with a key in his pocket knows a lock when he passes one. It would take two fingers, and a count of ten. At three he would have this man's banks under his hand, and at ten he would know the Path as he knew a friend's step. In a minute more he would have what ran in it, and how deep. That was the Bronze which a one-armed man had guessed at under his eave, and had never been told. A registrar would have given a year's pay for what lay a foot from his fingers. He could have had the whole of it and told nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:199:He said it to the dusk, as he had once told it to a lantern, and went up the rise to his three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:203:*Teague sat down next to me. Asked what I am. I could have read him. I asked instead. The day I stop asking is the day I'm the registry. Write it down. Again. Every time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:9:It was Karis who lit it. She had come over the stone with a morning's pencil in her arms and her cheek bleeding, and she put the sheets into Lira's lap and held out her right hand; and a point stood on the kindling that Seln had laid ready before first light. It was the first thing that any of them had declared in a day, and Cael saw her look at her own palm afterward, as she had looked at it outside another stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:27:She sat down where she had knelt, on the frozen turf, with her knees drawn up and her forehead on them, and it was as long as it takes to count a hundred before she lifted her head. Nobody went to her. Cael had heard her say on a mountain that her reserve was a purse; he had watched her empty it at a mill, and at two stones, and he understood by now that what she spent on such a morning was the very thing that a child at some northern gate was waiting for, and that she knew it to the grain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:29:"Twice. It'll hold twice. It will not hold three times this season, so don't ask it to," she said. "I've mended that twice in six days. The first time it was a clean hole, and I'd a road to do it on. This time it was torn, and I did it sitting in a ditch. There isn't a third in that flesh. If it goes again before the thaw, I shall be binding it like a carter's wife, and it'll knit as a carter's does, crooked, in three months. Have you got that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:75:It was only three words. But it had not come out as her other sentences came, each one finished and set down like a bowl on a board; the middle of it had given a little under her, as a plate gives underfoot. He made no answer, since he did not think that she could have borne to be answered. She had told a mill that she would be nobody's sixth, and a board at Thornwater that she was a stop; she had eaten out of their bowl for a week, standing; and this was the only way that she had of saying what a person became to her when she had gone into a marsh after his friend and watched him go into worse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:85:"I know what I'll get. I've had it six times." She was counting already, under the words. "I've had my hands on every one of them this morning, to see what that place had done. I'll not sit down to my bread with one left out, only because he can't be read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:95:"They're not gone again; you'd not have walked that path. They've been leaned on, by a thing that I'd not like to put a weight to." She sat back on her heels. "I can't shut what I can't read. You've heard me say it till you're sick of it. That's ten days of knowing that you've ribs, and they'll talk when it turns cold, and they'll not do more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:97:"Ten days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:99:"From this morning. Count it yourself; you will anyway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:103:"I told you a night that's only a night, and I meant one with nothing in front of it. You've not had one since, and you'll not get one on this road. But I've been looking at you for a quarter of an hour, and there's another kind." She nodded at his chest. "You read it frightened, in a loft. Tonight there's nothing left in you to be frightened with; I doubt that you could be afraid of a wolf. So you'll read it tonight, when the rest are down. Then you'll sleep, and you'll read it once more when you wake, before you've had time to think. That's three, on three days, with a night between each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:113:The first was the one that she had lit, at the track's end, with wet people round it and a line of breeches steaming on a rope between two bundles of reeds. The second she built for herself, twenty yards off and upwind, no bigger than a hat, since she would not have sparks near paper. She had the lid of the middle box for a table there, and her morning's work laid out on the turf under pebbles, one sheet beside another, to lose the damp that they had taken from a wall. She had gone into that place for the inner faces of four walls, and had come out with as much of one of them as a person can rub upside down in an hour with a girl holding her by the belt; and she set about it now as if the hour had been a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:115:She was as wet down the front as a person can be who has lain for an hour on her stomach on a stone with a spring slopping over it. Cael saw her shiver, from the near fire, and saw that she did not know she was doing it; she had her notebook open and three clean leaves before her, and her pencil had already begun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:125:"Seln says that a night won't hurt it." She did not turn round. "There's one. If I let it down, I've a stick with a string on, and nobody here to bend it who isn't asleep or mending. It stays in the bow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:129:"I know what he read. I watched it come up out of a hole this morning, the whole of it, when a carter had said that nobody ever saw so much." Her right hand moved on the stock, a finger's breadth, and lay still. "I'm not saying that it's coming. I'm saying that I've one, and it's in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:131:He left her to it, since she had sat on a stair for a day once with that same bow, for the same reason, and had been right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:139:Cael had been on roads with that woman for nineteen days in all, on a mountain and a moor and a ridge, and he had never once seen it. She lay down last at every camp, a stride and a half beyond the farthest of them, with a mule between, and at Thornwater she had shut a door. She had told him on a rock that there was nobody there who had not seen her asleep, and he had taken it for a manner of speaking; she slept as a person pays a debt, privately, to have it over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:151:Not one of the four of them got up to put the blanket back, since she would have woken. They let her be, in the middle of them, with a big man's wrist in her hand, while the short day went over toward the rise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:153:He wrote the day's bill then, with his eyes still swimming a little, and he kept it to what the day had cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:155:*Hundred and nineteenth day. The track's end, outside the stone. All six out. Brom's shoulder opened on a wall and is mended a second time, an hour; it will not be mended a third. Four bolts spent; one in the bow, with Lira.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:159:*Compression: the strike, standing. The old ribs. Ten days, she says.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:169:He read himself that night when the others were down, by her order, and he was an hour about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:175:At ten the short reading gave him his own gait, quick and slow, and tonight a good deal more slow than quick. He went on beyond ten, into the deep reading, and it was easier than it had been in a loft, since he had nothing in him tonight that could hurry it; he went along the dry hiss of the Wind at a walk, looking for a side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:177:He knew now what he was looking for. He had been in a river with banks, on a ridge, for a minute, and had felt them stand close and high on either hand like the walls of a mill-race. He went the whole length of the Wind and found none. The millstone was under it, and ran into it, and he could not have said where; he tried the wick, and the bellows, and the held note, which was thinner tonight than he had ever heard it, and they were ten, and not one of them had an edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:183:He had been afraid of it for seventeen days. He looked for the fear as he went, and it was simply not there, having been spent that morning with a good deal else on a ledge; and he came to the crowded place under his breastbone as a tired man comes to his own gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:187:There was no sound in it, and nothing in it moved. He brought the ten to it one after another, as he had in a loft, and every one of them leaned aside and went by and closed again beyond. Those were the three things that he had counted on the night of his birthday; he counted them again, and they had not changed by a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:191:He had stood on a wall that morning and looked down at a spring. The water there had come up against a fallen slab and piled, and curled back behind it in a slow eddy, as water does when it meets a thing lying in its road. He looked for the piling now, and there was none. Not one of the ten came up against that place. Each of them began to lean a long way short of it, and came back to its line a long way past; they went by it as a river goes round a stone that has lain in its bed for as long as there has been a river, without a ripple, along a way that was worn smooth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:199:He had something to point at now. The old words went onto it, and fitted, as a name fits a person whom one has known for years only by letters.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:209:He woke while the stars were still out, with frost on his blanket, and read it a third time before he had fairly remembered where he was. He was rested, and cold, and clear in the head, which was a third temper and as unlike the loft as it was unlike the night before; and the place lay where it had lain, with the ten going round it by the same worn way. It took him less than a quarter of an hour, and he wrote one line afterward by feel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:211:*Hundred and twentieth day, before light. The third: where it was, as it was, the same.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:217:"Three. The loft, and last night, and now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:219:"And a night between each." She rubbed her palms together. "Then I'll hear it. Come round here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:245:"There are ten in me. You found more than one and couldn't count them. It's ten, and I've counted them three times. Not one has a side. I looked for an hour last night for a bank like these, and there isn't any."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:253:"They don't meet it. That's what I had last night that I hadn't before. Water piles against a stone that's fallen in its way. These don't pile. They lean off a long way short of it, and come back a long way past. They go round it as yours goes down its bed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:263:"I gave it one a long while back, on an old leaf, when it was only something that another person's hands had caught the edge of. The still place. I never had anything to lay the words on till last night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:287:He did, and it surprised him how little he wished to move it. Nine days ago he would have given a good deal to hear that one word; he had tried to buy it at a well with a thing half looked at, and had been told what a single morning was worth. He had it now for the full price, three readings and a night between, and it was only a word about a debt, and it left the rest exactly as heavy as she had made it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:293:"I don't know what it is, and nor do you. I know what a person does with a place in himself that he's found: he goes back to it every night with his fingers." She was already turning toward Brom's blankets. "You've looked three times. You know where it is, and that it keeps. Let it keep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:3:They went in at first light on the hundred and nineteenth day, with a mist lying on the reeds as thick as wool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:5:Seln stepped over the grey stone first. Lira went second, with the healer's bow hanging from her right hand by its stock, spanned, and one of the five bolts held in the groove under her thumb. Karis had a roll of thin paper under one arm and her pencils in her hair. Cael walked fourth, and Brom behind him, with nothing on his back for the first time in three days and his right arm bound across his chest. The healer came last, with a pannier on her shoulder and her sleeves pushed up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:29:The floor was a ledge. It went round the foot of the four walls, three or four strides broad, under a foot of water; and in the middle of the room there was no floor at all. The plates had gone in there, a long time ago. He saw a hole as wide as a cart is long, with green depth in it. The water over it stood up in a low smooth dome and slid away on every side, and that was the spring. In the very middle of the hole one great slab lay aslant, like a table that has lost two legs, with its upper corner a hand under the surface.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:41:He did the other thing, which he had done in a room under a floor four days before without being asked. He stepped up beside her, and turned his left side to the wall, and bent his back, with his one good hand on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:67:Cael ran. He went along the ledge under the northern wall, away from the place, since a second weight there was the last thing that Brom wanted; and at the next break he got his hands on the stone and hauled. His right forearm had stopped a crossbow's stock five days before, and it told him so. He came over the top on his belly, and turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:75:Nobody moved, and for the space of ten breaths the only sounds in that place were the spring, and five people getting their wind, and the small knocking going round below them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:85:Cael looked at where each of them was, since there was nothing else that he could do for them. Karis and Lira sat on the northern wall near its low end, with the bow between them. Seln had gone along the same wall-top at a crouch, past Cael, and round the corner, and stood now on the eastern wall, which was the tallest, where he could see the whole of the hole. Cael was at the corner between them. And Brom lay by himself on the far side of the water, on a strip of wall no longer than a cart, which ended at the gap on one hand and at nothing on the other. Its top was green, and wet, since the spring's water slopped over it on the way out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:145:It shot from under him on the wet, and Brom's body did what any body does. His right arm came out of its binding. It caught the wall's end above his head and took the whole of him, on a shoulder that a healer had shut five days ago and given a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:165:She did not walk and she did not run. She skipped. She went from plate to plate with her arms out and her coat flapping, a short quick hop to each, and never the same plate twice, and never two counts in one place; a square woman in a dead man's coat, coming across thirty yards of broken floor like a child on stepping-stones. It would have been comical on any other morning of his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:181:There was Anchor, which he had set for the first time yesterday, on his knees, on a kerb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:185:It had left its round for a stone on a plate. It had left everything, yesterday, for a point that would not come away. What would it leave a fallen man for?
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:239:It could not tip the slab, and it would not leave it; and so it had done what it had done at the kerb, and drawn in its length about the one thing on its floor that would not come away. But the hole was not the dish. There was no room in it. It had come up out of the green depth coil upon coil, and lay heaped round the slab and over the ledge and against the foot of three walls, with the spring pouring off its back. It was longer than the room by twice. It was the colour of mud above; and where it had rolled to get its jaws about the stone, the whole of one side lay upward, as pale as tallow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:279:Seln had the bow again, spanned, with the third bolt in it. He had been holding it out to her. He did not answer; he went along the wall-top to the corner at a run, bent double, and round it onto the eastern wall, and Cael saw him bring a crossbow to his shoulder for the first time in two years of knowing him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:299:The spring stood up again over the place in its smooth low dome.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:307:"I spent your week," said Brom. His face was the colour of the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:309:"You spent it in a morning. I said that you would." She had her shoulder under his left arm, and she was wet to the waist. "Walk. You can be sorry on the grass."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:5:Karis took her hands away from it. It was a strip no longer than her forearm, and it had come from a shelf at Ternhall, in the bottom of a box, across a Line and a mountain with a herd on it. It lay now on a carter's hide, on the lid of that same box. Its upper end rested on the ring that she had walked on the ninety-sixth day, and its edge passed through the middle of the ring that she had walked that morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:19:"By a finger, at the end of four hands of calfskin, on a map that a man made by counting his suppers." Her voice was small and quite steady, and she had not taken her eyes from the gap. "I said that I should find out how far wrong a map of days could be along one line. That is how far. I think it very handsome of him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:23:"The ruler is brass. It is the two rings under it that I answer for, and I set one where a carter bent his road and the other where a broker's paper said a day and a little aside." She laid a finger beside the third. "This one I drew round a place where he had lifted his hand. He told me, on the evening that he gave me the skin, that he had seen that corner once, from a hill, with water standing in it, and had never had a wheel within a day of it. So I have three things on this lid, each of them a little wrong, and I cannot say by how much."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"His south road lies under your edge for a day's ticks beyond this stone. After that there is a single stroke, which is how he draws a track that he would not take a cart along, and it keeps beside the edge for a day more. Then it stops, and there is nothing under the brass that a wheel made."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:33:"Then that is the order in which we shall walk it: a road, and a track, and whatever a man remembered from a hill." Karis rolled the skin. "A ruler on a map of days gives me a direction that is good to the breadth of this pencil at the length of my arm, and by the third evening that is a strip of country a mile wide. I am not going to walk a line. I am going to walk a strip, with six pairs of eyes, and look at all of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:39:"Three days, by his ticks?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:45:"I shut that shoulder last night. A mend so deep wants a week of somebody's eye on it, as her arm wanted its fortnight, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends an hour of herself on a thing and walks off in the week that it's deciding. That's the hundred and twenty-first." A finger followed the thumb. "There's an edge at the end of that brass. I said in a loft where somebody ought to stand when you cross one, and I learned yesterday what it's worth to stand there." Another finger. "And I've sent word north that I'm nineteen, or more. It's the *or more* that I'm spending."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:49:"So I'll see the shoulder through its week, and I'll be at your next edge with my sleeves up. After that I'm the route, and nobody says a word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:55:It was Brom who answered her, and he did it by going to the left-hand box for the bowls. He set out six on the turf between the two irons, as he had on every night since a grey stone on the Fallow; and the healer, who had told a girl at a mill that she would eat out of the sixth and not sit in a place, watched him do it and held her tongue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:59:They left the stone at first light on the hundred and sixteenth day, and for three days the country was kinder to them than it had any need to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:61:The frost had gone off in the night. Cael woke to a low grey sky and a smell of wet earth, which he had not smelled for three weeks, and by the middle of the forenoon the ruts were dark and soft where they had rung the day before. The carters' road came back to its line beyond the low ground, as a one-armed man had said that it would, and went on south and east under the foot of the ridge. A salt cart had been along it a week ago, and nothing since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:71:"You're carrying on the one side and walking as if you had two. Let that strap out a hand, and take it across you." She waited while he did it. "That's a week I've given that shoulder. I'd as soon you didn't spend it for me in a morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He let it stand. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:81:On the second morning the road swung away south toward a hold whose smoke they could see, and the brass did not go with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:83:There was a track where Seln had said there would be: two ruts, grown over, with a tree as thick as Brom's leg standing in one of them. Nobody had driven it in a lifetime. It ran on along the low ground between pale grass and standing pools, and the day was so mild that gnats hung over the water in the afternoon, and a few yellow leaves were still on the silver-backed trees. Brom walked with his coat open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:93:She had the brass on the hide again as she said it. In three days he did not once hear her use the word that Brom had used; she spoke of *the third*, and of *the place*, and of what she would do with her cord when she stood at its edge, as another person might have spoken of a town that she had lived in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:95:The track went under the turf on the morning of the hundred and eighteenth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:97:It did not end at anything. The ruts grew shallower for a mile, and then there were none, and they were walking on wet grass with the hide for their only road. Karis carried it open before her in both hands. Where the carter's single stroke stopped, his wavy lines began; and inside the hour there was water on either hand of them, black and still, lying in the low places among the reeds exactly as a man had set it down on a calfskin twenty years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:123:"Big. Under. Comes up. Nobody's seen it whole." She rubbed her palms together. "He'd a name for it, the wold-wyrm; I'd not know a wold from a wash. He said that he'd not have gone down to those reeds for a year's salt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:137:"You shall, with the cord," said Karis. "Not today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:157:"He wrote it in a room under a floor, three days from here. I can tell you nothing more of him than that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:175:"The hounds went out," said Brom. "Every dawn, and home at night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:187:It was a hundred and eighty-five yards from the stone to that kerb, as Karis paced it afterward, and nobody else among them could have told iron from a reed at such a distance. Cael did not doubt her. Somebody had come here five cold terms ago with a cord and a mallet, and had driven one stake on the line, where he was sent to drive it. Then he had walked in along that grey path to set another on the lip of the thing itself; and whatever lay under the lip had not cared for the weight of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:219:They walked. Though not one of them could have said so yet, that was the rule of the place, and it kept them company the whole of the way in: a hundred and eighty-five yards, which Karis counted under her breath, with something in the reeds holding its station on them for exactly as long as they did not stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:275:She gave it to him before she had thought, and then she stared, and so did the rest of them. He had read out a great many things to that notebook in a hundred days: the width of a road, and the age of a bar, and what a paper meant. He had asked each time that it should be entered as said. He had never once put a mark in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:277:He wrote four short lines, in the small square hand of a man who had filled other people's files for fifteen years, and read them over as he read anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:285:She wrote the day beneath it, and her initial, and did not alter a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:293:"I carry one fragment that was made for nothing else. It's Anchor, the thinnest of the ten. I've had it for the better part of a year, and it has asked at every sill and gate and rail from Ostrand to this kerb, and I've told it no each time." He made himself say the rest. "I've let it look, and I've taken two of another person's down. I have never set one, and I can't tell you that I'm able to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:349:They made their camp at the track's end, a few yards outside the grey stone, with the two mules hobbled on the rise. It had come on cold again with the dusk. Seln lit the fire, and the six of them sat close about it while Karis said what she wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:351:"The walls are cut on the inner side. I could see the courses through the gap, and I could not have read one stroke of them at thirty yards if I had sat on that kerb for a week." She had her hands round her knees. "So it is the inside or it is nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:359:"I'll lift nothing. I'll not sit at a stone and listen, either. I did that for a day in a hole."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:381:Cael wrote the day's log with his back against the old stake, as he had written one at another stake three days' walk behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:383:*Hundred and eighteenth day. The edge of the third one, at the track's end. A marsh, and the reeds stop where the sigils stop. One bar, thirty-three, old, and no second. A hall in the middle of it, and a thing under the hall's floor that a carter told the healer nobody has seen whole.*

