   139	It was the fifth time that he had read that town, and the first from below. He had always had it from a saddle or a ridge, laid out like a board; now it stood over him. The river went by at his feet, dark, between two shelves of ice that had grown since he saw them last until each was as broad as a cart is long. The ferry's cable sagged across from bank to bank with a white rime on it. The scow lay on the near side, and a man with a mallet was knocking ice from its running blocks. In the doorway of the round shed a brown gelding with one white foot stood under a blanket and watched him do it.
   140	
   141	Above the landing there was a long low building with a deep eave. The plank under the eave was bare of its book. There was the smith's chimney, and the taller house on the rise, with two. Along the water lay the meadow, where forty fires had burned in the dry months and twelve when he went south. He counted three.
   142	
   143	The snow was coming down on all of it alike, and on five people and a mule.
   144	
   145	He noticed where he had begun. He had started at the eave, and gone on from there. It was how a person counts a place when he knows already which roof he is going to sleep under.
   146	
   147	The man with the mallet had straightened his back.
   148	
   149	"You've not got my rope," he said. It was the ferryman, and he did not say it as a complaint.
   150	
   151	"No. We've not forgotten it."
   152	
   153	"Nor have I. Sixty yards, four-strand." He knocked a last cake of ice into the river. "I'll not want it this side of the thaw. There's one more crossing in her, tomorrow, if that shelf lets me; the post comes down to the far bank for it. Then I take my lines in."
   154	
   155	"There's a light in it," said Brom, of the long low building. "And I can smell his fire. I'd like to be told that I'm allowed to stop walking."
   156	
   157	"You were to see a woman about a knot first," said Lira.
   158	
   159	"She'll be in there. They're all in there. There's nowhere else to be."
   160	
   161	He was right. The room was fuller than Cael had ever seen it, since the cold term had driven the country in off its roads. There were fifty people on those benches, and a fire on the hearth that a man could have stood up in. The hide-woman was there, and three grey heads over a pair of dice, and two carters with their boots to the blaze. At the last bench before the hearth sat a man with leather on his wrist, with four about him at their distances. One of the four was a lean old woman with a bowl.
   162	
   163	Pike was under his sailcloth. His eyes came up as the cold came in, and went over the five in the doorway, and went over them again.
   164	
   165	"Shut that," he said. "You're letting my heat out. Then come here and tell me whether I've got it wrong."
   166	
   167	He had the ledger open already at their heading. He turned his shoulder to the room and gave out a line that nobody had yet heard, in the voice that he used under his eave. It was a recovery, on the Stair road, at a broker's posting. It was taken. Six had gone in. There were instruments found there, and brought up, and kept by the crew that found them; and six had come out.
   168	
   169	"Is that wrong anywhere?"
   170	
   171	"No," said Cael.
   172	
   173	"His clerk's been up my bank four times to ask what he's to put a mark to."
   174	
   175	"Nothing. They're staying with us."
   176	
   177	"So I'd heard." Pike dipped his pen, and wrote, and blew on it. In the last column, where a price went, he set down three things with a stop between each, and read them out as he made them.
   178	
   179	*three hundred; taken; not paid*
   180	
   181	He said the whole of it over, to the room. Then he counted down the leaf with the end of his pen.
   182	
   183	"That's nine."
   184	
   185	---
   186	
   187	He did not shut the book.
   188	
   189	"It's first snow," said Pike. "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw. I've done forty today. This is the last."
   190	
   191	He turned his shoulder again. Cael saw the hide-woman put down her cup.
   192	
   193	"*Crew of six. Ford cleared, traverse mapped, Ring entered, Stair entered and instruments recovered, Hall entered. No losses. Returned every time.*"
   194	
   195	It lay on that hot room like the first of the snow. There was not a carter there who did not know those three places by the tales that were told of them. Cael heard a bench creak, and a die roll off a board and nobody stoop for it.
   196	
   197	Pike had his pen over the end of the line, where a figure went.
   198	
   199	It stayed there, and he did not bring it down. He looked at the place for as long as it takes to count ten, with his one hand quite still; and then he laid the pen in its groove and did a thing that Cael had never once seen him do.
   200	
   201	He turned the ledger round.
   202	
   203	He took it by its corner and swung it on the bench, so that the writing faced the other way. Cael had read every line of that book upside down for a hundred and twenty days, across an oak plank, as he had once read a bailiff's list on a counter. It lay the right way up before him now. He read the season in a square black hand, and at the end of it a ruled space with nothing in it.
   204	
   205	"Teague says you need a column I don't have. I've kept this ledger thirty years. First time it's been wrong about a crew by being too small."
   206	
   207	He did not say it sadly. He said it as he had once said what a Bronze was worth who lost a carter: as arithmetic.
   208	
   209	"You've left it empty," said Lira.
   210	
   211	"I have. I'd put a figure there for any crew on that cloth, and be within a line of right. I'll not put one where I know that it's wrong." He looked at the space. "It can stand till I've a column. I told the girl so, the night that you went south."
   212	
   213	Then he shut the book on the empty place, and put his stone on it.
   214	
   215	Cael stood before a hearth with snow melting on his shoulders. A magister had written of him once that no instrument would see his like. He had meant it kindly, and had entered it in a daybook that Cael was not let to carry away. This man had turned his own book about on a bench, in front of fifty people, to show a boy the hole in it.
   216	
   217	By the fire one of the two carters had tipped his head back, to look at the sailcloth over Pike's bench. Five names stood there on the eleventh line, as they had for seventeen days.
   218	
   219	"Eleventh," he said.
   220	
   221	"Aye," said the other, and spat into the ashes.
   222	
   223	Somebody laughed, a long way down the room, and it was not an unkind laugh.
   224	
   225	The man with the leather on his wrist had got to his feet. He came down between the benches without haste, and stopped a stride from Cael, with his thumbs in his belt over the hand-axe.
   226	
   227	"After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes."
   228	
   229	It was not an offer, and it was not a question. He had seen a strip of brass run off the edge of a calfskin, and had asked nothing about it, and had walked five days home to say this.
   230	
   231	"Noted," Cael said.
   232	
   233	It was the third time that he had given that man the word. Teague nodded, by the width of a finger, and went back to his bench. The boy who sat there had stopped with a heel of bread half way to his mouth. He did not start again until his captain was down.
   234	
   235	Brom had not waited for any of it. He had gone straight down the room to the lean old woman, as he was told at a fork. He stood in front of her like a boy with a torn coat. She did not get up. She looked at four yards of webbing, once, from the top turn to the bottom, and at how he held the arm inside it; then she went back to her bowl.
   236	
   237	"It'll do," she said.
   238	
   239	Pike had heard, and he scratched the stump of his arm through its pinned sleeve.
   240	
   241	"Fence. Forty. Teague says it's yours if you want it; he's got the cold road," he said, to the fire, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "It's the long one, east, with the axe-head on its gate. He'd a day's posts still to set when he went off after you, and there's the whole far side to mend before the thaw. A hold will feed a crew that's mending its fence."
   242	
   243	All four of them looked at Brom. He had asked a one-armed man once what a board paid somebody with no Path at all, and had carried the answer against his skin for seventy days.
   244	
   245	"Ours," said Brom.
