books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md-106-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md:107:Nobody had suggested crossing. Nobody had needed to. The five of them stood on the crest above a cordon of iron round a circle eight hundred meters across with a shape at its middle that the dark had, and looked at it, and the thing they were looking at was not the shape. It was the line, and the fact that four of the five of them would go dark at it, and the fact that the fifth would not, and that nobody knew what a circle twice the size did to the four — whether it was the same dark at twice the distance, or a different thing — and that the one person on the continent the fourth site would let in without a mark on him was standing on the crest with his hands at his sides and a straightedge's worth of dread in his coat, and had not said a word.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md-108-
