books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-21.md-28-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-21.md:29:They spent the afternoon on trials, and he wrote it as one, because Karis would, and because the six of them had walked into two floors on folklore and a stake and had learned twice what that cost. The trials could not be run from the line; it was a hundred and seventy meters out in the marsh and nothing a man could throw would reach the water from it. So they crossed at the stone in the order Seln gave, Oryn checking her hands at the stride and saying "Gone," and went in along the one dry way — a causeway the drowning had left, a stride wide, standing water either side — to the hall's outer wall, and ran the trials from its coping on the marsh side, with the rule already walking with them: nobody stops on anything with water under it. The wall was not the floor; they stood on stone the floor could not reach and did not once go through the doorway. At dusk they went back out along the causeway and over the stone, and Oryn said "Back," and they camped at the line.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-21.md-30-
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-21.md-32-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-21.md:33:"Carters won't say it near water," she said. She was on the track's dry end with her hands turned over on her knees and the roll on her back, looking at the hall the way she looked at a thing with a rule in it. "There's a hall on a bearing south of the Stair road with a spring in it, and there's a thing under the spring, and the carters who go that way — there aren't many — call it the wold-wyrm. I've never seen it. Nobody I've had my hands on has seen it whole. I've had a man's leg that had been in its mouth, and he said it came up under him out of a foot of water like the floor had opened, and he never saw a head." She looked at the churned mud. "That's the whole of what the roads know. Big. Under. Comes up. Nobody's seen it whole."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-21.md-34-
