ines; Lira's order at first snow; the ankle unmended; Brom's shoulder bound, not mended a third time; Oryn's crossbow with Lira, one bolt left.\012- **The deployment count: 23 → 28.** Check each use (24 Anchor, a first point set as a trial; 25 the read under water; 26 Compression; 27 Anchor, a point laid by hand in the breach; 28 Tide on Oryn, asked and granted) against STATE_LEDGER §2's rules and Movement 7's ruling that the unbinding has no measured reach, rate or price. Rule on flag 6: the trial point counted as deployment 24 against the protected inventory's "Anchor … used twice this year, once each way" — which count agrees with the protected inventory and with what Book 8's plan expects (the early Book 8 audit is at book-08-before-the-paths/state/B7-RECONCILIATION-AUDIT.md)? Give exact fixes for the log lines if the count should be twenty-seven. Shadow NOT used. The self-readings not counted.\012- **The debt and the still place.** Two further self-readings on different days (the author: night of the 119th; before light on the 120th — are those "different days" as Oryn set it?), then all of it told to Oryn at once, with her leave for reading her (ask the person whose body it is — Movement 7's rule). "The still place" becomes a located, spoken finding; it is NOT presented as newly coined (it is Cael's log label in locked Books 5–6); shape and sensation only: "Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log."; no *source*, *before*, *primordial*, *recurrence*; no theory. Rule on flag 9's addition (Cael tells the other four on the 121st). **Teague is neither read nor asked**, and learns nothing of the mechanism; his crew unnamed, the older healer of no Path unnamed.\012- **The line across the sites (flag 1).** The closed text has the Fallow due east of Thornwater, the Stair west and south, the Hall south and east; the plan needs the sites on one line ("Three points make a line. Four make an argument."; "The sites were on an alignment. Not random."). Check the closed chapters' geography (road times in STATE_LEDGER; ch27, ch45–48, this movement) and rule whether the author's handling (no compass bearing claimed; Karis's "as near as this skin can show") is enough, or give exact fixes. Only "Someone made this pattern." (once; nobody answers) and "a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one." — no other maker sentence. Karis's hypothesis still unstated; her window ends exactly on the pencil stopping, no who or why.\012- Flag 5 (the old woman's knee, mended at Millrace, walking to Thornwater); flag 9 (Oryn leaving the bow and a roll of tape with Lira at the fork; the ferryman's rope; Pike's first-snow ruling-off custom). Money to ninety-six with every step on the page.\012- OWNER #49 (protected lines verbatim; never "his Path" outside them), #51 ("Tomorrow makes three" not explained; the closing's "walked into the quiet three times and come out" is its own protected count), #24, #25.\012- **For Book 8:** in section 8, list EVERYTHING Book 8's Movement 1 must inherit from the surviving prose, and say which findings of book-08-before-the-paths/state/B7-RECONCILIATION-AUDIT.md the closed Movement 7 and this movement confirm, change or withdraw.\012\012## Write\012editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-008/plan-audit.md: a verdict FIRST (RATIFY / RATIFY WITH LINE FIXES / REPAIR, with scope), then sections 1–8, then exact line fixes in exactly this format, each old string verified with grep to match exactly once in its file:\012N. `manuscript/chapter-NN.md`\012   old: `...`\012   new: `...`\012Use \n for a line break inside a fix. Give chapter:line for every claim. Never propose a fix inside a protected unit except to restore its exact wording.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:281:*Deployment since the Line: fourteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:153:"I was to be at this gate on the ninety-sixth. I came in on the hundred and twelfth. That's sixteen." The thumb went down. "There's a day's bench here still, that I'd have done tomorrow, and the road beyond it: two gates, and then the ford with the oxen on the hundred and twenty-first, where they looked for me on the hundred and fourth. That's seventeen." A finger. "Now put your ridge into it. It's a half-day up to wherever this is, and I'd not see the place before dark. A day while you're in it. A half-day down. That's two, if every one of you walks out on his own legs, and you've not once done that since I knew you." Another finger. "So the ford's nineteen. And beyond the ford there's a child that I heard on the eighty-ninth and can't hear now before the hundred and twenty-eighth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:81:"That one I have walked. I have its round, and its radius, and eleven sheets." The finger moved a long way west and south, to a blank among the carter's hill-hatching. "That is a day south of the ferry and a little aside; I rang it on the ninety-second evening, and wrote *guess*." It moved again, far down toward the corner of the hide, where the strokes of his water lay thick. "That is a long way south and east, with no road inside a day of it, and it is a guess as well. And the fourth is not on this skin at all." She lifted her eyes to the loft door. "It is on a sheet that he counted once, as a boy, and cannot place. One sheet had two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:219:*Nothing out here reads me. And at a line you can mark with a rock, every Path in this camp stops, and mine doesn't. Karis won't write her hypothesis. I won't write mine. We're going to find the other circles. Pike said nobody goes in twice. Tomorrow makes three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:247:Out of that gate on the hundred and eleventh, when she had said the ninety-fifth: sixteen. Two days of road to the next. Two gates after it, and an old woman on the way whose knee had never once kept to a healer's days; that was where the odd one would go. Then the ford with the oxen, where they had last seen her on the eighty-fourth and had looked for her on the hundred and fourth. She would come down to it on the hundred and twenty-first, if nothing broke, and that was seventeen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:53:*Hundred and eleventh day. The halting-ground above the wash. One reading done, two owed, and nothing tried tonight; I was in a hurry, and she never asked for that. Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:239:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:63:"I'm coming to them. You said four wheres. One's walked. One's this, with a paper nailed across it." She nodded at the sheets on Seln's knee. "The third's a long way south and east, with no road inside a day of it, and nobody's put a price on it at all. So we go and pace that one, on our own time, with a healer at the edge. I'm not saying leave them alone. I'm saying I'll not walk into the one that somebody's paying us to walk into."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:195:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:31:*Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:47:The last ferry crossed at noon on the hundred and thirtieth day, with two fingers of snow on its rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:99:It had been the hundred and twenty-second morning, eight days before, when Oryn left five people standing at a heap of chock-stones and took the left-hand road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:107:She had been a stop for nine days. She said it over without softening it. There had been a bench at the mill that she left a day short. There were two gates beyond the mill that had not seen her at all. There was the ford with the oxen, where they had looked for her on the hundred and fourth. She had said seventeen to a loft, and nineteen to a carter, and *or more* to a stone, and it would be the hundred and twenty-fifth before she so much as came down out of these hills. That was three weeks, to the day. Beyond the ford was the gate that had given her the coat, and beyond that the child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:135:She came down to Thornwater's eastern gate at dusk on the hundred and twenty-fifth day. The bar was drawn for her before she had called.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:221:The carter came over the saddle on the hundred and thirty-first morning, with a hand's depth of snow on the road and his eyebrows white, and he had a strip of bark for Pike and another that was not for Pike at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:3:They went in at first light on the hundred and nineteenth day, with a mist lying on the reeds as thick as wool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:217:*Nine. With the reading on the road, ten since Thornwater. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:281:But he could be half a day from where a certain paper pointed, with a spear and a bow and a boy, on honest business. Nobody could say that it rested on nothing, since it rested on three stakes and four marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:291:"I heard it. I never knew you take four marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:323:"No. It'll cost us the four marks; she'll not put her mark to two stakes of three." He was wringing out his breeches. "Get your bread. We're going up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:5:It was the hundred and fifteenth morning. He had slept as a stone sleeps, and woken to find that the ground came to his feet when it should, and that his right arm would bend. His left palm was wound in what remained of a roll of tape. He had counted four upright shapes on the rim before he had his boots on, one at each of the places where a hood had stood the day before, and he had been on his feet with nothing in his hands before he saw whose they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:351:"We've a stake to set at a weir," he said. "There's four marks hangs on it." His eyes went to the calfskin that Karis was unrolling. "I'll tell the one-armed man that he's five strokes to look at. He'll want to know what he's to write."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:357:*Hundred and fifteenth day. The stone at the Stair. All six up, and nothing spent. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three. A hundred and two in hand. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against a broker's mark, and that mark is never going on this paper.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:45:"I shut that shoulder last night. A mend so deep wants a week of somebody's eye on it, as her arm wanted its fortnight, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends an hour of herself on a thing and walks off in the week that it's deciding. That's the hundred and twenty-first." A finger followed the thumb. "There's an edge at the end of that brass. I said in a loft where somebody ought to stand when you cross one, and I learned yesterday what it's worth to stand there." Another finger. "And I've sent word north that I'm nineteen, or more. It's the *or more* that I'm spending."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:59:They left the stone at first light on the hundred and sixteenth day, and for three days the country was kinder to them than it had any need to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:61:The frost had gone off in the night. Cael woke to a low grey sky and a smell of wet earth, which he had not smelled for three weeks, and by the middle of the forenoon the ruts were dark and soft where they had rung the day before. The carters' road came back to its line beyond the low ground, as a one-armed man had said that it would, and went on south and east under the foot of the ridge. A salt cart had been along it a week ago, and nothing since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:95:The track went under the turf on the morning of the hundred and eighteenth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:389:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-four.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:71:It had been the hundred and seventeenth morning, three days before, when Teague took his crew up the notch from the mill with one more in it than had come down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:75:He had set it. The woman with the net had put her mark on the shingle, and there were four marks in his belt that he had counted as lost. He had sent his word north by the mill's carter, in so many words, for the man with the book: five strokes to look at, and all of them walking; and the three hundred was to stay where it lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:113:They were on the brow of the low ground before noon. The four crossbows had lain where he left them, by a grey stone, and the frost had kept every print about it; and from between two bars of iron the trail went off south and east, as plain as a ditch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:131:It was the middle of the hundred and twentieth day when the ground fell away in front of him, and he saw reeds, and grey walls standing in water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:163:*Three. Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:301:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:3:It had been the afternoon of the hundred and nineteenth day when Karis sat down at the far fire with three clean leaves before her, and by the dusk of the hundred and twentieth she had risen from them only to sleep and to pace a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:91:She laid it out for them on the hundred and twenty-first morning, in daylight, on the lid of the middle box, with all of them looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:3:They came to the fork two hours after sunrise on the hundred and twenty-second day, and the healer stopped the grey mule in the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:5:It was a plain enough place. The road divided at a heap of chock-stones that carters had left, and the right hand went on south and east under the foot of a long bare ridge. The left climbed away toward a notch between two hills, with the frost lying blue in it, and beyond the notch there was nothing to be seen but sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:3:They turned for home on the hundred and twenty-fourth morning, and it took them six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:49:He woke on the hundred and twenty-ninth morning lying on his left side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:55:On the hundred and twenty-fourth night, the first of the road home, Seln read a page and a half that he had by heart already.

