### editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md
5:Karis took her hands away from it. It was a strip no longer than her forearm, and it had come from a shelf at Ternhall, in the bottom of a box, across a Line and a mountain with a herd on it. It lay now on a carter's hide, on the lid of that same box. Its upper end rested on the ring that she had walked on the ninety-sixth day, and its edge passed through the middle of the ring that she had walked that morning.
29:Seln had been reading the hide over her shoulder, as he read any paper.
37:The healer had listened to the whole of it with the grey mule's rope over her shoulder.
45:"I shut that shoulder last night. A mend so deep wants a week of somebody's eye on it, as her arm wanted its fortnight, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends an hour of herself on a thing and walks off in the week that it's deciding. That's the hundred and twenty-first." A finger followed the thumb. "There's an edge at the end of that brass. I said in a loft where somebody ought to stand when you cross one, and I learned yesterday what it's worth to stand there." Another finger. "And I've sent word north that I'm nineteen, or more. It's the *or more* that I'm spending."
49:"So I'll see the shoulder through its week, and I'll be at your next edge with my sleeves up. After that I'm the route, and nobody says a word."
61:The frost had gone off in the night. Cael woke to a low grey sky and a smell of wet earth, which he had not smelled for three weeks, and by the middle of the forenoon the ruts were dark and soft where they had rung the day before. The carters' road came back to its line beyond the low ground, as a one-armed man had said that it would, and went on south and east under the foot of the ridge. A salt cart had been along it a week ago, and nothing since.
63:Seln went a quarter of a mile in front, where he could be seen against the sky. Karis walked behind him with the hide under her arm, and at every halt she unrolled it on her knee, and laid the brass along it, and looked from the skin to the country and back again. Lira came next, on the whole of her foot, and a road gone soft was no better for her than a road gone hard. The great boot slid in the mud as it had skated on the ice, and her right arm went out, and her left stayed where a knot held it.
71:"You're carrying on the one side and walking as if you had two. Let that strap out a hand, and take it across you." She waited while he did it. "That's a week I've given that shoulder. I'd as soon you didn't spend it for me in a morning."
79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He let it stand. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
103:The ground fell away in front of them into a basin as round as a plate. It was full of reeds, the colour of old straw, with black water shining between the stems; and the reeds all stopped at one edge, on every side, as though somebody had gone round them with a pair of shears. From the foot of the rise the old ruts came out of the turf again and ran straight down to that edge. Beyond it something pale and narrow went on across the marsh in the same line, as straight as a ruled stroke.
123:"Big. Under. Comes up. Nobody's seen it whole." She rubbed her palms together. "He'd a name for it, the wold-wyrm; I'd not know a wold from a wash. He said that he'd not have gone down to those reeds for a year's salt."
151:Seln had gone down on one knee. He looked at the scale, and at the way the turf had closed about the foot; then he got up, and walked ten yards along the reeds on either side of the ruts with his eyes on the ground, and came back.
153:"That has stood five cold terms. It is of an age with the Fallow's, and with the old one at our last stone, and I should say that the same mallet drove all three." He brushed his knee. "There is no second. Nobody has come back to this place with a newer bar and a mark under his number; or if he came, he drove nothing. I shall not choose between those."
185:"Bent. Over, about the height of a knee, with its head lower than the bend. Like a nail that's been struck from underneath."
193:"We learned what the Fallow did to each of us before we walked a furlong into it," she said. "I should like to know what this one does before I kneel down in it with my back turned."
205:They crossed. Karis's sigil went out at the stride, and she said *gone*, quietly, as she had said it at two other stones. Behind her the healer's lips were moving, and they stopped.
223:It was two strides broad, of the same grey, and it went away to the right and the left in a great curve; Cael put the whole round of it at seventy yards across. Within it there was water, a foot or two deep and as clear as a well's, moving slowly outward and spilling over the kerb's low places into the marsh. Under the water lay the floor. It had been one floor once. It was broken now into great plates, like the ice on a pond that has gone to pieces and frozen again, and every broken edge was round. Thirty yards off, in the middle of it, the walls stood.
229:Karis sat down on the grey with her knees drawn up, and set Hesk's clock beside her on a folded stocking.
231:She timed it. The knocking went away round the dish to the left, behind the hall, and was lost; and when the hand of the clock had gone a quarter of the way round its face, it came back from the right, plate after plate, and passed below their boots. She let it go round three times before she would write the figure.
243:The plate tipped. Cael saw only two things: a darkness as broad as a door rising at the plate's edge, and the two halves of it parting like the shells of a mussel. Then the stone was not there. The plate fell back with a slap that wetted the six of them to the knee, and the water rocked, and was clear.
253:She threw side-arm, low, with the whole of her one good shoulder in it.
271:"It had. And it passed under five places where that stone had struck and gone on, and it did not touch one of them."
319:It was like having a tooth drawn. Every other fragment that he owned went thin and far away, Wind and Storm and the tenth together, and the cold of the water, and the kerb under his knees; there was only the one point, and something very strong that wished to take it up, and himself saying that it should stay. The jaws came down a second time, and a third.
335:He was on his hands and knees, shaking, and Brom's left fist was in his collar.
351:"The walls are cut on the inner side. I could see the courses through the gap, and I could not have read one stroke of them at thirty yards if I had sat on that kerb for a week." She had her hands round her knees. "So it is the inside or it is nothing."
371:Lira had the healer's bow across her knee, and she was looking past the fire at the dark place where the reeds began.
379:Brom laughed, once, and stopped, since it had pulled at his shoulder.
387:*Anchor: one point, set on a plate at the kerb's foot, and held for sixty counts. The first that I ever set. It came at eleven and stayed with it till I let go, and while it did I had nothing else at all. Counted in the number. I'll not call a trial a use.*
389:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-four.*
