169:These did not stand alone. Anchor had known it before his eyes did; it had asked at two bars with an old road between them, ten days ago, and here it asked at every pace, in its thin held note, as far as he could hear. There was a bar at his right knee, a hand's length clear of the turf, and another a pace beyond it, and another a pace beyond that. They went on so in both directions, one to a pace, round the head of that valley, for as far as he could see iron against the grass.
175:He moved to the next on his knees, and the next.
179:Lira had gone along the fence to the left, counting under her breath, and Brom to the right. They came back together.
191:They had gone twenty cords along that fence when she stood up, with the short cord in her fist. It was full dark.
197:Seln had not got up from his knees. He was looking along the bars, away into the dark, as he looked through a paper.
209:It was the question, and all five of them knew it. A line half this size had put out five sigils at a stride and given them back at a stride, the same, with nothing lost. Would one of twice the size give them back? There was no cord that could tell her that. Somebody would have to walk over that iron with a Path in her and come out again; and the one person who could have stood at the fence with her sleeves up was two days gone through a gap.
229:*Four sites. One line. The last one's twice the size and the Compact fenced it. The bearing runs through it and keeps going. Karis says four points is an argument. I say it's a bearing — the kind their maps draw as nothing. Home is Lowmarch now. I just wrote that without noticing. Nineteen, ten fragments, a still place at the center of me, and a line on a map that somebody drew before there were maps. We're not done. We're oriented.*
### editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md
17:Brom laid six bowls at every halt, and took one back. The mule went on the low side of nothing. Karis did her sums walking, and Seln brought his one word down the file; and to Cael, who had learned that crew's sound on a hundred roads and had learned it again at a fork with a pair of boots gone out of it, five had already begun to seem the number that a road was walked by.
23:He could not lie on them. He slept sitting, against a box or a wall, with his knees drawn up. He woke at every turn of the watch with the three old breaks as plain to him as three fingers laid on his side. They were no worse on the road. They only spoke, exactly as a healer had said that they would, and he counted her ten days off against them each night as a man counts off a sentence: the sixth on the ridge, and the seventh at the stones, and the eighth under the fence of a hold that would not open its gate.
31:He was three strides behind. He saw the boot go, and her right arm fly out; and he saw the left, under the grey wool, start to come and stop at a knot. She went down on her right shoulder and her hip, hard, with the bow clattering on her back. She lay for one breath with her cheek on the ice.
33:Brom had the mule's rope in his good hand, and he did not let go of it. Not one of them went to lift her. Each of them knew what she had been told at a stone about that arm, and what she would say to a hand under it.
35:She got up by herself, on the right hand and both knees, as she climbed a ladder.
57:He read it on his watch, as he had on every night since a room under a floor. He sat with his back to four sleepers and the limp little book open on his knee, tilted to what the embers gave. He did not need the light. He used it regardless, since there is a difference between remembering a hand and looking at it.
59:It was a small hand, and upright. It pressed. It ruled no margin of its own, having been given a ruled leaf, and it kept inside the blue lines as a careful man keeps to a path. He knew by the slope of the fours that their writer had stood to write them, with the book against his thigh. He knew that the man had been surprised at the eighth of eight figures, since he had gone back and underlined it; and that he had been more than surprised at the line below, which he had gone over twice until the pencil cut.
63:He had told four people at a fire that he wanted a face. He had gone up a ridge for one, and had been given six hoods and a bow with its maker's stamp filed off. He had broken that bow across a stone. He had not broken a thing in anger since he was a boy, and he was not proud of it, and he had entered it.
65:This was the nearest that he had come. It was a person's own book. In twelve years of that matter he had handled a great deal of paper. Every leaf of it had been written by a clerk, at dictation, for somebody up a stair. Nobody had dictated this. A man had gone alone into a hole in the ground with a cord and a mallet, and had put down what he found there, for himself and for no reader, in the order in which he found it, and had been astonished.
83:It was on the fourth day of that road, with the wind gone into the north, that he saw what he had been doing.
87:He had not meant to keep it so. He had put it into his coat in a cold room because it was registry paper. Reading registry paper was the one craft that he had brought east with him. That had been true. It was also true that he had read it to nobody since. A researcher had wanted one figure out of it, at a fire by a marsh, and had not felt able to ask. She had stopped with her pencil over a leaf, and looked at her own cuff, where the figure had been washed out. He had gone to her. He had held the page to the light for her three times with his fingers shut on its spine.
91:He went over it as he would have gone over a return. The case was shut for cause. There were names in it, and what was done to their owners; and there was a leaf in it that was no copy. It would be opened on a day, before the right people, or it would burn those whom it was kept for. He did not doubt that. He had audited it on a great many watches.
119:He could not dispute that, and he did not try. She had the book open before he had turned to go on; she walked the next mile with her nose a hand from a dead man's pencil, and went into a rut to the knee, and did not appear to notice.
127:The first snow met them at the river.
129:They had come down the south road since noon, past a well with a new rope on its windlass, under a sky the colour of a slate. Cael felt something touch his face as the meadow opened, and took it for ash from somebody's fire. Then there was another, on Lira's sleeve, which did not melt.
145:He noticed where he had begun. He had started at the eave, and gone on from there. It was how a person counts a place when he knows already which roof he is going to sleep under.
149:"You've not got my rope," he said. It was the ferryman, and he did not say it as a complaint.
167:He had the ledger open already at their heading. He turned his shoulder to the room and gave out a line that nobody had yet heard, in the voice that he used under his eave. It was a recovery, on the Stair road, at a broker's posting. It was taken. Six had gone in. There were instruments found there, and brought up, and kept by the crew that found them; and six had come out.
189:"It's first snow," said Pike. "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw. I've done forty today. This is the last."
191:He turned his shoulder again. Cael saw the hide-woman put down her cup.
215:Cael stood before a hearth with snow melting on his shoulders. A magister had written of him once that no instrument would see his like. He had meant it kindly, and had entered it in a daybook that Cael was not let to carry away. This man had turned his own book about on a bench, in front of fifty people, to show a boy the hole in it.
219:"Eleventh," he said.
227:"After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes."
235:Brom had not waited for any of it. He had gone straight down the room to the lean old woman, as he was told at a fork. He stood in front of her like a boy with a torn coat. She did not get up. She looked at four yards of webbing, once, from the top turn to the bottom, and at how he held the arm inside it; then she went back to her bowl.
### editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md
5:The inn's keeper was a slow heavy man with a cloth over his shoulder, who had drawn their jugs for a hundred and twenty days and had never been asked for anything else. She told him what she wanted: a dry floor, and a door that shut, and a table that nobody ate at. It was for paper, she said. She would have it until the thaw.
9:She took the purse out of her coat. Cael had seen that woman pay for a cart, and a boot, and a kid; each time she had opened her notebook first, and done the sum, and done it again beneath. She did not open it now. She put her fingers into the neck of the bag and laid six marks on the board, one upon another, with her eyes on the door behind the chimney.
17:Brom set the kit on the table. It lay there in its tarred canvas, with the strap coiled on the lid, as it had lain on a floor under the ground: a cord knotted at the fives, and a mallet, and a bar that had never been driven. Beside it Karis put a dead man's ledger, shut. The three boxes stood against the warm wall, and each of them had one leaf in it that was the same as its fellows.
21:She laid them in three heaps along the deal, and did not try to make their edges agree; the Fallow's were long strips, and the Stair's were leaves, and the hall's were whatever a person could tear from a roll with her teeth. There were eleven in the first heap. There were twenty-nine in the second, and twelve in the third.
25:"Fifty-two sheets. Forty-one kinds, at the two that I could do properly. Three perimeters of one size, and a fourth that is twice it." Karis leaned on the deal with both palms. "I had three boxes of other people's stone when I crossed the Line. I should not have said that there was anything east of it to put in them."
29:Cael did not go to the hearth with the others. He sat down at the meal-sack end of that table, with Hesk's book, while she worked; and he did the thing that he did at the start of anything, which he had last done on the night that he was nineteen.
31:*Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-eight.*
37:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
39:*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.*
47:The last ferry crossed at noon on the hundred and thirtieth day, with two fingers of snow on its rail.
49:The ferryman had been at the shelf since first light with a pole and an axe, to cut her a lane. Half the meadow stood on the landing to see it. The scow went out along its cable slowly, shouldering the broken ice, with the gelding walking his round in the shed and the boy calling the turns. On the far bank a man in a hat with one side cut away stood waiting beside a mule, as he had stood at a ford on the fourth day past the Line.
77:He wrote them at the end of Pike's plank, on two leaves torn out of Hesk's own book, with the horn ink-pot and a borrowed pen. He had owed both for a long while, and he had been waiting for a thing to say; and he had not expected, when it came, that it would go into so few words.
83:*Vell. There's a second ledger on the continent with my name in it, kept by a one-armed man in a town on no map, and this week he stopped numbering me. He said his book was wrong about us by being too small. You said once that somebody should have a record that doesn't answer to the Compact. There are two now. The session still stands. — C.*
91:The scow went over once more, and came back empty. Then the ferryman and his boy took her lines in. They drew the hauling rope off the drum, fathom by fathom, stiff with ice, and coiled it in the shed; and the gelding was led away up the bank to the smith's stable with his blanket on.
103:The jenny went as she had always gone, which was badly. She led as a gate swings. For the whole of the first morning Oryn's right hand kept going out to a small brown cheek that was two days behind her on another road; it met a grey ear like an oar, and came back. She let it. She had told a girl in a yard that a beast should not be named, and had learned at a wicket how little that helped.
107:She had been a stop for nine days. She said it over without softening it. There had been a bench at the mill that she left a day short. There were two gates beyond the mill that had not seen her at all. There was the ford with the oxen, where they had looked for her on the hundred and fourth. She had said seventeen to a loft, and nineteen to a carter, and *or more* to a stone, and it would be the hundred and twenty-fifth before she so much as came down out of these hills. That was three weeks, to the day. Beyond the ford was the gate that had given her the coat, and beyond that the child.
113:She began with what her hands had, as she had been taught. They were full again. She had mended nobody for three days, and slept in a ruined hold with her back to a wall, and she could feel the purse of her reserve sitting heavy and whole, as it had not sat since a mill. They had held a shoulder twice, and it was mended, and bound by a woman who knew more about holding than she did; she had watched a drover's turn laid three times and could lay it now herself. They had held an arm with eight marks on it, all taken. They had been on a boy's wrist for a count of ten and had got what they always got.
125:*So are you.* It had come out of her crooked, and she had heard it come. She went over it now, strictly, with her knuckles ready. He was on the route. That was the whole of what the words said, and they were true of a miller's man and an old woman on a hurdle. She had gone into a dish of water after a shoulder, since she was nearest. He had gone into a hole after her. Neither of those had anything to say to a morning on a plank at Thornwater, when a boy lay still and let her spend.
133:It was a high bare way between two hills, with nothing on it but frost and a few sheep that belonged to nobody, and she slept each night with her back against the jenny's belly and her feet in her pannier. On the fourth afternoon the ground began to fall. There was a valley under her with a pale road in it, going east and west, and she knew that road; she had gone round a ring of stones by it, thirty times, with a mule.
135:She came down to Thornwater's eastern gate at dusk on the hundred and twenty-fifth day. The bar was drawn for her before she had called.
151:There were seven of them under the back room's window in the morning, in the frost, and the first was sitting bolt upright on the bench's end with a stick across her knees.
157:"I rested it ten days. Then a carter came by my door and told me that you'd gone off your round after a crew, south, onto bad ground. So I thought that if you weren't coming to my doorstep, I'd find out whether the knee would come to you." The old woman rapped it with her knuckles. "It's a day and a half over the low place. I did it in two. I'd one of the boys with me to carry the bread."
173:It was a long stooped carter whom she knew, fetching for the gates up the water, and he had been as far as the ford with the oxen and a gate beyond. He gave it to her in the yard, as he had been given it, in the order of the road. The mill had had nothing worse than a crushed thumb. The two gates above it had buried nobody. At the ford a man had lost a finger to a chain and kept the hand; they had bound it themselves, badly, and it had not gone bad. And at the gate where the little one was, the breathing was no different. She coughed at night, and slept after.
183:It was the steading's woman who told her about the carter, on the evening of the hundred and twenty-eighth, with the sky gone the colour of a slate.
193:She did not keep a book. She had said so to the pale one across a fire: a healer who needs a page to know who is owed has stopped looking at them. She had never in thirty rounds set down a word about any person whom she had had her hands in. But those five were two days south, with a saddle between that would be shut by morning, and she could not look at them. There was a girl down there with an arm in a knot of hers, who had been told *first snow*; and by the feel of that sky she would be looking at it out of a window tomorrow.
203:The small one had been in a loft when Oryn said that an old woman's knee did not care what was under a floor. She was a person who liked to know how a thing came out.
209:She went over what she was leaving out, as she would have gone over a bench at the end of a day, to be sure that nobody had been missed.
217:*Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
227:It was a hand that Cael had never seen. It was square, and stiff, and it pressed, like a person lifting something heavy. He read the four lines out, as they stood, a stop at a time: the route, and a knee, and what three weeks had cost; and then the thaw, and a map, and what he was to do when he was told.
233:It was Lira who said it. She had turned on the hearthstone, and her eyes had gone past him to that window, where the snow was lying a hand deep on the sill. Then they came down to the grey wool across her own breast, and to a knot that was not hers.
237:"After the thaw," said Brom.
253:"Someone made this pattern."
257:"I have ten things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no file I can see — and five people, one of them on a route, who've walked into the quiet three times and come out." He turned it over once and let it stand. "And a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one."
259:Lira, one-armed, feeding the fire: "Then we follow it."
261:Brom: "After the thaw."
263:Karis, writing: "Noted."
