27:Lira, one-armed, on the taped foot, walking as she had walked since the Ring — the arm across her chest, the good hand free, the stride not one hair shorter for it — and carrying Oryn's crossbow across her back on its strap, which she had not given back and Oryn had not asked for. Brom with the kit on his good shoulder and the middle box on the same one and the other carried still and forward, and no word about it, and Oryn's eyes on it at every halt. Karis with the map in her coat and the straightedge in her hand, open, because she had opened it and had not found a reason to close it. Seln ahead, always ahead, with the case on his back and the ledger inside his coat, reading a road nobody had walked for a season. Oryn with her mule's rope in her left hand and her right hand out of the coat, at every halt, as if a hurt might arrive at the fire and need it. And himself, with the ribs that had paid for the Hall saying so at every halt now that the cold had them, and a healer's two days on them gone to four, and ten things held to nothing and a still place at the middle of them that he did not touch, because a healer had said *stop touching it* and a man who had been told that by the only person who could read him did as he was told.
