9:He catalogued the five of them at the landing as he had catalogued them at every halt of the year, because it was the last halt and he wanted the count. Lira, one-armed, the sling gone grey with five weeks of road, the taped foot put down flat in the snow, Oryn's crossbow across her back on its strap; she had walked six days from the fourth site on that foot and had not once said the word *ankle*, and would not until a healer said the other word first. Brom, with the kit on one shoulder and the middle box on the same one, and the other shoulder carried in the same guarded set it had kept since a hall — still, and slightly forward — though the healer of no Path at Teague's fire had said it wanted binding and not hands, and it had been bound, and had healed under the binding as a shoulder heals when it is left alone by a man who cannot leave things alone. Karis with the three copies rolled under her arm in oilcloth against the snow, the ledger inside her coat where Seln had let her carry it for the last two days, and the straightedge in her hand, because she had not once put it back in the box since the marsh. Seln with the case on his back and his eyes on the landing door, counting who came out of it. And himself, with nothing in his hands, and ten things held to nothing, and the ribs quiet again since the marsh, and a still place at the middle of them that he had read three times and had not touched since, because a healer had said *stop* and he had stopped.
