editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:19:"All eight. They've taken no harm from a stair or a wall, which is more than you deserved of them." She drew the wool up and tied her own knot, high, with the hand at the collar-bone. "It holds nothing. I said first snow, near enough, and I'll say when; you'll not say it for me because a flake's come down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:23:Lira had the bow off her back. She held it out by the stock, with the one bolt lying in its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:35:Nobody answered her. She had told them at a mill what she would do on the day that the round could not spare her, and what they were to say to it. Cael had written down, where he wrote such things, that he would not ask her to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:49:She shifted the rope on her shoulder. She was looking up at the notch, and her face was as tired and as level as he had ever seen it; and whatever had given under her at a marsh's edge, three days before, had been put back where she kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:59:There were four of them behind him, and one mule. They stood at the heap of stones as she had left them: a small man with a case on his chest, and a small woman with a hide under her arm, and a very big one bound up like a parcel, and a girl with a bow on her back and one sleeve empty. It was the crew that had come down off a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second day past the Line, and it looked a good deal the worse for wear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:81:It was the word that he gave at the end of every watch, and Cael had heard it some hundreds of times. He heard it seven times more before dark. Each time Karis nodded, and wrote the hour, and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:83:There were five of them, and he had been right about the sound. It was thinner. Nobody walked last now with her eyes on Brom's right shoulder; he found himself doing it, and was told by Brom, without heat, that one healer had been enough and that this one had no hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:85:Lira walked beside him for a mile in the afternoon, on the frozen turf, with the bow knocking against her back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:93:They slept on the ridge in a hollow, without wood, in every blanket that they had. Brom laid six bowls on the turf. He looked at the row, and took one back; and it passed without remark, since he had done exactly that on a hundred nights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:101:By noon he could see for thirty miles. There was no road in any of it, and no smoke, and not a fence or a roof; there were hills going away blue behind hills, with the white lying in their folds, and a sky over them so large that he could not look at the whole of it at once. Four people were strung out along the turf ahead of him, small and dark. A man who knew every road out of a town was walking toward a place where there was none. A girl who had been ranked every week of her life was a mile from the nearest thing that could have measured her, with a bow on her back that she could shoot and could not bend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:111:It was true, and he let it stand; and the other thing was in the same coat with it, and had been all morning. They were walking along a ruler. Somebody had set three floors in a row, as a person sets stones along a path; and when he looked ahead down the ridge, to where the strip went on into the blue, he was more afraid of what was under the brass than he had been of a thing in a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:119:The ridge ended there. It broke off in a long brow, and under the brow a valley began: a great shallow head of pale ground, scooped out of the hills as a bowl is scooped, and open toward the south. The light was going. What there was of it lay level across the grass and showed him the size of the place, which he did not at once believe. Seln gave it one word, as he gave everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:141:They left the mule on the brow, hobbled, with the boxes. Karis took the long cord over her shoulder, and her lamp, unlit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:151:At a hundred and ninety, or within a pace or two of it, her left boot came down beside one of the dark uprights, and she stopped as she had stopped at the reeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:153:"Gone." She drew the foot back. "Back. It's here. It's at the stakes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:155:"A hundred and ninety," said Karis, at the ruts. "Not forty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:161:He could have gone over. He knew it as he stood there. He had walked across three such lines and felt nothing but the ground, and he was the one person at that iron for whom Lira's *gone* had never had any teeth. He kept his boots beside theirs. It was a crew's line or it was nobody's, and he had learned on a white floor what it cost to hold a different ruling from the people whom he was ruling for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:189:She did it twenty times because she did not believe it, and he watched her cease to disbelieve it at about the twelfth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:195:Her voice was quite steady. She opened her notebook against the lamp, and did the sum in it, with her lips moving. She had said two hundred of the Stair, by her eye, and had been twenty short and a stride; and he saw the figure that she wrote now, which was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from a line that she had not crossed to a middle that she had not seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:199:"That's not a survey. That's a cordon. They've been here more than once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:229:*Four sites. One line. The last one's twice the size and the Compact fenced it. The bearing runs through it and keeps going. Karis says four points is an argument. I say it's a bearing — the kind their maps draw as nothing. Home is Lowmarch now. I just wrote that without noticing. Nineteen, ten fragments, a still place at the center of me, and a line on a map that somebody drew before there were maps. We're not done. We're oriented.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:17:Brom laid six bowls at every halt, and took one back. The mule went on the low side of nothing. Karis did her sums walking, and Seln brought his one word down the file; and to Cael, who had learned that crew's sound on a hundred roads and had learned it again at a fork with a pair of boots gone out of it, five had already begun to seem the number that a road was walked by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:23:He could not lie on them. He slept sitting, against a box or a wall, with his knees drawn up. He woke at every turn of the watch with the three old breaks as plain to him as three fingers laid on his side. They were no worse on the road. They only spoke, exactly as a healer had said that they would, and he counted her ten days off against them each night as a man counts off a sentence: the sixth on the ridge, and the seventh at the stones, and the eighth under the fence of a hold that would not open its gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:25:Lira walked in front of him, and he watched her do it for six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:31:He was three strides behind. He saw the boot go, and her right arm fly out; and he saw the left, under the grey wool, start to come and stop at a knot. She went down on her right shoulder and her hip, hard, with the bow clattering on her back. She lay for one breath with her cheek on the ice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:33:Brom had the mule's rope in his good hand, and he did not let go of it. Not one of them went to lift her. Each of them knew what she had been told at a stone about that arm, and what she would say to a hand under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:57:He read it on his watch, as he had on every night since a room under a floor. He sat with his back to four sleepers and the limp little book open on his knee, tilted to what the embers gave. He did not need the light. He used it regardless, since there is a difference between remembering a hand and looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:63:He had told four people at a fire that he wanted a face. He had gone up a ridge for one, and had been given six hoods and a bow with its maker's stamp filed off. He had broken that bow across a stone. He had not broken a thing in anger since he was a boy, and he was not proud of it, and he had entered it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:91:He went over it as he would have gone over a return. The case was shut for cause. There were names in it, and what was done to their owners; and there was a leaf in it that was no copy. It would be opened on a day, before the right people, or it would burn those whom it was kept for. He did not doubt that. He had audited it on a great many watches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:97:He stopped in the road, and waited for the four of them to come up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:123:His own hand went to his coat that evening, on watch, and found a strap, and the corner of a lock, and nothing beside them. He let it lie there. He had thought that he might want the thing back by nightfall, as a man wants a tooth that has been drawn. He left it where he had put it, that night and the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:127:The first snow met them at the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:129:They had come down the south road since noon, past a well with a new rope on its windlass, under a sky the colour of a slate. Cael felt something touch his face as the meadow opened, and took it for ash from somebody's fire. Then there was another, on Lira's sleeve, which did not melt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:139:It was the fifth time that he had read that town, and the first from below. He had always had it from a saddle or a ridge, laid out like a board; now it stood over him. The river went by at his feet, dark, between two shelves of ice that had grown since he saw them last until each was as broad as a cart is long. The ferry's cable sagged across from bank to bank with a white rime on it. The scow lay on the near side, and a man with a mallet was knocking ice from its running blocks. In the doorway of the round shed a brown gelding with one white foot stood under a blanket and watched him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:149:"You've not got my rope," he said. It was the ferryman, and he did not say it as a complaint.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:161:He was right. The room was fuller than Cael had ever seen it, since the cold term had driven the country in off its roads. There were fifty people on those benches, and a fire on the hearth that a man could have stood up in. The hide-woman was there, and three grey heads over a pair of dice, and two carters with their boots to the blaze. At the last bench before the hearth sat a man with leather on his wrist, with four about him at their distances. One of the four was a lean old woman with a bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:167:He had the ledger open already at their heading. He turned his shoulder to the room and gave out a line that nobody had yet heard, in the voice that he used under his eave. It was a recovery, on the Stair road, at a broker's posting. It was taken. Six had gone in. There were instruments found there, and brought up, and kept by the crew that found them; and six had come out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:179:*three hundred; taken; not paid*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:189:"It's first snow," said Pike. "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw. I've done forty today. This is the last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:191:He turned his shoulder again. Cael saw the hide-woman put down her cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:215:Cael stood before a hearth with snow melting on his shoulders. A magister had written of him once that no instrument would see his like. He had meant it kindly, and had entered it in a daybook that Cael was not let to carry away. This man had turned his own book about on a bench, in front of fifty people, to show a boy the hole in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:219:"Eleventh," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:227:"After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:235:Brom had not waited for any of it. He had gone straight down the room to the lean old woman, as he was told at a fork. He stood in front of her like a boy with a torn coat. She did not get up. She looked at four yards of webbing, once, from the top turn to the bottom, and at how he held the arm inside it; then she went back to her bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:241:"Fence. Forty. Teague says it's yours if you want it; he's got the cold road," he said, to the fire, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "It's the long one, east, with the axe-head on its gate. He'd a day's posts still to set when he went off after you, and there's the whole far side to mend before the thaw. A hold will feed a crew that's mending its fence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:243:All four of them looked at Brom. He had asked a one-armed man once what a board paid somebody with no Path at all, and had carried the answer against his skin for seventy days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:The inn's keeper was a slow heavy man with a cloth over his shoulder, who had drawn their jugs for a hundred and twenty days and had never been asked for anything else. She told him what she wanted: a dry floor, and a door that shut, and a table that nobody ate at. It was for paper, she said. She would have it until the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:11:"Ninety-six in hand," said Brom, who had done it for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:21:She laid them in three heaps along the deal, and did not try to make their edges agree; the Fallow's were long strips, and the Stair's were leaves, and the hall's were whatever a person could tear from a roll with her teeth. There were eleven in the first heap. There were twenty-nine in the second, and twelve in the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:25:"Fifty-two sheets. Forty-one kinds, at the two that I could do properly. Three perimeters of one size, and a fourth that is twice it." Karis leaned on the deal with both palms. "I had three boxes of other people's stone when I crossed the Line. I should not have said that there was anything east of it to put in them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:31:*Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:39:*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:49:The ferryman had been at the shelf since first light with a pole and an axe, to cut her a lane. Half the meadow stood on the landing to see it. The scow went out along its cable slowly, shouldering the broken ice, with the gelding walking his round in the shed and the boy calling the turns. On the far bank a man in a hat with one side cut away stood waiting beside a mule, as he had stood at a ford on the fourth day past the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:73:"He says that it is true to a hair," said Karis, at his elbow. She was looking at the brass as she had once looked at a founding stone. "I should like to see it against mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:87:He folded the two, and put no place on either, and gave them to the ferryman's boy with a copper apiece. The clerk's packet went into the same bag. Seln watched it go down the bank, with his hands behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:91:The scow went over once more, and came back empty. Then the ferryman and his boy took her lines in. They drew the hauling rope off the drum, fathom by fathom, stiff with ice, and coiled it in the shed; and the gelding was led away up the bank to the smith's stable with his blanket on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:113:She began with what her hands had, as she had been taught. They were full again. She had mended nobody for three days, and slept in a ruined hold with her back to a wall, and she could feel the purse of her reserve sitting heavy and whole, as it had not sat since a mill. They had held a shoulder twice, and it was mended, and bound by a woman who knew more about holding than she did; she had watched a drover's turn laid three times and could lay it now herself. They had held an arm with eight marks on it, all taken. They had been on a boy's wrist for a count of ten and had got what they always got.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:125:*So are you.* It had come out of her crooked, and she had heard it come. She went over it now, strictly, with her knuckles ready. He was on the route. That was the whole of what the words said, and they were true of a miller's man and an old woman on a hurdle. She had gone into a dish of water after a shoulder, since she was nearest. He had gone into a hole after her. Neither of those had anything to say to a morning on a plank at Thornwater, when a boy lay still and let her spend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:143:She led the jenny in under the gate-walk. A lean man stood up there with a long bow at the length of his arm, as he had stood on every evening that she could remember, and he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had never known him do that for her. She understood, crossing the yard, that it was not for her: it was for a girl with one arm, who had sat on those planks beside him and shot at a sack, and he was asking after her in the only way that he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:193:She did not keep a book. She had said so to the pale one across a fire: a healer who needs a page to know who is owed has stopped looking at them. She had never in thirty rounds set down a word about any person whom she had had her hands in. But those five were two days south, with a saddle between that would be shut by morning, and she could not look at them. There was a girl down there with an arm in a knot of hers, who had been told *first snow*; and by the feel of that sky she would be looking at it out of a window tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:211:There would be nothing in it about a plank. That stood where she had put it, and a strip of bark was no place to move it from. There would be nothing about a still thing under a boy's breastbone; she had told him to leave it alone, and she would not be the one who put a finger on it from two days off. She would not ask after a mule. And she would not tell five people that she had been glad of their bowl, since she had eaten out of it standing, on purpose, for nine days, and they had every one of them seen her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:217:*Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:225:Cael took it to the hearth. There were not a dozen people in that long room so early. Brom was laying out the bowls at their end of it, as he had at every halt, and Lira sat on the hearthstone with her right hand full of kindling. Karis had come out of the room behind the chimney with the hide under her arm, and Seln stood at the far end of the fire, by the small window that looked down on the landing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:229:Nobody spoke for a breath. Brom had six bowls in a row on the board. He looked along them. He had put the last one down a little apart, as he always did; and he did not take it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:235:"She'll say when," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:239:Karis had the hide unrolled on the board by then, between the bowls, with a pebble on each corner. There were four rings on it. The fourth lay at the very edge of the skin, where the carter had never had a wheel; and through the middles of the other three a line was ruled in pencil, very fine, which ran on through the fourth and off the hide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:253:"Someone made this pattern."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:255:She had not written it. She said it once, to the board, in the small exact voice; and nobody at that fire answered her. Cael counted four seconds by the tick of the wood. Lira put a stick on. Brom was looking at the sixth bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:265:Down the fire, the man who used to file reports about him watched the snow on the ferry landing with evident professional approval and said nothing, which — from him — was home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:They came over the crown of the rise as he finished. Cael knew the man in front by the way he stopped there, with his thumbs in his belt, and looked at everything before he came on. He looked at the hall first, and for some time. Then his eyes came down the old ruts to a rope of stiff breeches between two bundles of reeds. They went to a big man lying on his back with an arm strapped across him, and to a girl on a grey stone with a crossbow on her knee. Last of all they went, as they always went, to the number of people who were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:25:He left his three on the rise. They sat down where they stood, a crew's length apart, and the boy got out his bread. Teague walked down the ruts with the woman at his shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:57:The two of them knelt, one on either side. He could not follow the whole of what they did. The shirt-tail came off, and a pad of raw wool went up under the arm. Then a strip of stout webbing as broad as a hand was carried round Brom's chest, and over the point of the shoulder, and back beneath the elbow, three times; and the lean woman laid that figure without once looking at her fingers. Oryn held where she was told to hold. Half way through she stopped the other's wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:89:"For them," said the bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:97:The boy had stopped eating. The spear had said nothing, and the old woman had looked at Teague over her bowl as she had looked at him for nine cold terms whenever he was about to do something that she would have to bind afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:99:"There's a man with them who had a bolt through his shoulder," Teague told her. "Their healer shut it that night, with her hands, on the grass. She's a good one; I've seen her work. He walked off next day with a case on his other side."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:105:She had put her spoon down. He knew her opinion, since she had given it to him over the bow's hip more times than he could count. A thing put right in an hour by a Path was a thing that nobody afterward thought to tie. Hands were quick, she said, and quick was not the same as held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:109:He had not asked her to come. He had only told her what he had seen, and she had named the beast that she would load. It was how she had joined the crew in the first place, nine cold terms ago, at a ford with a carter's leg in it: she had looked at how the thing was being carried, and had put down her bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:113:They were on the brow of the low ground before noon. The four crossbows had lain where he left them, by a grey stone, and the frost had kept every print about it; and from between two bars of iron the trail went off south and east, as plain as a ditch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:121:There was no print over theirs but his own crew's. He had gone back along it himself at each dusk, a mile, to be sure. He had lain on his face on a knoll for an hour on the second night, with the cold coming up through his coat, and watched the way that they had come. Nothing moved on it. Nobody was behind them, and nobody had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:123:"Well?" the bow had said, when he came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:135:There was a camp at the track's end with two fires. He counted, as he counted before he did anything, and he counted his own first: a spear, and a bow, and a boy, and an old woman with a board. Then he counted the camp, and there were six in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:137:He looked at the rope between the reeds. There were breeches on it, frozen as stiff as boards, six pairs, and they had not got so wet by walking on grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:139:Teague stood on that rise with his thumbs in his belt, and added it up. They had gone into the Fallow, six of them, and come out; a one-armed man had said so twice under his eave. They had gone into the Stair and one had walked out of it declaring. And here was the drowned hall, which nobody went near, and a rope of wet breeches, and six at a fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:141:A book had columns. It had one for what was sent for, and one for what came back, and one for what was paid. He had been at the head of Pike's cloth for a good many cold terms by filling those three better than other men. He could think of nothing that this crew had done since the Fallow that would go into any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:145:He sent the old woman down first, since a shoulder would not wait on his thinking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:155:Nobody had done that to him for a long while. A big boy had done it once, on a stone floor, with a circuit's crowd shouting over them. He had won a bout. He had crossed the floor to the loser without any hurry, and had lowered himself, and sat with his forearms on his knees, so that his head should be no higher. Teague sat exactly so. He was ten lines up a sailcloth sheet, and he had put himself down on the cold turf beside a boy, so near that their shoulders were a foot apart, and he was looking at the reeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:177:Teague's head came round. He had not looked for that, and for the length of a breath Cael watched him weigh whether it was an insolence. Then he seemed to find that it was only a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:7:She had never done such a thing in her life. A leaf was written once, and dated, and laid in its place, and the place was entered; that was what a box was for. But there were three boxes, and she had seen what a mountain could do to a cart. On the seventy-eighth day everything that the five of them owned had gone into a gorge except those three, and they had come off the hill only because a very large person went back for two of them, and a boy broke three ribs over the third. She had counted the loss that night, when she could count again. It had been blankets, and a kettle, and a rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:15:Then the walls, and here she was careful, since she had been greedy that morning and knew it. She had eleven sheets from the Fallow, where the bands ran round the feet of eleven stones, and twenty-nine from a room under the Stair. She had twelve from the hall. That made fifty-two, and the last twelve were all from one wall, rubbed upside down in an hour, by a person who was being held by the belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:17:She had gone through them twice by the fire, with the cup of barley at her knee. There were thirty-three kinds on those twelve sheets. Every one of the thirty-three was among her forty-one, and there was not a stroke on any sheet that was not. She entered it so: *one wall; thirty-three of the forty-one; none strange; a sample, and no more.* It would have been very easy to write a larger sentence, and she had struck larger sentences out of better people's work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:27:A blanket had come onto her shoulders at some hour of the first afternoon. She had not seen it arrive. She had noticed, some time later, that she was no longer shaking, and had put up a hand, and found wool; and under her fingers at the corner there was a darn that she knew, since she had watched it made by lamplight in a loft, in very small stitches, by a person who mended his own things and nobody else's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:39:Seln sat down on his heels at her elbow. He had the limp little book out of his coat already, open at the first page, and he held it to her fire at the height of her eyes, with his thumb beside the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:73:Nobody arranges a test of that kind. It had been set for her, by a ruler, and she had passed through it on her feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:77:*The three middles lie on one straight edge, as near as this skin can show.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:99:"Three points make a line. Four make an argument."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:101:It was said in the small exact voice, and it was the only sentence that she gave them. She did not tell them that she had tried to make it miss. Cael knew that she had, since he had watched a strip of brass go up and down a calfskin by firelight for the better part of an hour; and he knew by her face that it had not obliged her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:113:"So there's a thirty-four," said Brom, from his blanket. He had been propped against the right-hand box, strapped like a parcel. "On along it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:119:He went over it as he went over anything. There was meal for six days in the left-hand box, and a little bacon. There was a shoulder that was bound and would not be mended a third time, and an arm in a sling that was to hold nothing till the snow. The frost had come back in the night, harder than they had yet had it, and a healer had said at a stone that the snow would not be long. And there was a line. He had been walking toward something for a hundred and twenty days without knowing that it had a direction, and it had one now, and it went off the edge of a calfskin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:145:"See that you do." She looked past him, at the hide. "There's a road a mile east of this water; I saw it from the rise. That quiet one says that it lies under your brass for a day, and that it forks. The left hand goes up through a gap, north and west, to Thornwater, in four of that carter's ticks." She took up the rope. "So I walk your road for a day, since it's mine as well. Where it forks, I'm the route."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:149:Cael was the last to leave the grey stone. He looked down the ruts to the reeds, and along the pale straight path, to four walls standing in water with the morning in them. A thing lay under those walls with four bolts in it. It would go its round again, he supposed, once in a quarter of an hour, when it was able; and no carter would ever know that anybody had been in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:161:And the healer came behind Brom with the grey, at the one pace that she had, with her eyes on his right shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:167:It came toward them a little after noon, behind a single mule, with a load of raw hides roped under a cloth and a smell going before it. The carter walked at the near wheel with a scarf over his nose. He looked at six people, and two beasts, and a sling, and an arm bound to a chest; and he lifted one hand to them, as carters did on that side of the Line, and they lifted theirs, and nobody broke step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:179:The fence had fallen outward all along one side. The long house had no roof, and a tree stood in the cart-yard; but one gable was whole, a wall of turf and stone twice Brom's height, and they made their fire in the lee of it. Brom laid out six bowls. Then he looked about him for wood, and saw a fallen rafter in the grass, and bent to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:191:Cael waited until the bowls were empty. He had made up his mind to it on the road. A thing had been the healer's to hear before anybody, and she had heard it; and there were four people at that fire who had been told at a loft's ladder that it existed, and had asked him nothing since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:209:"Then I shall not ask where it goes in the box until you tell me that it goes." She put her bowl down. "I have nothing to measure it with, and I am saying so before anybody supposes that I have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:213:"Five bowls tomorrow," she said, presently.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:5:Seln stepped over the grey stone first. Lira went second, with the healer's bow hanging from her right hand by its stock, spanned, and one of the five bolts held in the groove under her thumb. Karis had a roll of thin paper under one arm and her pencils in her hair. Cael walked fourth, and Brom behind him, with nothing on his back for the first time in three days and his right arm bound across his chest. The healer came last, with a pannier on her shoulder and her sleeves pushed up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:17:The water came to the middle of his shin. He did not stand in it; he went straight on, at the pace of a man crossing a yard, and the four of them went down after him one by one. The plates were slick under Cael's boots, and each of them gave a little as his weight came onto it, and rocked back as he left it, so that it was like walking over the lids of a row of great chests. He counted his own strides. He did not let any one of them be longer than its fellow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:31:"Left," said Seln. "The low place. I go up, and I take the bow from her, and then her hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:33:He went at the first of the breaks without slackening, and put both palms on the round of it, and was sitting on the wall's top before Cael had seen how. Lira held the bow up to him by its stock. He laid it behind him, and took her right hand in both of his, and she walked up the cut face of the wall on her whole sole as though it had been a stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:57:It opened as a trap-door opens. The plate under Brom's boots went up at its inner edge, toward the hole, with a roar of water; and Cael saw Brom do the only thing that was in him to do. He did not jump. He straightened his back. He drove up from his heels with the whole of his strength, and Karis went off his shoulders like a sack off a hoist, and landed on her chest across the top of the wall with her legs kicking. Seln had her by the coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:61:He fell along the tipping plate toward the gap they had come in by, on his left arm, and rolled. Where he had stood there was a darkness as broad as a door, and the two halves of it came together on a yard of water and the skirt of his coat. Cael saw the cloth tear. He saw a head as blunt as the bow of a skiff, the colour of mud, and no eye in it anywhere. Then it had gone down, and the plate dropped back, and Brom was up on his knees in the wash of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:67:Cael ran. He went along the ledge under the northern wall, away from the place, since a second weight there was the last thing that Brom wanted; and at the next break he got his hands on the stone and hauled. His right forearm had stopped a crossbow's stock five days before, and it told him so. He came over the top on his belly, and turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:79:"Karis," said Lira. "Here; I've the bow. Cael's along from us, at the corner. You're across the hole. Brom?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:85:Cael looked at where each of them was, since there was nothing else that he could do for them. Karis and Lira sat on the northern wall near its low end, with the bow between them. Seln had gone along the same wall-top at a crouch, past Cael, and round the corner, and stood now on the eastern wall, which was the tallest, where he could see the whole of the hole. Cael was at the corner between them. And Brom lay by himself on the far side of the water, on a strip of wall no longer than a cart, which ended at the gap on one hand and at nothing on the other. Its top was green, and wet, since the spring's water slopped over it on the way out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:107:It was cover, and it was a trap, and the same yard of stone was both. Cael understood it as he had understood a stair under a floor: nothing could come at them where they sat, and they could not leave it. He watched the plates lift below him, in a line that went round the hole and came back; and when it passed beneath his boots he could see a length of back under the clear water, as broad as the ledge itself, going by for a great deal longer than he liked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:119:"No, I can see that you're not," said Lira; and she laid the bow down on the stone, and took a fistful of Karis's belt in her right hand, and held on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:123:It went on for the better part of an hour. The mist thinned over their heads and went from grey to white. Seln stood on the tall wall and said *there* each time that the line of lifting plates passed below him, and it passed a great deal oftener than once in a quarter of an hour; the thing had given up its round, and was going about that one room as a dog goes about a tree. Cael's breeches froze to the stone. He counted the sheets as they came up, since he counted everything, and then he made himself stop, since a man who is counting paper is not watching a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:141:He could not stop Brom. He could only watch him do it: the left hand spread on the stone behind him, and the left boot let down the broken end of the wall, feeling for the sill; and the whole weight of a very big man going out, slowly, over a hand's breadth of green.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:145:It shot from under him on the wet, and Brom's body did what any body does. His right arm came out of its binding. It caught the wall's end above his head and took the whole of him, on a shoulder that a healer had shut five days ago and given a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:161:There was nothing fine about how she went. She had stood on a kerb for an hour with her arms folded, thirty yards from a shoulder that she had mended, on the wrong side of a line, with nothing in her hands. She had watched that shoulder go, through a gap in a wall; and she was the nearest living person to a patient who had fallen. She stepped down into the water as she would have stepped off a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:177:Lira had the bow up, and nothing to loose it at but water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:231:It came after the slab as it had come after a stone, and the slab stayed. He felt the jaws close on the place. Everything that he had went thin. He was on his hands and knees on a plate with water to his elbows, and he could not have said which way the wall was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:247:It was Seln who sent it across the room, in the voice for distances; and beyond the gap a square brown woman with a very big man's arm over her shoulders did not turn her head to ask why. There were two strides of plate between the pair of them and the sill, and nothing under those for as long as a boy could hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:253:She had slid down from her knees to the seat of her breeches on the wall-top, with her left leg folded under her and her right knee drawn up. The sole of the great boot was planted whole on the stone. Behind her right shoulder the wall rose a foot higher, where a course had not broken; she set her shoulder against that, and laid the stock across her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:269:Seln had the bow out of her hand before the word was finished. He stood up on a yard of stone, with his boot in the iron and the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped. He laid a bolt in the groove from the sleeve at his belt, and put the bow into her hand again, and she had not once moved her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:279:Seln had the bow again, spanned, with the third bolt in it. He had been holding it out to her. He did not answer; he went along the wall-top to the corner at a run, bent double, and round it onto the eastern wall, and Cael saw him bring a crossbow to his shoulder for the first time in two years of knowing him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:303:"It is alive," said Seln. He was coming back along the wall with the bow. "And it is going down, and it is hurt, and I do not know how long any of those will last. Karis. Paper. We walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:305:They came off the wall on the side of the gap, one at a time, and did not stand. Seln had Cael's arm across his shoulders. Lira carried the roll, and Karis had her finished sheets held against her chest in both arms, with her face scraped raw along one cheek where the stone had had it. They went along the ledge, and over the sill, where the healer got up from her knees with Brom's weight on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:309:"You spent it in a morning. I said that you would." She had her shoulder under his left arm, and she was wet to the waist. "Walk. You can be sorry on the grass."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:315:On the kerb Seln stopped, since a person could. He set his boot in the stirrup once more, and spanned, and laid the last bolt in the groove; and he held the bow out to Lira by its stock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:319:She took it. Brom was looking at the roll of paper, and at what Karis held, with his coat black from the shoulder to the belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:11:The coat came away from Brom's shoulder with a sound that he did not care for, and under it the binding hung in a loop, and the linen beneath was black.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:13:Oryn knelt, wet to the waist, with the wet stiffening on her as the frost found it, and she did not seem to know. She laid one hand on the front of the shoulder and one behind, and shut her eyes, and her lips reached three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:21:It took her an hour by the sun, which had come up white over the rise while they were in the hall, and Cael watched the whole of it from where he sat. He had seen that woman mend a shin, and an arm, and this same shoulder by lamplight, and he had never seen her do it shaking. She shook with the cold from the first quarter onward, steadily, in the legs and the back, while her two hands lay round the joint as still as if they had belonged to another person. Seln put a blanket over her from behind, and she let it lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:23:Lira held nothing; she sat on the grey stone with the bow across her knee, and looked from the healer's hands to the reeds, and back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:27:She sat down where she had knelt, on the frozen turf, with her knees drawn up and her forehead on them, and it was as long as it takes to count a hundred before she lifted her head. Nobody went to her. Cael had heard her say on a mountain that her reserve was a purse; he had watched her empty it at a mill, and at two stones, and he understood by now that what she spent on such a morning was the very thing that a child at some northern gate was waiting for, and that she knew it to the grain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:41:"I know what you were doing. I watched you do it." She had her hands under her arms. "You put her on that wall with your back, and then you sat on the wrong one for an hour with nothing to do, and then you got down. I'd have got down. It's the arm that I'm angry with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:55:There was the other thing, for which he had no good word. He had held a point against a pair of jaws while four bolts went in, and it had taken everything else that he owned to do it; not one of the ten fragments had yet come back to where it lived, and he felt like a house from which the furniture has been carried out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:75:It was only three words. But it had not come out as her other sentences came, each one finished and set down like a bowl on a board; the middle of it had given a little under her, as a plate gives underfoot. He made no answer, since he did not think that she could have borne to be answered. She had told a mill that she would be nobody's sixth, and a board at Thornwater that she was a stop; she had eaten out of their bowl for a week, standing; and this was the only way that she had of saying what a person became to her when she had gone into a marsh after his friend and watched him go into worse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:79:"Lie still. I want to read you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:93:She had him lift his shirt, and she pressed with the flat of her thumb in two places, watching his face and not her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:113:The first was the one that she had lit, at the track's end, with wet people round it and a line of breeches steaming on a rope between two bundles of reeds. The second she built for herself, twenty yards off and upwind, no bigger than a hat, since she would not have sparks near paper. She had the lid of the middle box for a table there, and her morning's work laid out on the turf under pebbles, one sheet beside another, to lose the damp that they had taken from a wall. She had gone into that place for the inner faces of four walls, and had come out with as much of one of them as a person can rub upside down in an hour with a girl holding her by the belt; and she set about it now as if the hour had been a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:117:When he looked again, there was a blanket round her shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:121:Lira had not left the grey stone. She sat on it with her foot set down whole and the healer's bow along her knee, with its nose toward the reeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:125:"Seln says that a night won't hurt it." She did not turn round. "There's one. If I let it down, I've a stick with a string on, and nobody here to bend it who isn't asleep or mending. It stays in the bow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:129:"I know what he read. I watched it come up out of a hole this morning, the whole of it, when a carter had said that nobody ever saw so much." Her right hand moved on the stock, a finger's breadth, and lay still. "I'm not saying that it's coming. I'm saying that I've one, and it's in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:131:He left her to it, since she had sat on a stair for a day once with that same bow, for the same reason, and had been right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:137:She had been counting his wrist every quarter of an hour, by whatever clock she carried, and she had sat down to do it once more with her legs crossed and his hand in her lap. She had got as far as closing her fingers. Her chin was on her chest, and her back had gone round, and the blanket had slipped off one shoulder; she was breathing through her mouth like a child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:151:Not one of the four of them got up to put the blanket back, since she would have woken. They let her be, in the middle of them, with a big man's wrist in her hand, while the short day went over toward the rise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:155:*Hundred and nineteenth day. The track's end, outside the stone. All six out. Brom's shoulder opened on a wall and is mended a second time, an hour; it will not be mended a third. Four bolts spent; one in the bow, with Lira.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:171:Seln had the watch. He sat on the grey stone where Lira had sat, with his back to the fire, and whatever he heard behind him he gave no sign of hearing; the rest were four long shapes under blankets. Cael lay on his back between the embers and the old iron, and laid two fingers on the inside of his wrist and a palm on his breastbone, and let his breath go out until he had none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:189:He did not come away this time, and he lay and watched how they went round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:211:*Hundred and twentieth day, before light. The third: where it was, as it was, the same.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:213:When he raised his head, she was sitting on the far side of the dead fire with her arms round her knees, watching him. She had her own coat on again, and he could not tell how long she had been awake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:235:"I'm asking, then. Will you let me read you, now, while I tell it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:263:"I gave it one a long while back, on an old leaf, when it was only something that another person's hands had caught the edge of. The still place. I never had anything to lay the words on till last night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:275:"A still place that everything goes around. I've never felt that in anyone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:283:"You weren't frightened, saying it. I'd have felt that; your step didn't alter." She rubbed her hands down her breeches. "That's the three, and the telling, as I set them at a well. It's paid. The other thing was never a debt, and it stands where I put it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:11:She did it three times. Cael watched the bright edge go down the hide, past the square beyond the ridge that was a mill, and out over the place where the carter's hill-hatching thinned. All six of them had their heads over the box by then, with the healer a stride and a half behind the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:29:Seln had been reading the hide over her shoulder, as he read any paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:37:The healer had listened to the whole of it with the grey mule's rope over her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:45:"I shut that shoulder last night. A mend so deep wants a week of somebody's eye on it, as her arm wanted its fortnight, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends an hour of herself on a thing and walks off in the week that it's deciding. That's the hundred and twenty-first." A finger followed the thumb. "There's an edge at the end of that brass. I said in a loft where somebody ought to stand when you cross one, and I learned yesterday what it's worth to stand there." Another finger. "And I've sent word north that I'm nineteen, or more. It's the *or more* that I'm spending."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:49:"So I'll see the shoulder through its week, and I'll be at your next edge with my sleeves up. After that I'm the route, and nobody says a word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:55:It was Brom who answered her, and he did it by going to the left-hand box for the bowls. He set out six on the turf between the two irons, as he had on every night since a grey stone on the Fallow; and the healer, who had told a girl at a mill that she would eat out of the sixth and not sit in a place, watched him do it and held her tongue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:71:"You're carrying on the one side and walking as if you had two. Let that strap out a hand, and take it across you." She waited while he did it. "That's a week I've given that shoulder. I'd as soon you didn't spend it for me in a morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He let it stand. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:131:"Gone," she said. She drew the foot back by the length of itself. "Back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:197:"It is the old work. It comes up out of the mud as those stones on the Fallow came up out of their floor, with no bed under it and no join, and whatever heaves that mud has not shifted it by a hair. I think that it will carry us." He looked round the five of them. "We shall go as we went down a mile of grass once with a grey thing behind us: at one pace, in file, and nobody stops to look at anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:209:The path was a stride wide, and it stood a hand above the water. The reeds rose on either side of it higher than Brom's head, dry and rattling, so that they walked in a kind of lane, with a strip of grey sky over them and black water at both elbows. Cael went fourth. He had his ten fragments lying quiet in him, on the far side of a line, and he kept his eyes on Karis's back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:227:The thing in the reeds had not followed them onto the kerb. But as he spoke, a line of plates lifted along the dish's near side, each a hand's breadth, one after another, and settled again with a small knocking; it was like watching a cat go along under a blanket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:253:She threw side-arm, low, with the whole of her one good shoulder in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:353:"There's a way to do it," said Seln. "It has given us its rule, and a rule is a plan. On the floor, we move. We rest only on what comes up out of the old work: the path, and the kerb, and the tops of those walls, which are a yard through. A wall-top is a kerb set on end." He counted it off. "We go in at first light. Lira carries the bow, spanned, since she can loose it and cannot bend it. I carry the hook and the sleeve. Karis has her paper and nothing else. Thirty yards of plates, at a walk, with nobody standing; in at the gap; and up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:371:Lira had the healer's bow across her knee, and she was looking past the fire at the dark place where the reeds began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:379:Brom laughed, once, and stopped, since it had pulled at his shoulder.

