editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:39:He went with her. He had not meant to, and he did not know that he was going to until his boots were on the frozen ruts beside hers, at her stride and a half, with the grey mule's breath on the back of his neck. She gave him one glance and let it be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-41-He counted, since he could not walk beside anybody without counting. He said nothing through the whole of it. There were a good many things that he might have asked on that piece of road: how late she would be now at a ford with oxen, and whether a child's chest had changed, and when he would see her. There was the other, which was whether she would ever tell him what she had refused to tell him on her table. He asked none of them. Each was hers to give, and she had a road to walk.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-57-He stood until the notch had her. She did not look back, and he had not thought that she would. Then he turned round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:59:There were four of them behind him, and one mule. They stood at the heap of stones as she had left them: a small man with a case on his chest, and a small woman with a hide under her arm, and a very big one bound up like a parcel, and a girl with a bow on her back and one sleeve empty. It was the crew that had come down off a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second day past the Line, and it looked a good deal the worse for wear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-61-He took out Hesk's book and wrote it standing, with the pencil cold in his fingers.
