editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:83:"It's colder than the frost up there," said Oryn. "And it smells of nothing. A cellar smells of earth, and a well of water. I've been in sick-rooms shut up for a month that had more in them to breathe than this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:105:"What are they," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:267:"You do," said Oryn, from the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:81:"I don't know," said Oryn. "But you'll sit still for it. You're grey, and you're no worse than you were, and I'd like to keep both of those."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:167:"Can you do it to this?" said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:193:"Ask first," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:251:It was the grey mule. She was hobbled above the stone with the brown one, and she had put up her head and brayed at the dusk, long and rusty, as she had no doubt brayed at every dusk of her life. The brown mule did not bray. She had turned her ears, both together, down the old road. She was looking straight at the place where he stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:255:It looked at them for as long as it takes to count five, and saw a mule that was making a noise and a mule that was not, and three boxes under a blanket. Then the hood came round again to the floor, and to the square hole in the middle of it, where nothing had stirred all day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:5:It went out of him as it had come. The sound of his own breath returned, loud and ragged, and after it the creak of frozen grass under his hip and the grey mule shifting her feet above the stone. He was a boy lying in an old rut at dusk, under the turf's lip, where nobody on that brow could have seen more of him than a shoulder; and whatever they had looked at for the last quarter of an hour, it had not been him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:127:"Down. Here, on the blanket, on your left side. You, bring that light and hold it where I point. You, the pannier, the one that's on the grey." She had Brom on the turf before any of them had moved. "And somebody give me a knife that'll cut ash."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:139:"That's one," said Oryn at the end of it. She sat back on her heels. Her face was the colour of the frost. "It's shut. It went through the meat and missed everything that matters, and you were born lucky. You'll carry nothing on that side till I've said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:153:"I know that it held. I felt where. I'm not scolding the arm." Oryn drew the wool up and tied it, shorter than before, so that the hand lay high against the collar-bone. "That's two, on a road, after three at a bench yesterday. There isn't a third in me tonight for anybody, and I'd thank you all to stay whole till morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:165:Seln had gone to the mules.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:169:"I tied those to be read. There is a stem of grass laid under every knot, and each is where I laid it." He set the lamp down. "Six people with a quarter of a mile of rim apiece do not leave it to look into a mule's load."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:189:He laid the two pieces on the turf, side by side, and went and sat down by the mules.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:261:He had been at the plank before the mist was off the river. The five had gone by it in the dark, two hours earlier, and he had heard the mule. Pike was setting out his book; and there was a shingle on the oak, six along from the left-hand post, which had hung there for nine days because no crew of any standing would trouble with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:307:They were at the weir by first light on the hundred and fourteenth, and the woman who kept the ford told him the news before he had asked for it. Five had come off the ridge the day before, at noon, with a mule; and the healer had gone up again with them, and had taken the mill's jenny, and had left word with a carter for a child at some gate to the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:325:Not one of the three asked him why. The spear took his spear. The bow got up off the bank, on the good leg first, and slung his stave; and Teague, who had been about to tell him that he might stay with the mill and the mules, saw his face and held his tongue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:19:The healer herself had not moved; he glanced round once and saw her, square and brown against the sunrise, with her sleeves pushed up to the elbow and two mules and three boxes on the slope behind her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:63:"Oryn. *Stop.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:79:"That was for me, then," said Oryn. "That bolt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:133:She ran *down*. She went away from the stone, and from the two mules, and from the only ground on which a Path would answer, straight down the middle of the old road toward a bare grey floor with a hole in it. She ran on the whole of her foot, slapping, like a child in its father's boots; a healer had told her four days ago that a certain bone was her own again, and she was using it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:241:Oryn put her two hands round the shoulder, above the wood and below it, and shut her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:271:Oryn had it in her hands before it stopped rolling. She laid the first strip round the shaft in front, close, and the second behind; she did it by lamp and by eye, as a carter's wife would have done it, and it took her a quarter of the time that it would have taken anybody else in that room. When she had done she pulled what was left of his coat across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:299:"Back up your six steps," said Oryn. "And if you put that arm out for anything again, I'll take the other one off you myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:3:"Well?" said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:5:She had called the halt herself, an hour south of the fork, where a grey rock as big as a cart stood up out of the ridge and broke the wind. The two mules were nose to tail in its lee. Brom had the bread out. She had sat down on a ledge of the rock with her coat pulled round her, and had looked at Cael across the trodden grass for as long as it takes to count ten before she spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:33:"Breathe out," said Oryn. "You're holding it. Start when you've none left."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:57:"Well, I'd hope so," said Oryn. "I've been one for eleven years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:71:She was silent for a little while, and behind him the grey mule was trying to eat Brom's sleeve, and being told in a low reasonable voice that it was not hay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:85:"Still and whole and willing," said Oryn, to the rock in front of her. "That's the three. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:99:"Forty," said Oryn's voice, a long way off. "You're helping. Don't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:101:He eased his neck. He had seen a man's shin close under those hands in an hour, and had watched her walk to a mule and unload it, for five days that she could not spare; and he had taken her for a person who gave things up. She gave nothing up. She was made as a road is made, to get to where the hurt was, and everything in her that was not road had been spent on it years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:117:She said it without any pride, and without the least surprise, as she might have agreed that her coat was brown. Then she got up off the rock and went to take the grey mule's rope out of Brom's hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:159:They brought the mules down to the brow. Karis gave Brom her box-rope to hold, and went on by herself down the old road toward the floor, with her arms a little out from her sides, counting under her breath. Cael walked a stride behind her and to one side, and he had no need to count, since he was watching the back of her neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:223:They made their camp on the outside of the stone, between the two irons, with the brow forty paces above them and the mules hobbled a little up the slope. Brom laid out six bowls, and he did it without looking at anybody, and the healer ate out of the last of them standing up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:225:She came by Cael afterward, at her stride and a half, with the grey mule's rope over her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:255:"I'll stand at that stone with my sleeves up. I said it, and I meant it." Oryn was sitting on her heels. "I've learned what my hands are worth on the far side of one. If a thing goes wrong in there, you bring it to me. It's two hundred yards. I've seen an arm carried as far."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:7:They were alone under the ash. The nearest of the other fires was forty yards off, with two carters asleep at it, and the mule stood between with the boxes under her blanket. Brom had built their own fire up. Lira sat on the middle box with her foot set down whole and her right hand over the knot of her sling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:151:Brom had got up while he was speaking, and gone to the mule, and stood with one hand on her withers and his back to the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:263:The frost was coming down, and he could hear it, very faintly, in the grass. Four people slept behind him, and a mule stood over three boxes; and at eleven other fires in that meadow there was nobody awake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:339:The stars had turned as far as they turned in one watch, and he got up, without a sound, and went round the four sleepers, and the boxes, and the mule, as he had gone round every camp of theirs since the shelf. Then he bent and touched the girl's right shoulder with two fingers. He chose that shoulder with care.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:5:It was Seln's hour, and Cael had given it to him without argument. A loaded cart took the whole of a short day to reach the mill; a crew that walked as they walked could be on the ridge by sunrise and at the fork before noon, and Seln wished to have the afternoon in hand. So Brom loaded the mule by feel. Lira ate standing. They went past Pike's bare plank and the smith's cold chimney, and took the south road in file, with the frost ringing under five pairs of boots and four small hooves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:15:The sun came up on their left hand, small and white. It showed them their own breath, and the mule's, and a road that ran on south along the ridge as far as they could see; and a little before the middle of the forenoon it showed them a place where the ruts divided.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:23:It came up to them out of the notch while they were still among the rocks: a slow heavy knocking, with water falling under it, at about the pace of a tired man splitting wood. Brom stopped the mule to listen. Then the track came round a shoulder, and the valley opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:49:"That's one," said Oryn. "You'll not close it on a rope before the thaw. Send in the child."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:73:"Off a ladder." Oryn had her hands round the joint already. "It's got a ladder in it. I can feel every rung. You went through the fourth, and you came down on this with the rest of you after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:87:Then there was only a boy with a swollen wrist between the five and the door. He had been sitting on his father's coat, and he came up when Oryn crooked a finger at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:91:"You," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:141:"That's my bench, then. Which of you is going to tell me why you're a day south of your board, with a mule of mine and three boxes, on a road that goes nowhere but a ford?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:187:"You go north tomorrow," said Oryn. "You'll say this at every fence you stop at, in these words, and you'll have it said on to the ford with the oxen. The healer's late. She was seventeen at the ford, and she's nineteen now, or more. She's a half-day south of the mill, at the edge of bad ground, since there's a crew gone onto it that she's had her hands in." She waited until his lips had done moving. "And at the gate beyond the ford, where the little one is: if the breathing changes, they're to send down the water to this mill, and the mill sends up the ridge. I'll come from wherever I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:193:He shifted his sack and went back to his cart. She watched him go, and Cael saw what he had seen on her face in a yard, when she unloaded a mule: it was the look of a person who has done the only thing that could be done from where she stood, and who knows to a grain how much less that is than enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:219:Oryn regarded her for the space of a breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:225:"I know what you said. You said it a deal better than he would have." The corner of her mouth went down, which with her was most of the way to a smile. "Get that mule off my fence-post. She's eaten the bark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:229:It was a jenny mule, mouse-grey and enormous in the belly, with ears like a pair of oars. She came round the corner of the shed sideways, with her head stuck out and her eyes rolling at the wheel, and it took the healer three tries to get her past it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:231:"They've had her standing since the barley was in," said Oryn, a little breathless. "She's eaten her head off and learned nothing. The miller says he'll thank me to take her as far as the snow." She threw her one pannier across the grey back and buckled it. "She leads as a gate swings. I've told them so on thirty rounds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:233:The brown mule had turned her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:239:"She's a mule. She knows where the hay used to come from." Oryn took up the rope. "You'll keep on the low side of her, as I told you. I'll go behind with this one, where she can't see the load and want it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:243:At the fork there was a flat rock, and Cael sat down on it with Hesk's book while the two mules were let to blow. The grey had come up the notch backward for a good part of the way. The mill's wheel was still to be heard below them, very small. He wrote under what he had written on a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:245:*Hundred and thirteenth day, the fork above Millrace. Oryn came. On her terms. She'll turn north the day she has to and I won't ask her not to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:3:The bar came out of Thornwater's western gate on the hundred and eleventh morning for five people, three boxes and a mule that belonged to none of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:5:Brom had loaded her by the well, in the grey, with the steading's woman holding the lantern. Two boxes hung from the pack-saddle on their own ropes, one to a side, and the third rode between them on a folded blanket with the empty pannier strapped over it. He had gone round the animal three times, pulling at each knot. Then he had stood back and looked at the load as he would have looked at a wheel that he had built himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:25:The row of white stones stood whole along the lip, as the hold had set it, and the water went over the sill no more than shin-deep, with a rim of ice along both beaches. Seln crossed first, and then Karis, who lifted her coat. Brom led the mule down to the edge on her off side, where he had walked all morning, and she set her forefeet on the wet rock and would not move them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:35:Seln, who had heard, walked ten paces up the far beach without a word. The mule went into the river as if nothing had ever been the matter. Brom, who had been leaning on the rope, went in with her a good deal faster than he had meant to, and came out on the other side wet to the thigh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:41:They made their camp that night on a carters' halting-ground a little above the wash, with the mule hobbled at the windward end and the three boxes under her blanket. Seln lit a small fire with his flint. They had kept that custom since the fifty-ninth day, on the strength of a finding, and it was a comfort still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:45:He owed a healer two readings of himself with the tenth fragment, on two different days, with a night between; and after those he owed her the whole of it at once. She had set it at a well that morning, with a pannier on the coping. Nine days had gone by since the first reading, in a loft, and he had let every one of them pass. She had seen why from a stride and a half. He had found a place in himself that did not move, and he had been afraid of it, and he had liked it better as a line on a leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:65:"It's gone home. He said that it would, in the cold term." Brom had the mule's rope, and he was looking at the smith's chimney. "Half of them have fences of their own to sit behind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:67:A cart was coming up the saddle road toward them, slowly, behind two mules, with a load under canvas that was white at the seams. Its driver walked at the near wheel. He was a round man, floured to the eyebrows, and he lifted a hand to them as carters did on a hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:79:"A carter's day. Less, for legs." He looked at the brown mule with some attention, and then at the boxes, and Cael watched him decide that it was no business of his. "You'll want to be quick, if it's her you want. She's never more than two days at our fence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:93:They went down into the meadow with the light going. The old place under the ash was empty, since a good many places were, and Brom unloaded there and rubbed the mule's back with a twist of hay while Seln walked once round the twelve fires and came back and said that there was nothing. A boy came for the standing-money. Karis gave him two coppers, which were too small a matter for her book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:147:"It's the wooden foot," said Lira. "The four who never lost a mule. They've gone over us again." She turned on the hearth bench. "We went into a place that you've never stepped over the edge of, and six of us came out. You've just said so twice. And it's cost us a line."

